{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions","id":1236176,"title":"What's Your Read on Sidereal Control of Heaven Re: Factions?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted","Sidereal Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-07-17T15:32:20-07:00","id":1236176,"title":"What's Your Read on Sidereal Control of Heaven Re: Factions?","content":"So,<br /> MoEP: Sidereals, and CoCD: Yu-Shan seems fairly schizophrenic to me regarding the relative levels of Bronze versus Gold Faction control and influence over Heaven. On the one hand, we hear again and again how influential Chejop Kejak is. He never gets audited, managed to pull off a pogrom of pro-Solar deities by having Ryzala frame them for Yozi-worship, and has his hand so far up the ass of the Wyld Hunt that when he wiggles a finger, an Ostiary in Cherak commits ritual suicide for the glory of the Dragons. On the other hand, we ALSO hear all about how Kejak's nearing expiration-date and the return of the Solars is causing many previously staunch Bronze Faction loyalist-deities to waver, reconsider their positions, or in some cases simply go off the Bronze script (like Hui Dai Ling, Director of the Crimson Panopoly, who wants to kickstart the Realm Civil War to get it over with.) Other previously neutral or Bronze-loyal deities are quietly giving their support to the Gold Faction (like Nara-O, whose conversation in a fluff comic with Kejak seems very indicative of his/it having lost faith in Kejak). We read about Ayesha Uru, expected to become the dominant force in Sidereal politics, once Kejak expires...and about the handful of prominent, pro-Solar deities who have survived every effort of the Bronze Faction to eliminate them, such as Lytek, the Golden Lord, Lo Ping, Uvanavu, and Luraname (the Syndics of Whitewall) who are doubling-down on their Solar commitments. Nysela, Charioteer of the Daystar, has become more active of late re: Solars/Abysalls/Infernals she calls to righteousness, than she has in well, ever.<br /> <br /> A lot of that seems to say the notion of Bronze dominance isn't as well-supported as is implied in places. The draw-down of the Wyld Hunt due to the incipient Realm Civil War gets mentioned frequently, but other than mentioning most of the errant Shikari have been recalled by their Houses, taking their House-troops with them, and the number of Wyld Hunt Chapter-Houses dropping from fourteen to six, just HOW drawn-down the Wyld Hunt has become by Direction, and therefore what level of resources the Bronze has to call on in the Threshold is something I find difficult to quantify. MoEP: Sidereals gives us a general idea of which directions are more concentrated examples of Faction-prominence to some extent, but again, not in the most quantifiable ways.<br /> <br /> What's the general consensus then? Beyond the ubiquitous &quot;Whatever you consider is most appropriate for your particular game&quot; that is always the cardinal rule. Realizing of course much of the reason for the vagueness is precisely to allow that selfsame Storyteller flexibility. Canonically, what are your thoughts?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-17T16:00:33-07:00","id":1236185,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions#post1236176\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So,<br /> MoEP: Sidereals, and CoCD: Yu-Shan seems fairly schizophrenic to me regarding the relative levels of Bronze versus Gold Faction control and influence over Heaven. On the one hand, we hear again and again how influential Chejop Kejak is. He never gets audited, managed to pull off a pogrom of pro-Solar deities by having Ryzala frame them for Yozi-worship, and has his hand so far up the ass of the Wyld Hunt that when he wiggles a finger, an Ostiary in Cherak commits ritual suicide for the glory of the Dragons. On the other hand, we ALSO hear all about how Kejak's nearing expiration-date and the return of the Solars is causing many previously staunch Bronze Faction loyalist-deities to waver, reconsider their positions, or in some cases simply go off the Bronze script (like Hui Dai Ling, Director of the Crimson Panopoly, who wants to kickstart the Realm Civil War to get it over with.) Other previously neutral or Bronze-loyal deities are quietly giving their support to the Gold Faction (like Nara-O, whose conversation in a fluff comic with Kejak seems very indicative of his/it having lost faith in Kejak). We read about Ayesha Uru, expected to become the dominant force in Sidereal politics, once Kejak expires...and about the handful of prominent, pro-Solar deities who have survived every effort of the Bronze Faction to eliminate them, such as Lytek, the Golden Lord, Lo Ping, Uvanavu, and Luraname (the Syndics of Whitewall) who are doubling-down on their Solar commitments. Nysela, Charioteer of the Daystar, has become more active of late re: Solars/Abysalls/Infernals she calls to righteousness, than she has in well, ever.<br /> <br /> A lot of that seems to say the notion of Bronze dominance isn't as well-supported as is implied in places. The draw-down of the Wyld Hunt due to the incipient Realm Civil War gets mentioned frequently, but other than mentioning most of the errant Shikari have been recalled by their Houses, taking their House-troops with them, and the number of Wyld Hunt Chapter-Houses dropping from fourteen to six, just HOW drawn-down the Wyld Hunt has become by Direction, and therefore what level of resources the Bronze has to call on in the Threshold is something I find difficult to quantify. MoEP: Sidereals gives us a general idea of which directions are more concentrated examples of Faction-prominence to some extent, but again, not in the most quantifiable ways.<br /> <br /> What's the general consensus then? Beyond the ubiquitous &quot;Whatever you consider is most appropriate for your particular game&quot; that is always the cardinal rule. Realizing of course much of the reason for the vagueness is precisely to allow that selfsame Storyteller flexibility. Canonically, what are your thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div>I run Sidereals as the Men in Black behind the scenes. They don't have direct control, but they can absolutely show up and disappear you, or frame you, or swipe your thoughts. They have false identities, are amazing spies, assassins, information brokers. They're the scary motherfuckers with shades and suits that you never want to meet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/800-fenrir666","name":"Fenrir666","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"300"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-17T22:05:46-07:00","id":1236297,"title":"","content":"My understanding is that Sidereals tend not to get overt control of things. They're Merlin or the Leverage Crew. When they want something to get done, they can't just go out and tell everyone what to do. Instead they have to go out and arrange things so that things happen the way they want, via &quot;luck&quot; and &quot;coincidence&quot; and a Sidereal smirking into their tea 100 yards away.<br /> <br /> This can be a very effective strategy if you have a firm grip on the ear of the boy king, a long period of time to arrange things, or other useful levers. However, if your puppets start ignoring the strings, everything needs to be done right now, and many of your other levers are starting to rot away, the weakness in the Sidereals' strategy becomes obvious. And right now, the DBs in the Realm are ignoring the monks calling for a renewed Wyld Crusade against the Solars, there is an order of magnitude increase in the number of Solars presently at large in Creation, and all of their enemies are pouncing on the opportunity.<br /> <br /> Chejop is something of an exception, since he's the nominal &quot;head sidereal&quot; and has been accumulating direct political power for thousands of years. But even if he called in all the favors he's collected and bought as many as he could, he still can't stem the tide.<br /> <br /> Sidereals spend their time gently redirecting rivers.... which doesn't help much against a flash flood.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>....","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/226-brilliantrain","name":"BrilliantRain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar226_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3718"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-17T22:17:47-07:00","id":1236300,"title":"","content":"Read first edition sidereals. 2e is just a shitty copy paste that ruins a lot of old lore for no reason. When i mean copy paste there are large chunks from sidereals and yushan that's literally from 1e sidereals. This is without the stuff that makes the bronze fraction look like incompetent because the writer has a clear bias against sidereals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":116,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","user":"Epimetheus","date":". 07-18-2018, 01:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T02:51:25-07:00","id":1236341,"title":"","content":"I think Fenrir and Brilliant rain have made pretty reasonable points. It was a good one about speed.<br /> <br /> As the masters in shadows, the Bronze faction is owed favours, knows how to blackmail or manipulate gods and elementals. But their only real institutional power is the Bureau of Destiny, which they have about 80% control of.<br /> <br /> I think it's important to the game that the Gold Faction is not too big. Maybe 5 Elders plus another 5-15 young Sidereals who are motivated by dislike of the Realm, anti-establishmentism, or seeing an opportunity for the future.<br /> <br /> If the Gold Faction is too powerful, it makes it too easy for Solars, frankly, and detracts from their struggle. Though as the Solars rise, more Sidereals might flock to the Gold Faction.<br /> <br /> Saying this, I've always seen the Scavenger Lands as kind of &quot;easy-mode&quot; for Solars. You don't have to fight the Realm so much. So I think the idea of the Gold Faction being the local Sidereals, so PCs don't have to face much in the way of Sidereal assassins, is pretty reasonable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T03:31:14-07:00","id":1236352,"title":"","content":"Ya - Sids are agents of heaven, not lords of heaven. There is a BIG difference.<br /> <br /> A sidereal can't go to god X and say &quot;You have to do Y&quot;<br /> <br /> What a sid can do, is go to god X with a a scroll where a higher ranking god signed off on some orders, and go &quot;You have to do Y, because I have the paperwork that says so&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> oh sure, sids can have sneaky things going off on the side - loads of sneaky things - but unlike the solars, lunars or even DBs, then sids only officially have in-direct power.<br /> <br /> And yes, they have a nice charm-set that can do loads of weird and magical things - kick plenty of butt - but I'm talking besides that.<br /> <br /> <br /> Ultimately this should reflect in the power of the sids in Yu-Shan. Blackmail or not, favors to call in audits or not, the power of the various sid factions ultimately depends on how much the other gods want to cooperate... because as noted by other posters already, then some gods in Yu-Shan are powerful enough (or have sufficiently powerful friends) to avoid getting into trouble no matter what is thrown at them.<br /> <br /> That said, which faction is in power should ultimately - IMO - depend on the ST of a given story. Wizard of Oz has some good points too.<br /> <br /> <br /> One of the things I like to do, is have a sid NPC try to set up circumstances to make sure that a prophesied event happens (or doesn't happen) - and then get really annoyed when stupid mortals screw things up - then subtly recruit the players to try to fix things, off the books of course. That's in my experience a fun way to start a game that only spiral out of control (which of course is all the fun)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malfeas F'Tagn - go check out my epic MLP/Exalted crossover &quot;The Scroll of Exalted ponies&quot; @ Fimfiction","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/588-webkilla","name":"webkilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar588_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T04:43:17-07:00","id":1236363,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236300#post1236300\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">the writer has a clear bias against sidereals.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or they just wrote to what they thought was the tone of the gameline, or under instructions from a developer.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T05:12:50-07:00","id":1236375,"title":"","content":"I think the problem is that there was support for playing a member of the Gold Faction in Cult of the Illuminated and something similar for the Bronze Faction never emerged, so when it came to updating them for 2nd edition they felt like snidely whiplash villains (because that's basically what they are in that book) and quietly replaced them with the Independents.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":7,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","user":"Lioness","date":". 07-18-2018, 08:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T06:19:44-07:00","id":1236385,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236375#post1236375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the problem is that there was support for playing a member of the Gold Faction in Cult of the Illuminated and something similar for the Bronze Faction never emerged, so when it came to updating them for 2nd edition they felt like snidely whiplash villains (because that's basically what they are in that book) and quietly replaced them with the Independents.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> the bronze faction has the immaculates and the entire realm... everything.... to draw on","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malfeas F'Tagn - go check out my epic MLP/Exalted crossover &quot;The Scroll of Exalted ponies&quot; @ Fimfiction","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/588-webkilla","name":"webkilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar588_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T07:13:08-07:00","id":1236394,"title":"","content":"If that's true then Kejak is completely off his rocker by allowing the Civil War to go down now.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T07:18:16-07:00","id":1236396,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236363#post1236363\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Or they just wrote to what they thought was the tone of the gameline, or under instructions from a developer.</div> </div> </div> </div>From what i understood, there a clear bias from the author. It could have been a developer thing but there was a log of work to make sidereals just look incompetent even at their job. I'm glad that 3e will be ignoring it regardless because it was just obnoxious. Not only to get a terrible and borderline unsuable charmset just to rush the book out on time. But also because of how much was blatantly copied over.<br /> <br /> There were several things 1e edition did that were ruined in 2e. The first is removing the fact that sidereals control the fate of all directions. The maidens have them do things to ensure specific fates but the conventions exist for sidereals to plan the general destiny of things. This is how the bronze faction has controlled the fate of the realm 'off duty' but still due their tasks. Though,even if they control the fate of things, it's not mind control. They have to actively work to create a destiny. The civil war is looming because sidereals don't know who to back at this time and are trying to prevent it from happening due to the extent that it would weaken the realm. 2e made them more like a nucance to the bureau of fate that they put up like children, except for chejop who controls it all.<br /> <br /> Then there was the implication that bronze sidereals would purposely allow gold ones to die when the first edition emphaized that no matter what happened, sidereals were a fellowship. The only time it happened was the usurpation and that was the singular exception. This is in edition to all the minor unnecessary changes that were added as a screw you to sidereals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":116,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","user":"Epimetheus","date":". 07-18-2018, 10:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T08:12:48-07:00","id":1236410,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236396#post1236396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> From what i understood, there a clear bias from the author. It could have been a developer thing but there was a log of work to make sidereals just look incompetent even at their job.</div> </div> </div> </div>Everything that you're referring to seems circumstantial and highly speculative.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">2e made them more like a nucance to the bureau of fate that they put up like children, except for chejop who controls it all.</div> </div> </div> </div>I recall the Second Edition book as still having the directional conventions, that functioned in basically the same manner.<br /> <br /> The most dramatic change between Editions that I can think of was the addition of Oversight, and while that <i>could</i> be attributed a motivation of &quot;I hate the Sidereals, and want to include a setting element based around constantly hobbling them&quot;, it could also be attributed to a simple idea about adding some bureaucratic and secret service intrigue, which would not be out of place. I'm no fan of it myself, but I see no benefit to attributing it to some kind of enemy action.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then there was the implication that bronze sidereals would purposely allow gold ones to die when the first edition emphaized that no matter what happened, sidereals were a fellowship. The only time it happened was the usurpation and that was the singular exception.</div> </div> </div> </div>How does that demonstrate a bias against them?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is in edition to all the minor unnecessary changes that were added as a screw you to sidereals.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your conclusion seems pre-conceived enough to not really accommodate much discussion.<br /> <br /> You know, it's at times like this that I become interested in reading Exalted: the Lunars. I don't doubt that it's not the best book, but I'm curious to see exactly how much it aligns with the narrative that has sprung up around it.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T08:34:04-07:00","id":1236421,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236410#post1236410\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Everything that you're referring to seems circumstantial and highly speculative.</div> </div> </div> </div>I suppose that's true. Most of it I heard from other people.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I recall the Second Edition book as still having the directional conventions, that functioned in basically the same manner.<br /> <br /> The most dramatic change between Editions that I can think of was the addition of Oversight, and while that <i>could</i> be attributed a motivation of &quot;I hate the Sidereals, and want to include a setting element based around constantly hobbling them&quot;, it could also be attributed to a simple idea about adding some bureaucratic and secret service intrigue, which would not be out of place. I'm no fan of it myself, but I see no benefit to attributing it to some kind of enemy action. </div> </div> </div>The conventions were heavily reduced in addition to oversight being added. However, it was a huge difference. Sidereals didn't need oversight because they were allowed to mess with creation. That was the point of the conventions. They were still exalts with the creation-ruling mandate in place. There were things in 2e Sidereals, that resulted in them getting punished for doing things they are legally allowed to do just to make it seem like the Sidereals are just lackies. The entire set up for sidereals in 1e was do field jobs, gain power in both convention and bureau and plan fate. In 2e, it seems like only a few of the old guard had any power and the rest were just james bond which paints a much different picture. It's just one of many unnecessary changes just to reduce sidereal influence.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How does that demonstrate a bias against them? </div> </div> </div> This was just to show how little the people writing sidereals knew about them and one of many reasons I would suggest reading 1e.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your conclusion seems pre-conceived enough to not really accommodate much discussion. </div> </div> </div>These conclusions are actually rather recent from what I've heard from what people who played the game before I came into it have said, (though I will admit it is hearsay) and my recent re-readings of both books. I still would suggest the 1e lore over 2e because it just does a better job of paint sidereals in both a way that makes more sense to the setting and sidereal PCs.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T09:07:22-07:00","id":1236435,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236421#post1236421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This was just to show how little the people writing sidereals knew about them and one of many reasons I would suggest reading 1e.</div> </div> </div> </div>You know, I wouldn't actually dispute the idea that somebody who ever wrote Sad Ivory* or any other Sidereal as killing a fellow of a different faction did so because they were unaware of or forgot anything establishing that they don't do that.<br /> <br /> But I don't really think that's a bad thing, nor do I think that's a reasonable expectation to make of a freelance writer.<br /> <br /> Some things are probably extreme enough to need to be curtailed, such as that time somebody apparently wanted to state that the Incarnae were the souls of Cytherea, but I do think that sometimes mistakes or contradictions need to be accommodated both on the grounds that it's not really fair or practical to expect any given writer to know the lore exhaustively, and sometimes a deviation like that can produce worthwhile results.<br /> <br /> Certainly, this particular detail you're referring to seems marginal enough to be an odd example of &quot;showing how little they know about them&quot;. That seems like a criticism to give to something major and front-and-center, such as the number of Castes that they have, or being agents of Heaven.<br /> <br /> It didn't show that they knew little about them, just that they knew less than everything.<br /> <br /> * Does she count for this? Her write-up in the Second Edition core described that incident as being a shameful secret that she feared the consequences of, so that suggests that it exists in the paradigm of &quot;Sidereals killing each other is not done&quot;. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T09:35:30-07:00","id":1236445,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236435#post1236435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You know, I wouldn't actually dispute the idea that somebody who ever wrote Sad Ivory* or any other Sidereal as killing a fellow of a different faction did so because they were unaware of or forgot anything establishing that they don't do that.<br /> <br /> But I don't really think that's a bad thing, nor do I think that's a reasonable expectation to make of a freelance writer.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was less referring to sad Ivory and more to kejak's dreams write up as well as other implications that one would even think of sending assassins to deal with another sidereal. Also, considering how much the first book emphasized it, and how much of the book was clearly looked and lifted for a DIRECT copy paste. I would say it's a reasonable expectation to make.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T14:23:47-07:00","id":1236558,"title":"","content":"The addition of oversight doesn't turn the Sids into lackeys - plus, my reading of the 2nd ed lore about Oversight makes it out to be rather random, and very much up to the ST on how, if at all, its going to be used. Oversight is whatever you make of it, if it makes it into your game at all","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malfeas F'Tagn - go check out my epic MLP/Exalted crossover &quot;The Scroll of Exalted ponies&quot; @ Fimfiction","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/588-webkilla","name":"webkilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar588_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T14:47:24-07:00","id":1236566,"title":"","content":"When reading something, it's worth keeping in mind who it's written for and why it emphasizes what it does. Some of the things you're reading are aimed at different audiences or are intended to serve different purposes, which is why their emphasis differs and why they seem so different in tone. Roughly, there's three viewpoints here that the text takes at different points:<br /> <br /> 1. Broad setting coverage intended to set the backstory for the game and lay out its general tone. These tend to put an emphasis on how the Bronze faction has ruled the world, <i>especially</i> up until five years ago. When reading these it's also important to remember that the return of the Solars changed some things. Some of the stuff you're talking about - like Kejak purging Solar loyalists - happened a long time ago. He absolutely couldn't pull that today. The return of the Solar Exalted made supporting them an actually viable position rather than (to most of the gods) a point of meaningless doctrine, so most gods are going to be more cautious about who they back until the dust settles. The fact that he's nearing the end of his lifespan has also somewhat weakened his influence. It's worth pointing out that this emphasis that the Bronze Faction's grip is weakening isn't necessarily because the authors are &quot;biased&quot; against Solars - it gives players breathing room to tell their own stories. And not just Solar players or Gold Faction Sidereals - unrest in Heaven and threats to Bronze Faction supremacy are obvious plot hooks for Bronze Faction PCs in the same way that eg. the Bull of the North's main purpose is a plot-hook-slash-boss-encounter for dynastic Dragon-Blooded.<br /> <br /> 2. Things written for Sidereal players. These tend to emphasize the cool parts of being a Sidereal (ideally), but also the dangers and threats that the Five-Fold Fellowship faces and enemies suitable for you to go up against.<br /> <br /> 3. Things written for Anathema. These emphasize potential plot-hooks in Yu-Shan, as well as potential enemies within the Bronze Faction. So, naturally, you get two things - lots of Solar friendly gods for your ST to use as NPC supporters, and lots of emphasis on Kejak Chejop and a few other Bronze Faction Sidereals or affiliated gods as bosses.<br /> <br /> The last one is important because it's what gave CoCD: Yu-Shan the feel that there was a Solar-friendly deity hiding under every rock. That's not intended (and it's not how the overall setting is described), but the book is written from the assumption that running an Anathema game in Yu-Shan is going to be intrinsically difficult without a friendly deity to provide you a foothold, so several are provided.<br /> <br /> I do think that the a lot of the 2e write-up of Yu-Shan lacked nuance. It isn't a matter of it being biased towards one thing or the other, it's a matter of everyone and everything being written too heavily towards one role. I think that the book both over-emphasized Sidereal influence in Yu-Shan and over-emphasized how many gods still <i>care</i> about the Solar Exalted one way or the other, even now that they're back. The vast majority of Yu-Shan is corrupt and decadent. The politics there need to be deeper than just &quot;Kejak uses Ryzala as a puppet&quot;, too. (And, generally, there needs to be more focus on what the gods care about other than Solars, what political factions they break into and so on. It's bad enough to have Sidereal politics completely centered on the Solars, but at least they have the Great Prophecy. Most gods are going to have other concerns.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T15:02:23-07:00","id":1236570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>webkilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236558#post1236558\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The addition of oversight doesn't turn the Sids into lackeys - plus, my reading of the 2nd ed lore about Oversight makes it out to be rather random, and very much up to the ST on how, if at all, its going to be used. Oversight is whatever you make of it, if it makes it into your game at all</div> </div> </div> </div>As I've stated, it wasn't just oversight it was a lot of things. Especially how the bureaus and conventions work in 2e which are different enough from 1e to be noted as a demotion. Not just that but the times sidereals have been censured for things they able legally able to do because they too are part of the creation ruling mandate. Yet, time and again the sidereals or the yu-shan book forgets this or ignores this fact.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T15:46:50-07:00","id":1236586,"title":"","content":"I have to disagree with your theory Aquillion, concerning the pro-Solar, neutral, or previously staunch Bronze-loyalist and now wavering deities being simple hooks/resources for a game foothold.<br /> 1) There's a rhyme and reason to the pattern of weakening Bronze influence. Example: At one point, Ryzala was willing to engage in framing pro-Solar deities for Yozi worship, and not flinching when those deities got smelted into Starmetal on those bogus charges. Cue the Solar Return, and five years later she won't even allow a Solar in Yu-Shan to be attacked by the Bronze. That's a paraphrasing of a direct quote. Nara-O, Kejak's direct (theoretical) superior, is shown in several phases of his weakening faith in Kejak's vision for Creation. 2E CoCD: Yu-Shan made a deliberate choice to retain/re-iterate his support of the Solars, in exchange for Lupo bringing a Solar to visit him once a year. It wasn't a copy-paste mention, but something written by someone who read the CotI info, and chose to work it into the CoCD: Yu-Shan treatment of Nara-O.<br /> <br /> 2) A DISTINCT portion of the pattern present is that those pro-Solar deities who HAVE managed to survive all this time in a Bronze-dominated Heaven are showing distinct signs of their stars beginning to rise again. Taru-Han is growing more and more obsessed with her illegal soul collection, and her relative indifference to anything beyond simply retaining her position is making &quot;some Gods wonder if standing by while Lytek, the Right Hand of Power was supplanted was truly the right decision.&quot; Add this to the text talking about the Gods/Goddesses attempting to badger the Golden Lord out of retirement in An-Teng, despite the Golden Lord's protests he's happy where he is.<br /> <br /> 3) In counterpart to #2 above, you've got all the stuff about various Bronze deities making uber-illegal moves. Ruvia's lieutenant wants to assassinate him, but Ruvia has learned about it. Laughing Ragamuffin, Uvanavu's subordinate is manipulating fate to hide the Heroin-producing Beasts of Resplendent Liquid belonging to the Guild, and is getting blackmailed over it. Hui Dai Ling wants to kick off the Realm Civil War, etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> 4) Talking about where all Sidereals have been pulled from, who controls what Direction, which Factions control what Committees...it seems like some developer started trying very hard to quantify how much control each Faction had, but then someone else continued adhering to the &quot;The Bronze controls 90%, the Gold 10%&quot; while ignoring stuff like Kejak using both the East as a &quot;dumping ground for Gold-Faction malcontents&quot; and the Sub-Convention on Essence Wielders as a drain on Ayesha Uru's time and resources, and how much Kejak now regrets those decisions in 20/20 hindsight due to the Solar Return. Stating Chejop Kejak not only can't get a Bronze seated as Chair of the Convention of Air, but is burning all kinds of favors simply to keep the Chair open was CERTAINLY intended to be a statement concerning weakening Bronze influence. Especially when taken in tandem with the repeated mention that more Sidereals are opting-out of Factional politics in favor of Nazri's Independents.<br /> <br /> As for a lack of nuance in CoCD: Yu-Shan, I thought the detailed write-ups of how the various Bureaus prey on one another, which Bureaus are allied, which enemies, and which Bureaus have how much clout relative to other Bureaus were excellent. I do DEARLY wish that the Secret Societies written up had been linked more meaningfully to the Bureaus those Gods must be part of. Using their jobs, and the favors their positions can grant, while wielding those favors received to advance their agendas is what I'd expect those Gods to care about, and a big opportunity was missed by the Devs not doing that, at least IMHO.<br /> <br /> Regarding Chejop uses Ryzala as a puppet: I thought there was a tight narrative about this. Kejak used his Bronze Faction to help a then-very minor deity Ryzala a) Get assigned to the Yozi-worship-investigating position, and then b) Helped Ryzala use that position to conduct a pogrom against pro-Solar deities, so the executed gods could be replaced with Bronze-loyal deities. That devil's bargain quid pro quo welded them together into the partnership which allowed the Bronze Faction to dominate. For me at least, it captured that &quot;Characters you love to hate&quot; sort of magic, which pushed my dislike of Kejak as a Hard Man Making Hard Choices into an outright fanatic more interested in being right than doing right.<br /> <br /> I prefaced this with saying I found the Sidereal/Yu-Shan material to be schizophrenic in result, but I don't believe that's all attributable to &quot;They meant the Bronze Faction really does dominate everything, and all this Gold/Pro-Solar stuff was accidentally blown out of proportion. There's simply too many examples of it for it to be that, again IMHO.<br /> <br /> After all, the return of the Solars was supposed to have given the Gold Faction new life. As for their only being 3-5 Elder Sidereals in the whole Faction, there are literally that many Elder Sidereals simply running the two written-up Cult training camps, and the 1E Cult book says it left the third training-camp, the one in the East unwritten so Storytellers could create it themselves. There's no reason to believe that camp doesn't have its own Elder Sidereal running it. More than that, I believe it strains all credence to believe there are NO Elder Gold-Faction Sidereals beyond those specifically written up. Especially since the logical inference of the statement that the Gold Faction is gaining ground because of the Solar Return would be that some of the previously middle-of-the-road Independents have drifted into the Gold. It's a lot easier to drift into the left aisle from the middle, than it is to &quot;turn traitor&quot; and cross to the left from the right.<br /> <br /> I'll freely admit I don't know where the % of Bronze-Vs-Gold control of Yu-Shan should lay, but I believe there's too much material piled up to say &quot;Nope, the Devs meant the Bronze control everything, and all this Gold-increasing/Bronze-weakening material just fell out of a gremlin's ass and landed on the printing press, to accidentally get published.&quot; Why repeat the number of Wyld Hunt Chapterhouses are down from 14 to 6 in CoCDs: North, South and East, otherwise?<br /> <br /> One of the things I MOST hope to get from 3E is a much more quantifiable answer to these issues. Leaving things open enough for Storytellers to put their own stamp on things is one thing. Not being able to tell what the canon intention actually is, well, that's something else altogether.<br /> <br /> Edit: I do think the story-foothold/plot-hook angle has something to it though. Mentioning things like the reason Yaogin is always dosing himself from the Golden Ewer is because he's in agony ever since an Elder Lunar poisoned him. Or any of the other tidbits about the various seedy things Gods are doing that they could be busted for if discovered...It's this stuff I think they intended to be the plothook fodder that answers the question &quot;What do the Gods care about, other than Solars and Sidereal Politics?&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-18-2018, 06:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T16:47:22-07:00","id":1236613,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236586#post1236586\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of the things I MOST hope to get from 3E is a much more quantifiable answer to these issues. Leaving things open enough for Storytellers to put their own stamp on things is one thing. Not being able to tell what the canon intention actually is, well, that's something else altogether.</div> </div> </div> </div>If they stick with 1e over 2e, a lot of these things will most likely change. The politics between 1e and 2e in yu-shan was similar in a lot of places (Because they were direct copy pastes in most cases, then tacking on material). Like seasons, but then different in a lot of others or rather they were notably absent to let story tellers flesh things out for themselves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T17:43:07-07:00","id":1236630,"title":"","content":"I don't really use the Bronze or Gold factions at all. In my personal head cannon the only people who consider themselves part of either are First Age survivors and they're only about three of them left after the Great Contagion, Fair Folk invasion, assassination, boredom, accidents, etc. Not to say that individual Sids don't have an opinion on the topic, but it was two thousand years ago. To me it's kinda like fighting over whether or not Caesar was good or bad for Rome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1882-ghost","name":"Ghost","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1882_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T17:57:12-07:00","id":1236635,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghost</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236630#post1236630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't really use the Bronze or Gold factions at all. In my personal head cannon the only people who consider themselves part of either are First Age survivors and they're only about three of them left after the Great Contagion, Fair Folk invasion, assassination, boredom, accidents, etc. Not to say that individual Sids don't have an opinion on the topic, but it was two thousand years ago. To me it's kinda like fighting over whether or not Caesar was good or bad for Rome.</div> </div> </div> </div>The point of the factions is that the great prophesy as far as the bronze faction are concerned didn't end with the usurpation. It began with it. As far as the bronze sidereals believe, the solars will still bring ruin to creation if they aren't dealt with. It's why they aggressively hunt solars and made the immaculate philosophy. Though there are a lot of new sidereals who haven't experienced the first age so they don't know which side to go with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T21:13:01-07:00","id":1236667,"title":"","content":"Agreed Epimetheus,<br /> The Bronze Faction says the Solars would have annihilated Creation, and the Usurpation was the only rational response. The Gold, meanwhile, (and IMHO accurately) attributes the Great Contagion and the Balorian Crusade to Bronze Faction expediency. Chejop Kejak's and Anys Syn's rejoinder to the 90%+ kill-rate of the Contagion, and the annihilation of 40% of Creation was &quot;Better a horrible ending, than horrors without end.&quot; Gold Sidereals can't wrap their minds around what they perceive as such inhuman, and (in their minds) ultimately needless callousness.<br /> <br /> Objectively, Canon weighed in that the Bronze Faction came within a hair's breadth of killing 100% of everything alive. Without the ghosts of the Usurped Solars Larquen Quen and Kal Bax working at cross-purposes to their peer, the Dowager, the Wyld energies accompanying the first surge of the Balorian Crusade into Creation wouldn't have been there to provide the inherited immunity in the living creatures still alive. Finally allowing the Contagion to burn out.<br /> <br /> In a way, the Bronze Faction was right. Solars DID bring about the end of Creation they foresaw. Dead Solars they Usurped. No Usurpation, and the Radiant Dowager would have continued working to catalog every Thaumaturgical Procedure the Primordials programmed into Creation.<br /> <br /> It's definitely Serious Business to the Sidereals, and for the life of me, I cannot grasp how the whole &quot;Sidereals may belong to different Factions, but there's still solidarity among the members of the Five-Score Fellowship is supposed to work. If you're Gold, you believe your Bronze Faction counterparts murdered and imprisoned the Exaltations which would've prevented the deaths of the Contagion and the annihilation of the Crusade. If you're Bronze, you view your Gold Faction counterparts as traitors to Creation, trying to bring about a resurgence of the beings you believe are the greatest threat there has ever been to Creation.<br /> <br /> Nor is it all political. Ghataru and Nasri are ready to deploy the Aerial Legion to protect the Solars in the Gold's CotI-training camps. To me, that reads like pre-WWI, just waiting for the bullet to hit Archduke Francis Ferdinand. The Realm has a pretty solid footprint in the South, unlike most of the rest of the Threshold. If a Wyld Hunt is going to chase a Solar anywhere (other than the Blessed Isle, of course) they're going to chase them in the South. Chiaroscuro, Paragon, the Lap, and the Varang City-States are all either Satrapies or at least tribute-payers nominally friendly to the Realm. Unlike literally every other Direction, there's no &quot;A Solar can walk openly, just so long as they aren't causing chaos and getting all totemic in the center of the capitol city&quot; locations. A Wyld Hunt manages to keep a bead on a Solar being moved by Cult members, and decides it wants to roll up all these Anathema-contaminated confederates of the fugitive Anathema, so their harriers hang back and keep watch. Meanwhile, the Solar is getting moved closer and closer to Kether Rock. At some point, the Solar is moved from the last safe-house onto the open desert, on the way to Kether Rock, and the Wyld Hunt decides its seen all the players aiding the Anathema it's going to see and moves in.<br /> <br /> Except (from the perspective of Maduka Shin) the Wyld Hunt is perceived as being close enough to Kether Rock as to jeopardize the fortress's secrecy, and the Wyld Hunt gets wiped off the map. That's not the sort of thing the Bronze will tolerate. Not in this era where Shikari are all-too-few. They can't just have the Realm march some legions into the deep desert. Not with every Dynastic House either making their own bid for the throne, or trying to play kingmaker. Every House is skittish of suffering the fate of House Tepet. They have precisely ZERO meaningful Heavenly military assets. The Aerial Legion is a very Pro-Solar institution. Even once it was clear the Solars were finished, Ghataru and the Legion were STILL willing to antagonize the Bronze Faction by building a memorial for one of the last Solars, when said Solar died on a run to reach the Jade Pleasure Dome and confront TUS. Several Sidereals died bringing that Solar down, and the Legion thumbed their noses at the Bronze Sidereals with that memorial-grave. Ghataru wants a worthy Dawn-Caste apprentice, so he can turn over control of the Legion to them. That's pretty pro-Solar.<br /> <br /> So, temporal and Heavenly military response is out. What's that leave, beyond just ignoring the fact the Gold Faction just wiped out personnel Chejop Kejak was successful in listing as &quot;Assets of the Bureau of Destiny?&quot; The entire situation is on the point of boiling over. It's immovable object/irresistible force-type ideological conflict. Kejak and Ayesha Uru have exhausted themselves keeping their cold war cold, but the status quo has a countdown. (Not just Chejop Kejak's lifespan. I don't know how 3E is intending to handle it, but the Reclamation has its own pressures to exert.) Forget the Lotus Massacre. Once Chejop Kejak is gone, Anys Syn will be the ranking Bronze Faction Sidereal. Thanks to Elder Syn's mastery of Infernal Hero/Monster Style, the Yozis can run her like a sock-puppet for a scene. A scene can be a long time. Certainly long enough to have horrible consequences. Which itself would have consequences for the Five Score Fellowship. It's the Elders who are the masters of Infernal Monster Style. If/when the Yozis pull that trigger, the non-compromised Sidereals won't magically know the Yozi mind-control was a one shot. Which Faction has more Elders that could be disgraced by this revelation?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T21:56:24-07:00","id":1236676,"title":"","content":"You're using canon, that will probably no longer exist. Which has been part of my point. I imagine the gods diving up factions will still be a thing. However, the reclamation is no longer a thing in 3e and we don't even know if Infernal Monster style will remain as is.<br /> <br /> The thing that 1e emphasized which was lost in 2e and kind brought back in 3e, is that solars were going fully insane. Yes, there was a chance to bring some back to sanity. However, without making them all see what kind of monsters they were becoming, the creation was going to become a living nightmare dictated by the whims of 300 insane and ultra powerful godkings. The contagion sucked, but it was still preferable to what to what the future was going to hold. With 3e's attempts to look at 1e, we will be dealing with a different playing field then the one you are talking about. The part that 1e emphasized wasn't that the gold thinks the bronze was wrong during the usurpation. It's that the usurpation, is done and over with. Now that the solars are younger they are more pliable. And make no mistake, the gold faction sidereal's don't want the solars to rule unopposed again. They want to control them so that they no longer pose the same unchecked power they once had.<br /> <br /> In addition, the reason the factions don't fight is because they are working to prevent creation from being erased from existence. They work for the bureau because without them the loom of creation would rip apart, and thus creation would fall back into the wyld. The future that they dictate, is what they do when they are &quot;off the clock.&quot; They may get in each other's way, they may sabotage each other's work indirectly, but they are not going to kill each other because they are only a hundred exalts. Each sidereal lost, is another that won't be around for another 18-60 years. Each elder sidereal lost, is a fountain of knowledge and wisdom that is indispensable to protection creation. In addition, they don't have the man power to sustain the losses from an internal war. So they have be in a position to never fight one another.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-18T22:31:16-07:00","id":1236680,"title":"","content":"Wait,<br /> You're telling me then that 3E is essentially making the Gold/Bronze divide irrelevant? They're just throwing out the whole Gold Sidereals disagreed with the Usurpation to begin with, got murdered on Kejak's orders for trying to warn the Solars? What was the initial conflict with the disagreeing Sidereals about if everyone thought the Usurpation justified? If this is the case, they're basically killing the Splat IMHO. If the only difference between the Bronze and Gold is boiled down to &quot;Bronze Kills Solars, Gold wants to use the returned Solars&quot; it removes every bit of the moral issue. Things like Chejop Kejak and Tammiz Ushun fundamentally disagreeing on the way forward was at the heart of, well, everything meaningful about the Usurpation. A very meaningful part of the conflict, from the perspective of members of the two Factions, was the resultant fallout of the Usurpation. It removes the meaning of WHY the Gold wants to use their Solars to supplant the Realm, if they think the Bronze are moral, level-headed guys just trying to do their job as best they can.<br /> <br /> I'd have to see the Developer Info on this to believe such a poorly-considered change is to be made. Your point about why the Factions don't fight is presently the reason Nazri's Independents exist. The Sidereals who are so sick of the Faction antagonism that is actively hindering the Five Score Fellowship from doing the job the Bureau of Destiny was made to do are opting out of the Either-Or Mentality of Bronze/Gold politics.<br /> <br /> That would essentially be a rewrite of the entire Exalted cosmology. I'm sure there are people who would love to see the Sidereal-Yu-Shan stuff go the way you say, but just as many who (like me) would find themselves sighing because we have to throw out huge chunks of the new books to make things make sense.<br /> <br /> I don't recall seeing mention of this in the new Core, but I've only briefly looked over WFHW beyond the Core, so I'm by no means a 3E authority. I DO know that 1E Core didn't represent the pre-Usurpation Sidereal ideology as nearly so reasonable. It only gets touched on a bit there, due to the Sidereals getting like a page, same as all the other Exalts and the Fair Folk, but...<br /> <br /> If the Faction Politics aren't impeding the Sidereals, it also pretty well deletes the rationale for there to be Independents, and I'm not at all sure that's a good choice. Can you point me to any of the Developer Info on this? I'd really like to read up on this.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T02:08:37-07:00","id":1236696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236680#post1236680\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wait,<br /> You're telling me then that 3E is essentially making the Gold/Bronze divide irrelevant? They're just throwing out the whole Gold Sidereals disagreed with the Usurpation to begin with, got murdered on Kejak's orders for trying to warn the Solars? What was the initial conflict with the disagreeing Sidereals about if everyone thought the Usurpation justified? If this is the case, they're basically killing the Splat IMHO. If the only difference between the Bronze and Gold is boiled down to &quot;Bronze Kills Solars, Gold wants to use the returned Solars&quot; it removes every bit of the moral issue. Things like Chejop Kejak and Tammiz Ushun fundamentally disagreeing on the way forward was at the heart of, well, everything meaningful about the Usurpation. A very meaningful part of the conflict, from the perspective of members of the two Factions, was the resultant fallout of the Usurpation. It removes the meaning of WHY the Gold wants to use their Solars to supplant the Realm, if they think the Bronze are moral, level-headed guys just trying to do their job as best they can.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>No. I guess it wasn't clear though but the oldest gold faction member is only like 800 years old. The Gold faction is only full of new younger sidereals. They don't remember the usurpation except in a few memories. The Gold divide happened because Ayesha remembered old memories of the solars and wanted to bring it back. She along with a few others decided to start it. However, for about 795 years it didn't have much traction. It had about a handful of people in the convention of the wood. It's only recently that it's been gaining more members. Newer sidereals don't remember the first age thus have no tie to it and it's still a small faction compared to what the bronze has. At the same time, a lot of what people miss is that the Gold don't inherently disagree with the fact that the solars were going crazy, only that it might have been possible to redeem them. The divide between the two isn't about the past. It's about the future. The bronze want to continue upholding the great prophesy and kill solars. The Gold believe they can be good again. Independents just want to do their job and the factions have always been duties that have applied in addition to their job. Both factions expect members to do extra work and some people don't want to do extra work.<br /> <br /> Also, this is a paragraph about the usurpation, which until sidereals 3e is a thing, is much more black and white about it. From page 21 of the 3e core.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Solars had labored for millennia under a great curse lain upon them by the vanquished enemies of the gods. In time the Solars grew wicked, deceitful and mad; their magnanimity turned to spite, their justice to tyranny. They began to turn against their subjects and each other, and the world was soon threatened by the very sorceries and machinations the Solars had used to protect it. As civil war loomed, the other Exalted saw no choice but to remove their masters. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":116,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","user":"Epimetheus","date":". 07-19-2018, 05:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T02:51:49-07:00","id":1236704,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236680#post1236680\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're telling me then that 3E is essentially making the Gold/Bronze divide irrelevant?</div> </div> </div> </div>I would characterise it as consolidating the relevance by focusing it on a time when Solars actually exist in the world again.<br /> <br /> Between old developer statements and the write-up in the Third Edition core, it seems to me that the basis of the Gold faction was intended to be the catastrophes of the Second Age being something that gave a number of Sidereals cause to revise and regret the Usurpation, and see the return of the Solars as an opportunity for redemption and making up for their mistakes.<br /> <br /> The nature of divisions within the Sidereals at the time of the Usurpation is not yet elaborated on; maybe it will be that dissenters were eliminated, maybe it will be that when the group cloistered together to divine and decide their future, they agreed from the outset to act as a single bloc and follow the decisions of the majority.<br /> <br /> I think the latter could be an intriguing premise for the history of the Sidereals and personality of some of the more middle aged or older members; the sense that placing the cohesion of them as a group over doing what they individually (or collectively as a sub-group) thought was right or proper, was a mistake that needs to be made up for. That the Time of Tumult is coming to a head of creating a schism in the Five-score Fellowship such that there has ever been.<br /> <br /> I've had arguments against the idea that previous premises of the Sidereal Exalted amounted to fighting a dispute that took place a thousand years ago, but I have to admit that in the face of possibly making the main breaking point of the group be something that happens in the present day, it has its weaknesses.<br /> <br /> But this is mostly speculative, not least because the developers have changed since the core was released.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If this is the case, they're basically killing the Splat IMHO. If the only difference between the Bronze and Gold is boiled down to &quot;Bronze Kills Solars, Gold wants to use the returned Solars&quot; it removes every bit of the moral issue. Things like Chejop Kejak and Tammiz Ushun fundamentally disagreeing on the way forward was at the heart of, well, everything meaningful about the Usurpation. A very meaningful part of the conflict, from the perspective of members of the two Factions, was the resultant fallout of the Usurpation. It removes the meaning of WHY the Gold wants to use their Solars to supplant the Realm, if they think the Bronze are moral, level-headed guys just trying to do their job as best they can.</div> </div> </div> </div>It sounds to me like you're saying that the Usurpation is only meaningful if the people behind it were not merely divided, but ruthlessly uncompromising and ready to demonise the opposition.<br /> <br /> As I think about it, considering some of the themes and premises of the Sidereals (namely that, for all of their mystical trappings and associations, they're still deeply rooted in a bureaucratic system and themselves partially consist of a number of planning committees), it seems to me entirely fitting to have an idea that there were people involved that were morally compromised for the sake of procedure and consensus. Who went along with something that they didn't wholly agree with or believe in, because they viewed the group breaking apart as being even more dangerous. That to finally break in the Time of Tumult might be a stronger narrative than having divided centuries ago, and only just getting the means to do anything about it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd have to see the Developer Info on this to believe such a poorly-considered change is to be made.</div> </div> </div> </div>Let's say for the sake of argument that my ideas are the ideas that a developer would have. Are they poorly considered?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">That would essentially be a rewrite of the entire Exalted cosmology.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it's something that's operating on a bit of a smaller scale than the cosmology.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the Faction Politics aren't impeding the Sidereals, it also pretty well deletes the rationale for there to be Independents, and I'm not at all sure that's a good choice.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, even within the premise that there's been a meaningful divide between Sidereals for ages, I don't think that Independents are the most useful or interesting part of the group. Besides being a supposed voice of reason to tut his fellows, what does Nasri really have going for him as a character?<br /> <br /> That being said, I think that within the premise as I have laid it out in this post, there's still a place for Sidereals who hold themselves apart from conflict about how to respond to the return of the Solars, because there are still other things for them to be worried about. I wouldn't say that Bronze and Gold Faction Solars are ignoring the likes of Rakan Thulio and his Getimian Exalted followers, or the black market on Exaltations, or threats related to the Deathlords and Fair Folk and who knows what else; on the contrary, either side might have different ideas about whether or not the presence of Solars makes any of those things better or worse. But Independents might be those people who think that any of those threats needs to be an even higher priority, and for the group to be divided or distracted is ceding too much ground to them. There once was a united front against the War on Heaven, and then the Solars return and everything comes to a head between those who adamantly believe in the justification for their removal and those that harboured doubts and regrets that have become exacerbated by every disaster since then, and in the middle are those who are trying to remind them that their house was burning down when this argument started.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T07:26:12-07:00","id":1236763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236696#post1236696\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">ople don't want to do extra work.<br /> <br /> Also, this is a paragraph about the usurpation, which until sidereals 3e is a thing, is much more black and white about it. From page 21 of the 3e core.<br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><i><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The Solars had labored for millennia under a great curse lain upon them by the vanquished enemies of the gods. In time the Solars grew wicked, deceitful and mad; their magnanimity turned to spite, their justice to tyranny. They began to turn against their subjects and each other, and the world was soon threatened by the very sorceries and machinations the Solars had used to protect it. As civil war loomed, the other Exalted saw no choice but to remove their masters.</span></i></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>Anyone else notice it says &quot;other Exalted&quot; and not &quot;Sidereals and Dragonblood&quot;?<br /> I'm not sure how much to read into it; it may just be hyperbolic synonymy. But it's interesting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T07:31:30-07:00","id":1236767,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236763#post1236763\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyone else notice it says &quot;other Exalted&quot; and not &quot;Sidereals and Dragonblood&quot;?<br /> I'm not sure how much to read into it; it may just be hyperbolic synonymy. But it's interesting.</div> </div> </div> </div>It does explicitly mention dragon blooded and sidereals in later passages. Though, since exigents are things, it could imply that they helped.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":116,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","user":"Epimetheus","date":". 07-19-2018, 11:08 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T15:30:40-07:00","id":1236924,"title":"","content":"Apologies,<br /> I realize I wasn't at ALL clear for my reasons why my ancient history-centric view of the Sidereal divide is relevant to modern Sidereals. If the Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade are retained, then it follows even the most foolish, oblivious Sidereal would be able to recognize the removal of the Solars and the exiling of the Lunars essentially opened the door for both events. By going with the idea that the Sidereals were united behind the Usurpation, no one in Heaven is essentially responsible for those catastrophes, because they all are, meaning either a) The write-up will be none of the Sidereals accept responsibility for the Contagion/Crusade being their fault for eliminating the Solars, which kills story hooks, or b) The write-up becomes those who perceive their responsibility are only diverging from the Bronze out of guilt and horror for what they helped usher in.<br /> <br /> Essentially, achieved consensus among Sidereals results in a) Deletes the entire &quot;The Sidereals own Great Curse lead them to usher in the very horrors they sought to stave off by eliminating the Solars&quot; tragedy. Which kills in one blow the tragedy of the Sidereals having been just as corrupted by the Solars, but ironically the Viziers were too blinded by their own corruption to see it, b) It eliminates the moral implications, which lead to brother-against-brother, lover against lover conflict between not Villain and Hero, but two groups who both perceive themselves as heroes trying to save Creation, and willing to sacrifice even their bonds within a brotherhood/sisterhood so strong the other Exalts gave them their famous name. If it all just diverges over 20/20 hindsight, there are no Sidereals who are in the slightest bit heroic. Just a group who lost their stomach for the choice they made.<br /> <br /> It moves the Sidereals down to be on par as a whole with the Abyssals and Infernals, instead of being a complex, nuanced splat. I was just reading the Dev Q&amp;A, only up to part 2, but I've read 4 times where Holden repeats how EX3 is supposed to have this optimistic vibe in a world with very real, very dark problems. The corruption of the Celestial Bureaucracy and the indifference of the Incarnae, coupled with ALL the Sidereals having no moral leg to stand on doesn't have the slightest optimistic feel to it, IMHO. The first two do an admirable job of the real, very dark problems, without dragging the entire splat down into negativity.<br /> <br /> More importantly, the Gold decision to keep the new Solars in the dark regarding the Sidereal role in the Usurpation takes on ENTIRELY different connotations in a United Sidereal Usurpation. Right now, it's at least morally ambiguous. The Gold having been willing in the past to try and redeem the Solars without resorting to the Usurpation gives them the credibility to say that keeping the Solars in the dark IS simply trying not to complicate the present with a past no one can change. Yet in a United Sidereal Usurpation, the Gold Sidereals keeping the returned Solars in the dark is simply conspirators still engaged in a conspiracy to continue perpetrating an Age-long deceit. It darkens the Gold agenda into something just as ugly as the Bronze agenda, and THAT I find exceptionally off-putting and repellent.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T16:06:12-07:00","id":1236935,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236924#post1236924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> By going with the idea that the Sidereals were united behind the Usurpation, no one in Heaven is essentially responsible for those catastrophes, because they all are</div> </div> </div> </div>That makes no sense whatsoever.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The write-up will be none of the Sidereals accept responsibility for the Contagion/Crusade being their fault for eliminating the Solars, which kills story hooks</div> </div> </div> </div>How? Why? Do you think that people only ever realise that they're complicit in things if somebody else blames them for it? You don't eliminate the idea of Sidereals who realise that they bet on the horse, just ones who don't unilaterally blame others.<br /> <br /> Except one can still have that possibility, because deferring responsibility and passing the buck is a fairly common response to a mutual disaster, especially from an administrative group.<br /> <br /> The only thing that really gets eliminated is the potential for one side to be perceived as a lot more correct than the other, and act self-righteous about it.<br /> <br /> \u2026 No, no even the latter is something that can be kept in some form, since the majority of living Sidereals did not take part in the Usurpation at all, and so can form political opinions on it retrospectively. Which has always been the case; it's just that now, stories will have to labour under the burden of herself (leader of the Gold Faction, whose name completely escapes me at the moment) forming that position based on her own evaluations, rather than being the inheritor of the Essence of the most ardent supporter of the Solars.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The write-up becomes those who perceive their responsibility are only diverging from the Bronze out of guilt and horror for what they helped usher in.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, we're raising the mutual questions of how many contemporary Sidereals actively participated in that action, and... soooo?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Essentially, achieved consensus among Sidereals results in a) Deletes the entire &quot;The Sidereals own Great Curse lead them to usher in the very horrors they sought to stave off by eliminating the Solars&quot; tragedy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Still makes no sense.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which kills in one blow the tragedy of the Sidereals having been just as corrupted by the Solars, but ironically the Viziers were too blinded by their own corruption to see it</div> </div> </div> </div>Nnnooo it doesn't. They still did The Thing. Does a dubious decision only count as such if somebody has openly dangled a possibly better alternative in your face, and you wilfully rejected it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It eliminates the moral implications, which lead to brother-against-brother, lover against lover conflict between not Villain and Hero, but two groups who both perceive themselves as heroes trying to save Creation, and willing to sacrifice even their bonds within a brotherhood/sisterhood so strong the other Exalts gave them their famous name.</div> </div> </div> </div>It means that all of that conflict gets to take place in the game that you actually play, rather than in the backstory.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If it all just diverges over 20/20 hindsight, there are no Sidereals who are in the slightest bit heroic.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would argue that there is something at least as heroic against somebody managing to revise a bad choice and/or go against the social pressures of the status quo, rather than retreating into rationalisation and obstinance regardless of the consequences. People changing their minds over important things, things that they've been told their whole lives is the norm, is actually pretty damn hard.<br /> <br /> Do you not think there's anything the slightest bit heroic in, say, a reformed member of a bigoted terrorist group not only reversing their stance, but actively campaigning for tolerance and against their former compatriots?<br /> <br /> Or are you only heroic if you made what somebody considers to be the correct choice the first time?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just a group who lost their stomach for the choice they made.</div> </div> </div> </div>The choice that four or five incarnations ago and your mentor and boss made.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It moves the Sidereals down to be on par as a whole with the Abyssals and Infernals, instead of being a complex, nuanced splat.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's about three things wrong with this sentence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">More importantly, the Gold decision to keep the new Solars in the dark regarding the Sidereal role in the Usurpation takes on ENTIRELY different connotations in a United Sidereal Usurpation. Right now, it's at least morally ambiguous. The Gold having been willing in the past to try and redeem the Solars without resorting to the Usurpation gives them the credibility to say that keeping the Solars in the dark IS simply trying not to complicate the present with a past no one can change. Yet in a United Sidereal Usurpation, the Gold Sidereals keeping the returned Solars in the dark is simply conspirators still engaged in a conspiracy to continue perpetrating an Age-long deceit. It darkens the Gold agenda into something just as ugly as the Bronze agenda, and THAT I find exceptionally off-putting and repellent.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't agree with... any of this, but it might actually be a basis for saying that the Cult of the Illuminated should not be written to keep such secrets from the Solars that it associates with anymore.<br /> <br /> If it was, a person so inclined <i>could</i> frame that in terms of the Sidereals having concerns that describing the Usurpation to Solars will shift their priorities away from saving the world. Possibly a bit concerned by a similar result to what happened with the Lunars; when you're four or five incarnations away from somebody who took part in the insurrection, it might create a sense of caution to have any memories of what two or three of those incarnations experienced at the hands of a Lunar before becoming the next incarnation.<br /> <br /> Also... ugly as the Bronze agenda? The Bronze Faction has some rather valid concerns about the Solar Exalted.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T19:55:16-07:00","id":1236989,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> 1) A Unified Usurpation DOES remove the moral element. The &quot;Resorting to twisting the Dragon Blooded into murder-weapons, and using our position as the trusted advisors of our Exalted cousins to massacre the Solars, imprison their Exaltations, and hound the Lunars into the Wyld with death-squads is *evil.*&quot; Versus the &quot;The Solars have strayed badly, but we have as much a duty to try and help them as we do to the rest of Creation&quot; being &quot;Good.&quot;<br /> <br /> In the mode you describe, the Bronze/Gold divide becomes a Utility Calculation. We (the now-Gold) find the consequences of the Usurpation, in hindsight, unacceptable. Let's use the returning Solars to supplant our comrades vision for Creation. There would be a complete silence on the fact that using dastardly, low means, lead to (shocker) a low, dastardly future.<br /> <br /> What I meant by saying if all Sidereals are responsible, then none of them are is: There's no external faction saying &quot;See, we told you this would end badly.&quot; The idea that no meaningful number of the Exalts called Seers of the Loom of Fate grasped that the total removal of 3/7ths of the Celestial Exalts, and the complete marginalization of the remaining 3/7ths had enormous potential to result in the very apocalypse the Bronze sought to forestall is well...stupid, to me. More than that, a Unified Usurpation retcons the meaningful example of the Sidereal Great Curse from existence, and that makes no sense to me when their Exalt-group has the highest concentration of surviving Elder Exalts. Out of all Exalt-groups, the Great Curse should be wracking the Sidereals the worst. Without the division at the time of the Great Prophecy, there's no meaningful sign of it at work.<br /> <br /> 2) I get the sense (I'll apologize if I've misconstrued your intent) you are a member of the camp that wants the Sidereal to be a Five Score Fellowship first, and Exalts with serious differences second, if ever. Your comment about the Independents being boring, I took as a sign of distaste for the idea of the Bronze/Gold divide being anything more than a philosophical difference. Personally, I feel that Bronze and Gold Sidereals SHOULD want each other dead. Every dead rival Faction-member is another chance to dispose of an Exalt hardened against your cause, and simultaneously a new opportunity to woo the inheritor of their Exaltation to your side while they're new and haven't yet had a chance to become objectively informed. I loved the quote that summed up the extent of cooperation between Bronze and Gold Sidereals regarding field-work. &quot;You don't sabotage a situation to the point it can't be resolved. That will get you audited and severely punished. You sabotage it in such a way that YOUR RIVAL can't successfully resolve the situation.&quot; I loved the idea that as each year of the Solar Return wears on, the tensions between Bronze and Gold keep exponentially increasing, to the point the first premeditated murder of a Gold Sidereal by one of the Bronze recently happened.<br /> <br /> ANYTHING that breaks up the Special Sidereal Solidarity that makes them an unbelievable aberrant among Celestial Exalts.<br /> <br /> Even if we return to 1E completely though, the Sidereals weren't that. The Cult of the Illuminated book was the basis for the Bronze and Gold preparing to murder each other over the Solars, after all.<br /> <br /> 3) No, a decision counts if someone didn't dangle a better alternative, but if someone DID present a better alternative, and that alternative was subsequently ignored in favor of mass-murder, it gives &quot;The Thing&quot; an entirely different characterization than if no better alternative had ever been offered.<br /> <br /> 4) Reforming from being Evil and doing Evil, is a much different thing than not having been Evil in the first place. A rogue Deathknight who abandons his/her Deathlord to try and better Creation may well be a Hero. The Solar who has saved towns from bandits, fed the hungry with the reward he received, and gone on to do more heroics without ever having murdered anyone in service to an ancient ghost trying to murder Creation IS more Heroic IMHO. Redemption is wonderful, but inherent to the concept is the implication one must regain moral ground the redeemed previously surrendered. Can the redeemed villain eventually become more heroic than the hero who was never a villain, but eventually reached a point where they decided to rest on their laurels? Of course. Yet if that hero instead continued on steadily being heroic, I would say he retains a &quot;heroic lead&quot; on the redeemed being, by virtue of never having fallen.<br /> <br /> A bit simplistic, perhaps, but that's a theme recurrent in Epic Fantasy.<br /> <br /> 5) You honestly disagree with the fact that if there never was a Gold Faction that advocated against killing the Solars and imprisoning their Exaltations, it doesn't radically change the connotations of keeping the Sidereal hand in the Solar Purge concealed from the returned Solars?<br /> <br /> To say it more clearly: If the Sidereals, these currently-alive Sidereals, who are protecting and training returned Solars...If these Sidereals know ALL Sidereals agreed the Solars all needed to die, but THESE Sidereals changed their minds about the Solars after seeing what Creation was like without them, but they choose to keep the Solars ignorant of that fact...That decision doesn't have an entirely different cast?<br /> <br /> What you said, about it distracting the Solars...that's the kind of argument you get to make and have it hold water when you don't have their predecessors blood on your hands. You keep saying three or four lives ago, but what an Exalt did in one life having far-reaching impacts on subsequent lives has always been a theme of Exalted. It's why Exalts get flashes of their past-lives.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T20:08:17-07:00","id":1236992,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236924#post1236924\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Apologies,<br /> I realize I wasn't at ALL clear for my reasons why my ancient history-centric view of the Sidereal divide is relevant to modern Sidereals. If the Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade are retained, then it follows even the most foolish, oblivious Sidereal would be able to recognize the removal of the Solars and the exiling of the Lunars essentially opened the door for both events. By going with the idea that the Sidereals were united behind the Usurpation, no one in Heaven is essentially responsible for those catastrophes, because they all are, meaning either a) The write-up will be none of the Sidereals accept responsibility for the Contagion/Crusade being their fault for eliminating the Solars, which kills story hooks, or b) The write-up becomes those who perceive their responsibility are only diverging from the Bronze out of guilt and horror for what they helped usher in.</div> </div> </div> </div>Uh, the contagion wasn't their fault except in an out of game meta way and the Bronze factor feel bad that those things blindsided them. They still think what the solars were going to do was worse then it. The surviving inner council of the bronze faction were all there during the usurpation and before it. This wasn't a spur of the moment thing but a deliberate act that took decades to plan and enact. You aren't going to make them &quot;See the light&quot; even without the great curse because the great curse only exacerbates what they already believe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-19T23:37:48-07:00","id":1237038,"title":"","content":"Well,<br /> I've been doing my reading, and it turns out you guys are right. It seems the Devs are going with a &quot;The First Age/Usurpation is unexplained, and it isn't productive to write material that judges past actors, instead of material about present actors who still possess agency to affect Creation.&quot;<br /> <br /> So, it looks like they're just giving the issue a whitewash. Disheartening, but not that unanticipated after &quot;Thaumaturgy is no longer a supernatural science, and mortals no longer access Essence, because we wanted to limit &quot;casual&quot; magical penetration of societies. Now, please ignore all the customized, specific magical penetration we wrote into societies that contradicts this.&quot;<br /> <br /> As for whether the Contagion was the Bronze's fault: I don't consider it an out-of-game meta thing to make a decision to eliminate 6/7ths of the Celestial Exalts, and then somehow expect Creation to be OK defended by 1/7th of the Celestial Exalts and the Dragon Blooded. Anyone with even human foresight could have divined the eventual result. The Dragon Blooded would do fine with the day to day, week to week stuff, but the first really major existential threat to Creation to emerge would overwhelm them. It didn't have to be the Contagion. There was that last enemy Primordial never accounted for, for example..By definition, take ANYTHING that the Solars/Lunars/Sidereals were collectively needed to deal with post-Great War, and a repeat of that would overwhelm Sidereals + Dragon Blooded alone.<br /> <br /> Not that I can't see the design cleverness in obfuscating the details of the First Age for 3E. Without the players having the meta-context concerning antiquity, it leaves the door open much wider to toss in things at a later date. The Devs seem to have a real hard-on in the Q&amp;A I'm reading against story-elements in 2E that &quot;collapsed the waveform.&quot; Examples: Return of the Scarlet Empress, the five listed options for the potential identity of the Emissary, and the &quot;truth&quot; of what makes the Chayans go nuts.<br /> <br /> It's a valid position, and it's theirs to take, but there are a LOT of people like me who enjoy lore being definitive. If I wanted to fill in all the major mysterious elements with my own unique stamp, I'd simply heist the Attributes + Ability or a D20 system, and build my own game.<br /> <br /> You make my point for me though <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"116\" data-vbnamecard=\"116\">Epimetheus</a> , the Bronze believe that, no matter what terrible fate befalls Creation, by definition the Solars would've done worse. That's absolutist thinking, and a wonderful exemplar of the Great Curse at work. Blurring out the details of the First Age serves to relieve the Bronze Faction of the villainous cast to their actions. If its just &quot;Yup, we all agree the Solars were nuts, obviously they have to go&quot; then there's no room for &quot;What if the Bronze was wrong? What if, as happens in so many epic myths when someone tries to Cheat Fate, the actor only served to bring about the Fate they feared? What if all this suffering was unnecessary, and there was another way?&quot; Nope, ALL the Solars were mad with power and hopelessly corrupt. Let's just kill nuance like the fact that some Solars, like the Queen of Whitewall (I can't remember how to spell its First Age name) hadn't social-fu'd her Dragon Blooded at all, and her people loved her so intensely the Bronze Dragon Blooded armies had to butcher all of them to get to her.<br /> <br /> As I said, it can be characterized otherwise, but after-the-fact reconsideration of the Usurpation is a Utility Calculation, not a moral stance. I was always most interested in the Gold Faction because, for all their many, many faults, they'd at least put their money where there mouths were. The original members fought and died, rather than becoming Brutus &amp; Cassius: The Fellowship. They endured nearly two millennia of political marginalization in Heaven for refusing to cave and profess &quot;Yes, of course your slaughter of the Solars, all your fellow Sidereals who would not bend their necks, the many thousands of Dragon Blooded, who chose to fight and die in battle against their brothers and sisters, rather than become back-stabbing slaves, and all those Lunars who wouldn't abandon their mates, Chejop. Oh wait, I forgot all the Gods and Elementals you had smelted into ash-trays and toilet seats on trumped-up charges.&quot;<br /> <br /> Instead, it's &quot;Well, 90% of Creation died, and we lost 40% of Creation, period. Perhaps we shouldn't have been so hasty in assuming we could overthrow the natural order without horrific consequences. WAIT, LOOK! The Solars have returned! Quick guys, if we hurry, we can scurry down there and cultivate them. We can press the reset button! Remember everyone, Ix Nay Usurpation-ay.&quot;<br /> <br /> I get (and I agree, so far as many elements) that it's better to focus on the agency of the PCs than spoon-feed characters-on-rails, but the Usurpation and its context has always served to inform the canon agenda Re: Yu-Shan. Drawing a curtain over the First Age with regards to the Usurpation is simplistic, and creatively reductive. YMMV, of course.<br /> <br /> Then again, I ADORED Lords &amp; Lands of Creation, which I gather are only slightly less despised around here than the Underworld Rapist-Rape Brothel, and Lillun (Relax, I utterly despise both of those things.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-20-2018, 02:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-20T03:26:07-07:00","id":1237067,"title":"","content":"Okay, keep it simple; don't break these long posts down one line at a time and respond to each with a miniature essay.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1236989#post1236989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> 1) A Unified Usurpation DOES remove the moral element. The &quot;Resorting to twisting the Dragon Blooded into murder-weapons, and using our position as the trusted advisors of our Exalted cousins to massacre the Solars, imprison their Exaltations, and hound the Lunars into the Wyld with death-squads is *evil.*&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Like, I can't really argue against this, because if it's a strongly held moral conviction then there isn't really a presentation or articulation that can refute it.<br /> <br /> But it's not a conviction that I hold (with regards to consideration of this fictional setting), and I'm not alone in that. I don't mean that as an expression of &quot;you hold a minority position, so get out&quot;, just as an expression of context. I should hope that I've presented my perspective (which, I should emphasize, is on a subject that is still unwritten and possibly undecided) in a manner sufficient to convey that it can be elaborate and nuanced. But if your perspective on the subject as based on straightforward distinctions between characters being good and evil, I doubt there's much common ground for further discussion.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I meant by saying if all Sidereals are responsible, then none of them are is: There's no external faction saying &quot;See, we told you this would end badly.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>That still makes no sense; the perspective that somebody can only be held responsible for something if they did it in spite of somebody trying to argue them out of it is nonsensical.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea that no meaningful number of the Exalts called Seers of the Loom of Fate grasped that the total removal of 3/7ths of the Celestial Exalts, and the complete marginalization of the remaining 3/7ths had enormous potential to result in the very apocalypse the Bronze sought to forestall is well...stupid, to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, the two prior Editions made it so that the Great Contagion and the Balorian Crusade took the Gold Faction by surprise as well, so it's not as if they were ever supposed to be arguing from a perspective of foreseeing the end of the world. That speaks more to the overall question of how Sidereal foresight should work, which is tricky. Otherwise it becomes a question of whether or not it's reasonable to expect such a destructive event to occur before there are any indications of it.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, you keep talking about the Sidereal Great Curse (I'm presuming you're referring to First Edition, Second made it kind of less distinct); the idea that, when they act in larger numbers, their foresight becomes skewed and their judgement is weakened. I'm not the biggest fan of it, and I'm not sure what it will be like this time around, but you appear to place a great stock in it. I'm not sure how that reconciles with an objection to the idea that they collectively made a big decision while overlooking some major details.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Without the division at the time of the Great Prophecy, there's no meaningful sign of it at work.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're going to need to explain that one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I get the sense (I'll apologize if I've misconstrued your intent) you are a member of the camp that wants the Sidereal to be a Five Score Fellowship first, and Exalts with serious differences second, if ever. Your comment about the Independents being boring, I took as a sign of distaste for the idea of the Bronze/Gold divide being anything more than a philosophical difference. Personally, I feel that Bronze and Gold Sidereals SHOULD want each other dead. Every dead rival Faction-member is another chance to dispose of an Exalt hardened against your cause, and simultaneously a new opportunity to woo the inheritor of their Exaltation to your side while they're new and haven't yet had a chance to become objectively informed. I loved the quote that summed up the extent of cooperation between Bronze and Gold Sidereals regarding field-work. &quot;You don't sabotage a situation to the point it can't be resolved. That will get you audited and severely punished. You sabotage it in such a way that YOUR RIVAL can't successfully resolve the situation.&quot; I loved the idea that as each year of the Solar Return wears on, the tensions between Bronze and Gold keep exponentially increasing, to the point the first premeditated murder of a Gold Sidereal by one of the Bronze recently happened.<br /> <br /> ANYTHING that breaks up the Special Sidereal Solidarity that makes them an unbelievable aberrant among Celestial Exalts.</div> </div> </div> </div>I keep saying that these are tensions that are interesting when they happen in the game, and within the scope and context of the Time of Tumult.<br /> <br /> Do you think that there should have been Sidereals who were undermining one another and their duties like that, say, four hundred years before the return of the Solars?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">3) No, a decision counts if someone didn't dangle a better alternative, but if someone DID present a better alternative, and that alternative was subsequently ignored in favor of mass-murder, it gives &quot;The Thing&quot; an entirely different characterization than if no better alternative had ever been offered.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree, but we clearly draw very different conclusions about what that difference in characterization is.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">4) Reforming from being Evil and doing Evil, is a much different thing than not having been Evil in the first place. A rogue Deathknight who abandons his/her Deathlord to try and better Creation may well be a Hero. The Solar who has saved towns from bandits, fed the hungry with the reward he received, and gone on to do more heroics without ever having murdered anyone in service to an ancient ghost trying to murder Creation IS more Heroic IMHO. Redemption is wonderful, but inherent to the concept is the implication one must regain moral ground the redeemed previously surrendered. Can the redeemed villain eventually become more heroic than the hero who was never a villain, but eventually reached a point where they decided to rest on their laurels? Of course. Yet if that hero instead continued on steadily being heroic, I would say he retains a &quot;heroic lead&quot; on the redeemed being, by virtue of never having fallen.<br /> <br /> A bit simplistic, perhaps, but that's a theme recurrent in Epic Fantasy.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, because I don't characterise the Bronze Faction as villains, I'm not really casting the subject in the light of Redeemed Evil, and I don't really require an alternative with a moral high ground as an entry point to the group.<br /> <br /> If somebody does... I don't think it's unreasonable to say that you know what you want, so just go ahead and play it like that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You honestly disagree with the fact that if there never was a Gold Faction that advocated against killing the Solars and imprisoning their Exaltations, it doesn't radically change the connotations of keeping the Sidereal hand in the Solar Purge concealed from the returned Solars?<br /> <br /> To say it more clearly: If the Sidereals, these currently-alive Sidereals, who are protecting and training returned Solars...If these Sidereals know ALL Sidereals agreed the Solars all needed to die, but THESE Sidereals changed their minds about the Solars after seeing what Creation was like without them, but they choose to keep the Solars ignorant of that fact...That decision doesn't have an entirely different cast?<br /> <br /> What you said, about it distracting the Solars...that's the kind of argument you get to make and have it hold water when you don't have their predecessors blood on your hands.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do think it has a different cast, just not the same one that you do, because many of my premises are different.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">You keep saying three or four lives ago, but what an Exalt did in one life having far-reaching impacts on subsequent lives has always been a theme of Exalted. It's why Exalts get flashes of their past-lives.</div> </div> </div> </div>If I say &quot;past lives mean whatever the hell the given speaker wants them to mean&quot;, that's both an expression that the concept is elastic and versatile enough to fulfil a variety of functions depending on the tastes and needs of the speaker in question, and a sense of weariness for people who declare that <i>their</i> conception of it is authoritative; not only superior, but the intended function as conveyed by books that hardly ever talk about it.<br /> <br /> I will say that there has to be room for Celestial Exalted that are <i>not</i> beholden to the legacy of a previous incarnation, and certainly not responsible for the decisions of somebody who acted centuries before they were born.<br /> <br /> Not that <b>no</b> Sidereals should be like that, just validity for some that are not.<br /> <br /> Oh God, this is still going too long; certainly too much to address a second post within. Maybe I'll respond to that one at length, maybe this is sufficient on its own... but there is <u>one</u> point that I feel is worth ending on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">It's a valid position, and it's theirs to take</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br /> <br /> Just to point out: John Morke and Holden Shearer don't get to take <i>any</i> position on the development of the game anymore.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-20T03:38:08-07:00","id":1237069,"title":"","content":"Can we actually talk about how the gold faction didn't exist until way after the usurpation. There was no gold faction until quite a bit after and ayesha didn't start it until a little after the scarlet empress ruleed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-20T06:06:09-07:00","id":1237108,"title":"","content":"I'm a little skeptical about all things unwritten at this point. I've been devouring whatever I can find on the subject, and I just ran into &quot;The way the Realm handled the few Solars who persistently reincarnated while the Empress was still around was for a Sidereal to pinpoint the region. If that region fell far outside Realm-territory, it was, and I quote *Time to put on the Dragon Armor, bind a few Agatae, and perform an aerial insertion into somewhere they had zero political authority to be. That's how seriously the Realm took the Solars.&quot;<br /> <br /> If the Bronze/Gold divide is entirely a result of post-Usurpation Utility Calculations, we also do away with the basis for Ayesha Uru's split with Chejop Kejak. I always loved the account of how the very sorcerous initiations he pressed her to undertake resulted in first Ayesha no longer ignoring Tammiz Ushun's memories, and then her sacrificing her ties to him and Bronze ideology as her sacrifice for the Sapphire Circle. With the change to Sorcery, that was already pretty well killed, one supposes, but now there's no basis for such memories to exist at all. Which, IMHO, is what you could call &quot;Exhibit A&quot; when I say this format is &quot;Creatively Reductive.&quot;<br /> <br /> It does seem that the setting has effectively absolved the Bronze Faction. At this point, I'm having a difficult time imagining what the difference even IS between the Bronze and the Gold. 1) No Great Prophecy, and by that, I mean no Vision of Bronze/Vision of Gold/Vision of Nightmares means no initial division. It then follows that 2) Chejop Kejak ordered no Sidereal murders, manipulated no Dragon Blooded into killing thousands of their own kind, and had no need to conduct a pogrom in Heaven to eliminate Solar loyalists. 3) Since everyone agreed the Solars must go, Lytek was never brutally assaulted, meaning the Gods of Yu-Shan do not deeply mistrust and even fear the Sidereal Exalted, and 4) There were no legal consequences to Usurping the Solars, meaning there would be no need to avoid the judgment of Heaven, and thus no need to break the Mask in the effort to obfuscate Sidereal culpability.<br /> <br /> Centuries pass: The Great Contagion and the Balorian Crusade ravage Creation. A minority of the Sidereals, mostly very young ones, decide they were wrong to do away with the Solars and Lunars. The Solar return provides them with the opportunity to cultivate those Solars as weapons against the Realm. I'm not sure why they would need such weapons, since the Realm (and all the very, VERY obviously foreseeable negative consequences of a hyper-oppressive Imperial police-state were THEIR idea, either in this life or a previous one), but there you are.<br /> <br /> Since everyone agreed the Solars needed to go, there haven't been Gods pining away for the good old days. Since the Bronze Faction didn't actually do anything Heaven didn't want done, they didn't make a considerable number of enemies among those deities simply too powerful to manufacture bogus charges against. Since those were hands-down the vast majority of the Gold support structure previously, it's difficult to imagine how the Gold could have ANY support in Yu-Shan. The Bronze has all the power, and immortals who love status quo no longer have viable motives like revenge or restoration of something they didn't want gone in the first place. The Gold could still work the bribes angle, but that was always the weakest leg of their support tripod, since as the minority faction with few elders, the resources with which to do the bribing of deities would be very minimal.<br /> <br /> As a post-Shogunate institution, it is difficult (for me) to perceive ANY Sidereals as less negative than Abyssals. Probably still more positive than Infernals, but without initial division, it's all hindsight and Gold juniors trying to supplant Bronze elders for (reasons). No initial division has all Sidereals signing off on the Realm, meaning no Sidereals are free of the Realm's legacy of strip-mining literally every last culture that can't fight them off with the help of geographical distance from the Blessed Isle.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-20-2018, 09:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-20T07:25:16-07:00","id":1237133,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237108#post1237108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If the Bronze/Gold divide is entirely a result of post-Usurpation Utility Calculations, we also do away with the basis for Ayesha Uru's split with Chejop Kejak. I always loved the account of how the very sorcerous initiations he pressed her to undertake resulted in first Ayesha no longer ignoring Tammiz Ushun's memories, and then her sacrificing her ties to him and Bronze ideology as her sacrifice for the Sapphire Circle. With the change to Sorcery, that was already pretty well killed, one supposes, but now there's no basis for such memories to exist at all. Which, IMHO, is what you could call &quot;Exhibit A&quot; when I say this format is &quot;Creatively Reductive.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Like I said, I don't think it's a bad thing for Ayesha's perspective to come primarily from her own assessments and evaluations, rather than the influence of her prior incarnation's convictions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">At this point, I'm having a difficult time imagining what the difference even IS between the Bronze and the Gold.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Bronze faction believes that the excesses of the Solars at the end of the First Age demonstrated them to be an intolerable danger to the fate of the world (as validated by prophecy), and that the kind of power that Solars have inevitably corrupts them, so the world can only be kept safe if Solars are eliminated or curtailed, even at the cost of removing the kinds of wonder enabled by Solar magic.<br /> <br /> The Gold faction believes that the history of the world since the Usurpation demonstrates that the removal of the Solars is not worth the cost, and would sooner stake the future on trying to manage the return of the Solars in a manner that will prevent them from becoming such a major threat again. That may or may not be validated by prophecy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">No Great Prophecy, and by that, I mean no Vision of Bronze/Vision of Gold/Vision of Nightmares means no initial division.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if those specific designations will be used, but the basic outline of the prophecy described in the Third Edition core seems to be the same. It might just consist less of easily divided categories, and possibly lacks the idea that the Sidereals who advocated for the course of trying to fix the Old Realm were willing to go to the bat for it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Chejop Kejak ordered no Sidereal murders, manipulated no Dragon Blooded into killing thousands of their own kind, and had no need to conduct a pogrom in Heaven to eliminate Solar loyalists.</div> </div> </div> </div>No. Even in prior Editions, most of those people knew nothing about any prophecies. There are absolutely still supposed to have been Dragon Blooded who sided with the Solars, and fought to the death on that conviction. There are probably still gods who have seen their stars fallen as a result of the Usurpation, and their political perspective therein.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since everyone agreed the Solars must go, Lytek was never brutally assaulted, meaning the Gods of Yu-Shan do not deeply mistrust and even fear the Sidereal Exalted</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know about Lytek, it may still be that they interfered with him while collecting the Essences, but still yes to this part; the prior developers didn't see value in the idea that the Sidereals were openly despised rather than being respected functionaries of the Celestial Order, and I see no reason why the current developers should not disagree.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">There were no legal consequences to Usurping the Solars, meaning there would be no need to avoid the judgment of Heaven, and thus no need to break the Mask in the effort to obfuscate Sidereal culpability.</div> </div> </div> </div>In First Edition, breaking the Mask was the result of them using a lot of astrology to conceal themselves from the memory and history of people in Creation, so that they could take up the role of distant, shadowy puppetmasters. I'm personally of the opinion that it being all about concealing their actions from the authorities of Yu-Shan, and especially that this results in a scenario where everybody knows what they did but are incapable of proving it, to be tremendously silly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A minority of the Sidereals, mostly very young ones, decide they were wrong to do away with the Solars and Lunars. The Solar return provides them with the opportunity to cultivate those Solars as weapons against the Realm. I'm not sure why they would need such weapons, since the Realm (and all the very, VERY obviously foreseeable negative consequences of a hyper-oppressive Imperial police-state were THEIR idea, either in this life or a previous one), but there you are.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry, are these Sidereals supposed to be pro-Solar or anti-Realm? If the former, it would seem to me that supporting the Solars against the Realm is for the benefit of the Solars, who are otherwise at something of a disadvantage. If the latter, why characterize those Sidereals as thinking that they were wrong to get rid of the Solars?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since everyone agreed the Solars needed to go, there haven't been Gods pining away for the good old days.</div> </div> </div> </div>On what basis do you keep conflating an idea about Sidereals with an idea about the entirety of Yu-Shan (and, if your comment on Dragon Blooded is anything to go by, parts of Creation as well)?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since those were hands-down the vast majority of the Gold support structure previously, it's difficult to imagine how the Gold could have ANY support in Yu-Shan.</div> </div> </div> </div>Vanileth would very much like it if there were flying machines again.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a post-Shogunate institution, it is difficult (for me) to perceive ANY Sidereals as less negative than Abyssals.</div> </div> </div> </div>Setting aside questions about how negative the Abyssals are, this is still weird. People with a genuine and somewhat legitimate motivation to save the world, and having failed in certain respects, are no better than (what I'm assuming you think of as) people actively working towards the world's destruction? Do you regard surgeons who lose patients to be as bad as murderers?<br /> <br /> And the Gold faction are no better, because their convictions don't have enough legacy? <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">without initial division, it's all hindsight</div> </div> </div> </div>What is your problem with hindsight? You seem remarkably hostile to the idea that somebody doesn't get things right on the first go.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Gold juniors trying to supplant Bronze elders for (reasons).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, reasons that you have both acknowledged and repeated in your own posts. It's curious that you would employ the kind of rhetoric that would write them off as vague or insubstantial.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">No initial division has all Sidereals signing off on the Realm, meaning no Sidereals are free of the Realm's legacy of strip-mining literally every last culture that can't fight them off with the help of geographical distance from the Blessed Isle.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not going to make presumptions about what part of the world you're from, but speaking as an Irish person, I don't find it hard to sympathise with the idea of people that are struggling to do good in the face of a legacy of oppressive systems, many of which they have directly benefited from.<br /> <br /> Mind, I'm also a cis, white, heterosexual, English-speaking man of reasonable mental competency and a decent economic background, so I'm not without a <i>few</i> systems to deal with.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-20T18:41:29-07:00","id":1237356,"title":"","content":"My last presentation was sort of a facetious exercise in highlighting the reductive absurdity of a Unified Usurpation. Not really what I believe any sane developer would actually do.<br /> Still, I'll bite. The reason I like a legacy-based informed view of dissension at the time of the Usurpation is (and I admit this to be entirely a subjective matter of taste) I appreciate the cachet of ancient disagreements still dividing Heaven. I enjoy the idea that if the Gold DID in fact go to bat for their beliefs, the linkages between them and the deities against the Usurpation, and now for a return to Solar rule are tied together by more than enlightened self-interest. That in the mire of corruption that Yu-Shan has become since the Incarnae grew indifferent, a portion of the Sidereals, despite enormous temptation to the contrary, never &quot;fell from grace.&quot;<br /> <br /> Stories about redeeming one's tragic mistakes can be profoundly moving, and incredibly powerful, but I prefer the default-setting point not be that the very best the Sidereals have to offer are betraying mass-murderers who now bitterly regret their actions. For me, it's the difference between a story about an Abyssal who folds to a Deathlord's blandishments while on the point of death, but immediately after Exalting goes &quot;What was I thinking? Of course I'm not going to help this lunatic murder the world, and I don't give a shit if that ultimately gets me killed&quot; and (Abyssal murders, pillages and destroys for three and a half years following his Black Exaltation) then has a moment of clarity, which leads to him going rogue.<br /> <br /> Only that's not an exact parallel. The Sidereals are peculiar in that moral authority has always (in my eyes) been a big part of who they are. You had the Bronze, so certain of their position that even the near-obliteration of all life, the shattering of all meaningful ties between Terrestrial and Celestial hierarchies, and losing almost half of Creation itself computes to no more than &quot;The Solars would've done worse.&quot; No moral authority here. You had the Gold, who added a common-sense &quot;Eliminating all the other Celestial Exalts is the equivalent of decapitating the patient to treat his brain tumor&quot; and took a stand, and when they lost, that loss reverberated forward. Costing them temporally, but at least leaving them and their inheritors the perceived moral high ground. Finally, you had the Independents, convinced that both sides were missing the point. That it is not, and <b>never was</b> the role of the Sidereals to decide the direction Creation's future should take. That in attempting to assume that role, the Five Score Fellowship had begun marginalizing their true mandate as those responsible for keeping Fate running smoothly through the Loom. <b>Not</b> defining what that Fate should be. (Which incidentally, is my answer to your previous question Re: What's the point of Nazri and his non-Faction, aside from tut tutting Kejak and Uru. Focusing on the correction of errors such as those caused by the presence of unbound demons, ghosts and Fair Folk in Creation. Not so much moral authority here, so much as remaining true to the non-binary ethos predating the concerns about the Solars.<br /> <br /> Without the cachet of legacy, the Bronze/Gold differences feel after-the-fact to me.<br /> <br /> As for why the Sidereals should be despised rather than respected functionaries: They expressly overturned the natural order on no basis beyond the hubris of &quot;We have decided, on behalf of Heaven and Creation, Solar rule must end, no matter what any of you think about it.&quot; Beyond that, using their position as the Solars trusted advisors to conspire against, betray, and murder those who trusted them by DEFINITION characterizes them a traitors. Why should ANY being who knows what the Sidereals did trust or respect lying, conniving, murderers? If the material about Lytek's assault ends up standing, this would be exacerbated by the aside &quot;..and we're prepared to use extreme force against anyone who gets in our way.&quot; The fact many of these Sidereals are now multiple lives removed from the Usurpation wouldn't be especially relevant to gods, goddesses and elementals who lived through it. Especially when they're seeing these new Sidereals taking orders from the surviving architects of the Usurpation. I always pretty much perceived it as Yu-Shan taking the position of &quot;We know what you did, and the Maidens know just how much we'd like to toss all of you out of Heaven on your ears, but we need you because of the Loom. Don't <b>ever</b> mistake our tolerating you as a necessary evil for anything more than that.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's the whole &quot;Never trust a turn-coat, because a man or willing to turn their coat once, will again&quot; and, unlike Creation, Yu-Shan's memory remains contiguous. This becomes the basis for how the Gold, despite a lack of Elders and the resources those Elders command, can meaningfully oppose the Bronze. If the divine/elemental inhabitants of Yu-Shan characterize the Golds as a) Not being traitors, b) Not having stolen agency over all Creation, and c) Not being responsible for the evils of the post-Usurpation era (the Realm being Exhibit A) then the hatred and distaste for the Bronze Sidereals is a powerful explanation for why, despite their temporal advantages, so much can be going on under their noses. That they only have what control they can exert by force and intimidation.<br /> <br /> I've never found the Hard Man Making Hard Choices angle for Chejop Kejak anything more than a flashing neon sign &quot;Clearly Antagonist Material&quot; with an arrow pointing down at him. Stuff like the Sidereal Elders being the ones eager to grasp at Malfeas-borne MA-styles to increase their personal power I loved. For me, it all boils down to &quot;The Solars never killed 90% of the world, or dissolved nearly half of it into formless chaos. Yet this guy (and this is true in every Kejak fluff-piece in every book) NEVER ONCE, not even for a moment, reconsidered the Usurpation. Like Obi-Wan said &quot;Only Sith deal in absolutes.&quot; (Which of course makes me a Sith <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ) Stuff like &quot;Better a horrible ending, than horrors without end&quot; in response to the horrors of the post-Usurpation world...Deliberately engineering the Immaculate Philosophy (which is even MORE evil now in EX3, because it's expressly <b>NOT</b> a true faith in any way, regarding the Elemental Dragons), and laying the groundwork for a rapacious, hyper-Imperialist police-state. With a state-religion which expressly informs the Dragon Blooded to embody the very worst of Solar character flaws, Ie: &quot;Go ahead and indulge in your rape-tourism, young Dragon Blood, you are a Prince of the Earth, and all these pitiful mortals exist to cater to the every need of your divine perfection.&quot; (That's actual a paraphrasing of the section on Dragon Bloods visiting An-Teng.)<br /> <br /> I don't know how anyone reads this stuff, and doesn't go &quot;Hmm, invented the most Objectively Evil religion in the world as a means of social control. Considers the Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade nothing but collateral damage. Helps his Evil Empire stamp out all threats to its hegemony. Of COURSE Chejop Kejak is a Setting Antagonist.&quot;<br /> <br /> For me, Golds who were once Bronze, had their &quot;moment of clarity&quot; or rejected Bronze methodology they supported in their last life are the appropriate medium for stories involving heroic Sidereals trying to make up for what they now consider tragic past mistakes. Making the default assumption that there was no meaningful number of Sidereals who opposed the Usurpation means that any heroic Sidereal HAS to be a Redeemed Villain. There's no room there for Sidereals who never fell. I don't object, and indeed enjoy, redemption stories. I just don't want the setting to tell me I have no choice if I want a Circle of Sidereals not in need of redemption.<br /> <br /> Edit: As an aside, I think a lot of people at least subconsciously are put off by just how dark the implications of Bronze behavior are. 99.75% of Exalted fan-fiction I've ever read that deals with the actual Usurpation itself always seems to include Bronze Sidereals speaking ever-so-regretfully to the Solar they're helping to slay about the necessity of their death. We get crocodile tears the reader is supposed to take as genuine from Sidereal lovers of Solars. The urge to lessen the repulsive aspects of doing something like luring a lover/spouse of 800 years to a romantic getaway, and coordinating with the Dragon Blooded assassins lying in wait for the now sexually-exhausted Solar, by having said Sidereal bitterly weeping, then drawing themselves up and reaffirming their resolve before following through is kind of telling, at least to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-20-2018, 09:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T00:37:56-07:00","id":1237429,"title":"","content":"Firstly, I'm not sure that 3e canon actually is saying there were no Sidereals who continued supporting the Solars in the Usurpation; I think the fact they're not mentioned in the Core book may just be word-count. I doubt the devs are keen on removing all nuance and having everyone act monolithically.<br /> <br /> Saying that...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237356#post1237356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I enjoy the idea that if the Gold DID in fact go to bat for their beliefs, the linkages between them and the deities against the Usurpation, and now for a return to Solar rule are tied together by more than enlightened self-interest. That in the mire of corruption that Yu-Shan has become since the Incarnae grew indifferent, a portion of the Sidereals, despite enormous temptation to the contrary, never &quot;fell from grace.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see why saying that the Gold Faction is fairly new means all Sidereals ever are responsible for killing the Solars. The majority weren't even alive then.<br /> (I mean, yes, the Gods might consider them to have been alive. But they weren't. So I don't get why you're so bothered by it. Do you consider people responsible for the sins of their past lives?*)<br /> <br /> *Not that Exalts actually were exalted in their past life. It was some other person with the same exaltation. But their own soul was almost certainly a completely different person.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> but I prefer the default-setting point not be that the very best the Sidereals have to offer are betraying mass-murderers who now bitterly regret their actions. </div> </div> </div>Oh, I'm pretty sure the leaders of the Bronze Faction don't really regret their actions. But I don't think they're presented as &quot;the best the Sidereals have to offer.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for why the Sidereals should be despised rather than respected functionaries: They expressly overturned the natural order on no basis beyond the hubris of &quot;We have decided, on behalf of Heaven and Creation, Solar rule must end, no matter what any of you think about it.&quot; Beyond that, using their position as the Solars trusted advisors to conspire against, betray, and murder those who trusted them by DEFINITION characterizes them a traitors. Why should ANY being who knows what the Sidereals did trust or respect lying, conniving, murderers? </div> </div> </div>What, like the Gods? Who betrayed their own masters/creators? Even the Unconquered Sun is a traitor.<br /> And all the gods feel that's quite justified because the Primordials were awful.<br /> So I don't see an issue with the fact that the gods who liked the Solars think the Sidereals are awful traitorous murderers, and the gods who didn't like the Solars think the Sidereals were overthrowing monstrous tyrants, just like they're supposed to do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've never found the Hard Man Making Hard Choices angle for Chejop Kejak anything more than a flashing neon sign &quot;Clearly Antagonist Material&quot; with an arrow pointing down at him. </div> </div> </div>Yes? Obviously he's there to be an antagonist. (My PCs turned his ghost into a wrackstaff.) Unless you're running a game of Sidereals hunting down Solars. What's your point?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Deliberately engineering the Immaculate Philosophy (which is even MORE evil now in EX3, because it's expressly <b>NOT</b> a true faith in any way, regarding the Elemental Dragons) </div> </div> </div>It was never a true faith. It had no real connection to the actual Great Elemental Dragons beyond the names. In 1st edition the Sidereals used to write books with revelations in, make them look old, and then let DBs find them so as to constantly manipulate the religion they way they wanted to. I doubt that's going to return.<br /> <br /> Actually the devs have been at pains to present how, while the origins might have been constructed by the Sidereals, the monks who evolve and spread the religion really do believe it, and that in the Realm it does generally encourage people to live more moral lives. It has its problems, no doubt, justifying the caste system and spreading intolerance of Solars+Lunars, but when a monk tells some peasants to lead morally upstanding lives, be honest, don't steal, help each other, etc, that's not evil.<br /> <br /> As far as I'm concerned, most religions in the real world are not true, but I don't think this necessarily makes them evil. I mean, I don't believe in Nirvana or Samsara or Buddhas or even agree that desire is the root of suffering, but just because I don't agree with his opinions on everything doesn't mean I think Gautama Buddha was an evil guy with an evil effect on the world, I'd say he was probably a net positive. Lying to people is evil, sure, but when the Bronze faction tells Immaculate Monks that Solars are extremely dangerous and need to be killed... they're not lying, they're telling the truth, as they see it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and laying the groundwork for a rapacious, hyper-Imperialist police-state. With a state-religion which expressly informs the Dragon Blooded to embody the very worst of Solar character flaws, Ie: &quot;Go ahead and indulge in your rape-tourism, young Dragon Blood, you are a Prince of the Earth, and all these pitiful mortals exist to cater to the every need of your divine perfection.&quot; </div> </div> </div>But not from 3rd ed.<br /> Also, the Immaculate Order actually told Dragonblood not to commit rape. They weren't very good at enforcing that, and (like many religions and philosophies) their own contradictions made it difficult to get through to DBs, but they did tell them that.<br /> <br /> I also, again, don't see what it has to do with Tammiz Ushan.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't know how anyone reads this stuff, and doesn't go &quot;Hmm, invented the most Objectively Evil religion in the world as a means of social control. Considers the Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade nothing but collateral damage. Helps his Evil Empire stamp out all threats to its hegemony. Of COURSE Chejop Kejak is a Setting Antagonist.&quot; </div> </div> </div>I don't really see what that has to do with Tammiz Ushan.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For me, Golds who were once Bronze, had their &quot;moment of clarity&quot; or rejected Bronze methodology they supported in their last life are the appropriate medium for stories involving heroic Sidereals trying to make up for what they now consider tragic past mistakes. Making the default assumption that there was no meaningful number of Sidereals who opposed the Usurpation means that any heroic Sidereal HAS to be a Redeemed Villain. There's no room there for Sidereals who never fell. I don't object, and indeed enjoy, redemption stories. I just don't want the setting to tell me I have no choice if I want a Circle of Sidereals not in need of redemption. </div> </div> </div>I don't see how a Sidereal from a tribe oppressed by the Realm who exalted last year rescuing a poor innocent Solar from the Wyld Hunt, and now comes to Heaven and goes looking for other Sidereals who want the Realm to fall and be replaced by a more equitable geo-political situation, is a &quot;reformed villain.&quot;<br /> <br /> Sure, there may be gods who consider them to be so because of what some guy with the same exaltation* did like 1500 years ago, but OOC, we surely know they're not.<br /> <br /> *Not even the same soul. Just the same exaltation.<br /> <br /> I'd also like to point out that in 1st ed and 2nd ed, Ayesha Ura was a former Bronze Faction member.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 07-21-2018, 03:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T01:18:09-07:00","id":1237434,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237356#post1237356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know how anyone reads this stuff, and doesn't go &quot;Hmm, invented the most Objectively Evil religion in the world as a means of social control. Considers the Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade nothing but collateral damage. Helps his Evil Empire stamp out all threats to its hegemony. Of COURSE Chejop Kejak is a Setting Antagonist.&quot;<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well the first part to refute would probably be the religion the Silver Prince started which exists to manipulate a society into leaving materials behind that he can use to turn their ghosts into soul steel with which the Silver Prince would then build a navy and army and use it to wage war upon the rest of the world. Just saying that strikes me as a bit more evil than falsifying things to make it so more people are willing to accept the individuals the Sids picked as the primary defenders of the world as the ones who should be in charge in order to limit conflict while also trying to imprint proper moral standards and behaviors.* This is also discounting that one Deathlord who's big project was trying to make a philisophical argument that everyone should just hurl themselves into Oblivion. Partially as I'm not entirely certain that counts as a religion and because apparently capital 'O' Oblivion isn't a thing anymore.<br /> <br /> As for Chejop being anything other than a setting antagonist. Yeah, its easy. He's an old man who's had to do a lot of things in his day. Some were easier than others, some had easier consequences, there were mistakes and there were achievements. The Usurpation was a big thing with far reaching consequences, it would be impossible not to look at every near apocalyptic threat and wonder how that would have changed had that incident not been deemed necessary. The bronze faction aren't cackling villains who want the Solars removed because they feel the Solars are a threat to their rule. The Bronze is based on the idea that as powerful as the Solars are and can be it is so easy for them to turn that back on the world and vorpral weapons are great right up until someone is using yours on you. <br /> <br /> The Gold aren't remorseful villains, they're people who think that the greatest tool their world ever had has returned to the field. Some might think the Usurpation was a mistake, others might think it was the right move, and still others aren't going to give a crap about it because what's done is done. A lot are going to be operating from the idea that maybe the Usurpation was necessary at the time because the situation was different. These new Solars aren't possessed of the same resources, backings, and power that the Solars of the First Age possessed; so the Gold figure they can use them. <br /> <br /> *Its not going perfectly, but that's more the flaw of the individuals being told and given backing as physically, mentally, and spiritually superior to others as opposed to an inherent intention of the system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T01:28:09-07:00","id":1237435,"title":"","content":"I think the issue is that things that are not black and white are being treated as black and white. These are subjective villains or heroes depending on the campaign.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T04:43:28-07:00","id":1237453,"title":"","content":"Somewhat belatedly but on the subject of the prophecy I do think it's important to keep a few things in mind:<br /> 1) it should be considered accurate. For one thing, it is; the vision of Bronze promised Creation surviving lessened but alive. &quot;But the contagion and crusade came inches from killing everything and only random chances like the mutation making it stop being virulent and the Scarlet Empress fending off the horse saved it!<br /> You're right. Random lucky chances. Identical to those employed by fate to bring about destined events. Thinking.....<br /> 2) it should be assumed that notes were checked. Every single vision of Bronze turned up bronze. Some percentage of the Gold visions turned up dark. How many? My usual answer is a thought experiment: <br /> Flip a coin. Heads utopia forever, tails thousand years of suffering before everything dies.<br /> Like those odds? Add a flip. Two heads in a row means utopia, either tails means darkness.<br /> Keep adding flips until it would be unconscionable to you, to bet Creation on that. <br /> Add another flip and that's the odds the Vision of Gold succeeds, assuming maximum possible Fate fuckery from the Sidereals. <br /> <br /> Basically enough to make it the sort of plan only PCs could condone and the kind that the others would no be horrible monsters for Night of Fallen Starsing their comrades and friends, to keep them from playing Russian Roulette with the entire world and ten spins on a revolver loaded with five bullets.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T04:49:47-07:00","id":1237454,"title":"","content":"Because from a narrative framing, it's the choice that can only be made any other way if the story you're telling is specifically about that choice. Trying to say the bronze were &quot;objectively wrong&quot; in that pivotal decision is just saying the setting as it is shouldn't exist. You'd be either playing in a different setting of utopia forever (which kinda renders Solar adventures meaningless, playing a setting where everyone and everything is dead or doomed, or.... Playing in a setting where a different bronze and gold faction made the choice because the utopia of the second age came and went and now the Solars are in cursed decline again.<br /> <br /> To not be gullible morons the Gold faction needs to be aware they were advocating the biggest gamble in the history of history, with a ridiculously slim margin of success AFTER they stacked every possible deck and tipped every possible scale in their favor.<br /> <br /> And they were advocating this against &quot;as long as we work hard, Fate itself will do everything possible to ensure absolute success, even if it costs us the weight of the world and every sacrifice made for safety.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":756,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","user":"Meianno Yuurei","date":". 07-21-2018, 07:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T07:40:43-07:00","id":1237499,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>i</i><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><i><span style=\"font-size:13px\">nvented the most Objectively Evil religion</span></i></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div> <div align=\"left\"><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I find that we have no common ground, if this is your assessment of the Immaculate Philosophy.</span></span></span></div> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T10:39:55-07:00","id":1237535,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237499#post1237499\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find that we have no common ground, if this is your assessment of the Immaculate Philosophy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, even if we're entirely discounting skeevy real-world faiths, there's way worse ones in the setting than the Immaculate Philosophy, and even <i>they're</i> not &quot;objectively evil.&quot;<br /> <br /> \u200b(<i>Man</i>, am I tired of that phrase.)<br /> <br /> \u200bSo, like, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"24378\" data-vbnamecard=\"24378\">Wyldwraith</a>, you keep coming back to how the Immaculate Philosophy was manufactured by the Sidereals; you've put rather a lot of emphasis on it as I've read the thread. But history as we know it is full of narratives created by self-serving people looking to keep society stable. Consider, if you will, the story of the American Revolution as it's fed to high school students in the US.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 07-21-2018, 01:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T12:13:54-07:00","id":1237551,"title":"","content":"WFHW preview had some nice bits on the Immaculate Philosophy, in particular mentioning that yeah, Monks take it seriously, and if some asshole Dynast is being a terrible Enlightened Being and causing misery and suffering beyond what they deem acceptable, an Enlightened Monk may well end up stepping up to kick his face in; they can and HAVE led peasant rebellions, Ikko-Ikki style, to oust a DB with too many excesses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T16:23:35-07:00","id":1237612,"title":"","content":"OK,<br /> When I said &quot;Worst, most Objectively Evil religion&quot; it was a Damages-based calculation, not a dogma/means calculation. By dogma/means, the Silver Prince's New Order telling everyone living that all their loved ones have reincarnated, when you're actually soul-forging them wins hands down, but the Skullstone Archipelago is one relatively small portion of an entire Direction. Contrast that with the Realm-backed Immaculates hunting down Ahlat's chosen ruler, Blood on the Horns, and shattering the tribes of Harborhead's culture, + the devastation of numerous invasions of the East + the subjagation/re-design of An-Teng +++ The greater hideousness of the New Order, outweighed by Less Intense Hideousness, in varying portions of all four Directions, in concentrated form on the Blessed Isle.<br /> <br /> Before we get too into the Immaculate monk championing the peasants, let us remember that &quot;Peasant eaten by a sorcerer's demon?&quot; Immaculate monk kills demon, then finds an (undoubtedly illegal) mortal demonologist (now that mortals can bind demons), or simply frames a mortal for it, rather than pin it on Irresponsible Dynast Sorcerer. I don't see the functionality of the Immaculate Philosophy changing a great deal, when they've retained the total primacy of the Dynastic Houses in Realm culture.<br /> <br /> I find it less relevant that the Immaculate Philosophy has its good points, and more that Chejop Kejak made the valuation that &quot;Acceptable levels of social control&quot; involved Exaltation-based species-ism.<br /> <br /> As for the &quot;playing Russian Roulette ten times with a five-chambered revolver&quot; characterization of the Prophecy of Gold, that's not a fair parallel. It's never even implied that the failure-percentage of the Vision of Gold was quantified. Let alone that it was this insane long-shot. The Vision of Bronze adherents made their stand on &quot;Any possibility of failure AT ALL is too high.&quot; With Chejop Kejak's famous speech to that effect. Neither do I accept the linkage that Sidereal efforts to bring about one Vision or the other could've been factored into the Prophecy, since Essence use makes the decree of destiny uncertain.<br /> <br /> In the same way as I've mentioned the same thing several times, the fact that present/modern Golds are not the individuals who were alive at the time of the Usurpation has been mentioned several times. It misses the point that a lack of Sidereals initially opposed to the Usurpation has impact on them by virtue of the legacy of their movement. Joining a movement begun by Sidereals against the Usurpation has a different connotation than joining a movement begun by those who regret the Usurpation. I'm fine with the Gold being a mix of those who were for it, those against it, and those who didn't even want to be involved one way or another. Just like I'm fine with those going the other way INTO the Bronze.<br /> <br /> I simply don't want the default setting-point to be &quot;The Gold grew out of retrospective of Usurpation consequences.&quot; If the Bronze Faction Ideology had total initial primacy, the Gold Faction is only a reactionary group. It's the legacy of the groups I'm concentrating on. Not the perspectives of each individual Sidereal, because the legacy of the initial division either limits or expands the moral connotations attaching to both groups. I LOVE the back-and-forth of &quot;Sidereal X was Bronze last life, Gold this one&quot; and vice versa. I HATE the idea of there being no room for a Gold Faction grown out of more than reconsideration of the initial decision to remove the Solars. You get the least-reductive result from initial dissension.<br /> <br /> As for judging Exalts based on their past-lives, I was under the impression that sort of practice was unique, specific and special about Sidereals. That things like &quot;This new Sidereal, who I believe has been misled and lead astray (because they just joined the Gold Faction) was my Sifu and a loyal member of the Bronze in their last life. What a tragedy this is! (said the Bronze Sidereal) was the sort of story element you found among Sidereals in particular.<br /> <br /> Edit: I believe I also mentioned earlier that I adore Ayesha Uru, and the whole &quot;Began Bronze, and the sorcerous initiations Chejop Kejak pushed her towards were the very thing that drove her to return/resurrect the Gold.&quot; I have NO problems with the Gold having &quot;died out&quot; for a time. The faction-swapping-through-incarnations is a lovely element of Sidereals. I simply don't see what the possible benefit of focusing the definition of the Gold on the point of Ayesha Uru bringing it back benefits anything but rationales why the old &quot;Bronze 95%, Gold 5%&quot; should exist.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-21-2018, 07:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T17:00:55-07:00","id":1237618,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237612#post1237612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK,<br /> When I said &quot;Worst, most Objectively Evil religion&quot; it was a Damages-based calculation, not a dogma/means calculation. By dogma/means, the Silver Prince's New Order telling everyone living that all their loved ones have reincarnated, when you're actually soul-forging them wins hands down, but the Skullstone Archipelago is one relatively small portion of an entire Direction. Contrast that with the Realm-backed Immaculates hunting down Ahlat's chosen ruler, Blood on the Horns, and shattering the tribes of Harborhead's culture, + the devastation of numerous invasions of the East + the subjagation/re-design of An-Teng +++ The greater hideousness of the New Order, outweighed by Less Intense Hideousness, in varying portions of all four Directions, in concentrated form on the Blessed Isle.</div> </div> </div> </div>You are aware you just tried to clarify &quot;Worst, most objectively evil religion&quot; by saying you take no aspect of the actual religion into account outside of people in it and are in fact discounting worse and more evil religion because they're smaller despite the original statement's qualification being in the world. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237612#post1237612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK,<br /> Before we get too into the Immaculate monk championing the peasants, let us remember that &quot;Peasant eaten by a sorcerer's demon?&quot; Immaculate monk kills demon, then finds an (undoubtedly illegal) mortal demonologist (now that mortals can bind demons), or simply frames a mortal for it, rather than pin it on Irresponsible Dynast Sorcerer. I don't see the functionality of the Immaculate Philosophy changing a great deal, when they've retained the total primacy of the Dynastic Houses in Realm culture.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except for the fact that those aren't Immaculate Principles, those are corrupt individuals in the system.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237612#post1237612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK,<br /> As for the &quot;playing Russian Roulette ten times with a five-chambered revolver&quot; characterization of the Prophecy of Gold, that's not a fair parallel. It's never even implied that the failure-percentage of the Vision of Gold was quantified. Let alone that it was this insane long-shot. The Vision of Bronze adherents made their stand on &quot;Any possibility of failure AT ALL is too high.&quot; With Chejop Kejak's famous speech to that effect. Neither do I accept the linkage that Sidereal efforts to bring about one Vision or the other could've been factored into the Prophecy, since Essence use makes the decree of destiny uncertain.</div> </div> </div> </div>Would you like the page number where it says that there was only a razor's edge chance that the Vision of Gold would go well and that it was so unbloody stable that it took a while for the Sids to get that these were dealing with the same prophecy?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237612#post1237612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK, <br /> In the same way as I've mentioned the same thing several times, the fact that present/modern Golds are not the individuals who were alive at the time of the Usurpation has been mentioned several times. It misses the point that a lack of Sidereals initially opposed to the Usurpation has impact on them by virtue of the legacy of their movement. Joining a movement begun by Sidereals against the Usurpation has a different connotation than joining a movement begun by those who regret the Usurpation. I'm fine with the Gold being a mix of those who were for it, those against it, and those who didn't even want to be involved one way or another. Just like I'm fine with those going the other way INTO the Bronze.<br /> <br /> I simply don't want the default setting-point to be &quot;The Gold grew out of retrospective of Usurpation consequences.&quot; If the Bronze Faction Ideology had total initial primacy, the Gold Faction is only a reactionary group. It's the legacy of the groups I'm concentrating on. Not the perspectives of each individual Sidereal, because the legacy of the initial division either limits or expands the moral connotations attaching to both groups. I LOVE the back-and-forth of &quot;Sidereal X was Bronze last life, Gold this one&quot; and vice versa. I HATE the idea of there being no room for a Gold Faction grown out of more than reconsideration of the initial decision to remove the Solars. You get the least-reductive result from initial dissension.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here's the thing you aren't phrasing it that way. You've spent most of this bloody thing arguing that the Bronze were objectively evil, that they are horrible and bad people and that the current Gold Faction needs to be people trying to make amends for the mistakes they made in allowing the Usurpation to be a thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T17:15:39-07:00","id":1237621,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><i><span style=\"font-size:13px\">let us remember that &quot;Peasant eaten by a sorcerer's demon?&quot; Immaculate monk kills demon, then finds an (undoubtedly illegal) mortal demonologist (now that mortals can bind demons), or simply frames a mortal for it, rather than pin it on Irresponsible Dynast Sorcerer.</span></i></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br /> <br /> I don't remember that at all. I think you might have made it up.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T17:27:38-07:00","id":1237624,"title":"","content":"Bronze Faction was definitely absolutely 100 percent in the right. I have met enough Solar PCs to know that &quot;kill 'em before they get XP, and try to find a way to stop them exalting in the first place&quot; is not only a reasonable response to Solars, but admirably forward thinking.<br /> <br /> I have seen Solar PCs blow up the moon, enslave cities with a song, free Yozis, snort ground up Neverborn, commit accidental genocide, commit intentional genocide, blow up Gem, blow up the Lap, blow up the Pole of Earth, possess innocent unsuspecting demon princes, cause civil wars in fitsof pique over not being treated with fear and reverence despite nobody knowing they were Exalted, literally throw Juggernaut on top of populated areas, kill Mela, carve propoganda into mountains or the moon with giant laser beams, grant unstoppable First Age Murder-Automata free will and emotions and then just... leave, juggle glass bottles containing samples of the Great Contagion, create whole new universes filled with people SOLELY TO MAKE THEIR MAGNUM OPUS NOVEL INTO REALITY, bind their friends into an eternity in Soulsteel rather than deal with grief or loss in a healthy manner, suck cities into Hell, light oceans on fire, release armies of unbound demons into the countryside because of an abrupt epiphany that slavery is bad, punch Time until it breaks, and build literally unkillable sorcerous poison-spewing monsters for funsies.<br /> <br /> After meeting as many Solars as I have, I believe most Gold Faction Sidereals are youngsters who admirably believe in compassion and not murdering people because of what color they glow. But I think most of 'em probably reconsider after actually meeting a Solar Circle.<br /> <br /> They don't have to be evil to break literally everything. They're like Kodiak bears in the middle of a crowded kindergarten.<br /> <br /> Edit: AND THAT ALL GOES TRIPLE FOR TWILIGHTS!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T17:58:48-07:00","id":1237632,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237621#post1237621\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't remember that at all. I think you might have made it up.[/SIZE][/FONT][/COLOR][/LEFT]</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically Wraith here is conflating the Scarlet Dynasty as a whole with the Immaculate Order, so its basically being presented as any incident where a Dynast gets away with something because of their station as a DB or Dynast its being listed as an inherent flaw and proof of how evil the Immaculate Order is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T18:02:14-07:00","id":1237633,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> It was actually a riff on the old &quot;Demon shekel&quot; concept, updated with EX3's sorcery-change to allow mortal sorcerers to summon and bind demons. Tell me that this retcon making available the knowledge a mortal could've summoned the rampaging demon isn't basically just an out for the Immaculates to not get into the business of a Heptagram graduate whose a member of a powerful House?<br /> <br /> For the record, when this thread started, I was interested in the (IMHO) apparent schizophrenia in the published material regarding the Bronze/Gold divide. It quickly morphed into another &quot;This is why 3E, and by extension 1E, interpretation is better&quot; thread. It's since spiraled well out of control. I get it, truly: People hate 2E, and are incredibly excited for EX3, because a) It's apparently going to hew more closely to 1E, and b) It yet retains all the unlimited promise of the unwritten. I happen to love the Hell out of much of 2E. There's stupid stuff, but 1E had stupid stuff too (Like the Lunars book).<br /> <br /> Neither am I unaware I'm essentially a few years too late to find a great deal of interest here in 2E. It kind of mystifies me, but I don't sense it's a productive point to pursue. You said it yourself, there's no meeting of the minds to be had. Truthfully, I'm beginning to resign myself to the necessity of gutting EX3 for better mechanics, and preceding apace with my own gaming. Out of all five threads I've begun, four turned into &quot;This is why EX3 will, or is, better&quot; in less than a page. The Lunar Eugenics escaped by dint of being 2E-flagged, and expressly about a 2E mechanic.<br /> <br /> So yes, I'm likely overly defensive and just...disheartened, and its making me grouchy. My apologies to one and all <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T19:00:35-07:00","id":1237644,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237633#post1237633\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> It was actually a riff on the old &quot;Demon shekel&quot; concept, updated with EX3's sorcery-change to allow mortal sorcerers to summon and bind demons. Tell me that this retcon making available the knowledge a mortal could've summoned the rampaging demon isn't basically just an out for the Immaculates to not get into the business of a Heptagram graduate whose a member of a powerful House?</div> </div> </div> </div>Uhm, are you not aware that in Ex2 mortal <i>thaumaturges</i> could summon demons and make deals without even needing to bind them?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/487-greyman","name":"Greyman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T19:50:46-07:00","id":1237668,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"24378\" data-vbnamecard=\"24378\">Wyldwraith</a> I would not say that everyone or even a majority of the Forum hates 2E wholesale. Hell, the VAST majority of my play experience was 2E and 2.5E, including a 2.5E shard I ran for my playgroup which spanned over two and a half years. What people have issue with comes in several varieties from my past several years worth of Lurking on this board. 1) 2E unnecessarily adding squick factor when not needed. I return to the humble Mercury Ant as just one example of many where specificity and an overarching need to categorize motivation took a fun and interesting thing and made it both sexual and confrontational when it was not needed. This was not a singular event, nor just contained within one book, and while it was also in several books (as you pointed to the Lunar 1E book was...very problematic as well) within 1E, it was not nearly as ubiquitous.<br /> <br /> 2) Over-specificity causing ONE TRUE CANON arguments. This one I think is the other compelling point. You mentioned in another thread a love of specificity/quantification and I can totally understand how awesome it can be to be down and delve deep into the lore. However where it becomes a problem is when it cages the types of stories and narratives that can be told within the framework of the Setting. By leaving the First age to each individual storyteller with some broad guidance, they can frame the story in a manner that best suites the chronicle they wish to tell. This is especially true in online play where players may not have the personal connections that are necessary when a ST moves away from established canon, and can lead to extreme arguments.<br /> <br /> 3) Related to point 1) but also dealing with 1E, the world and gaming environments have changed. Some would see this for the worse but I would contend that this has been a net positive, the kinds of things written just to be either edgy or to make a work feel ''mature&quot; no longer are written the same way. The introduction of the Red Rule and the alteration of the several items within the canon of 3E such as how the Solar/Lunar bond works is nothing but a net positive for this game, and gives players an environment I feel promotes better play and development than the previous incarnations.<br /> <br /> Finally, please continue to love Exalted in the manner you wish! If you wish to keep true to the lore of Second edition, and use that as the framework for where you tell your stories, no one here can tell you not to do that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11967-saipjas","name":"Saipjas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"270"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T22:34:17-07:00","id":1237692,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manual of Exalted Power: The Sidereals</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It took many weeks for the seemingly disparate prophecies that form the Vision of Gold to be acknowledged as a single foretelling. The outcomes were so different and strange at every turn that they might as well have been entirely different fortunes, save for one aspect: all were brought about by the Sidereal Exalted revealing their concerns of madness to the Solar Exalted.<br /> In some visions, the Sidereals revealed their concerns to a few Lawgivers who shared the Sidereals\u2019 concerns. They worked together to create a system by which the Solars might themselves be appropriately policed. Working hard to quell any concerns and to keep their system above corruption, the Solars could address the fears of the Seers, and lead Creation to even greater heights of glory, peace and justice than before.<br /> Other visions gave darker outcomes. In those visions, word of Sidereal concerns reached the Solar Exalted. Some Solars agreed, and cast a suspicious eye on their peers, never once considering that they themselves might be mad. Paranoia and accusations of corruption and madness led to civil war. Sidereals and Lunars were forced to either flee the pogroms or be swept up into them. Solar battled Solar until Creation became a wasteland and the Wyld consumed the ruins.<br /> The Sidereals who chose this vision believed they could manage the revelation and help the Solars regain their sanity. After all, the Sun\u2019s Chosen had succeeded at so much. If they could be persuaded to reform themselves, how could they fail?<br /> Many of the Sidereals were not so sure; indeed, even those who did not wish to see Creation lessened still sided with the Vision of Bronze because it was sure. The Vision of Gold fanned into many possibilities, never settling into a single, stable truth. The future foretold by the Vision of Bronze might take a thousand years or more to fulfi ll, but in the end, a new Realm would rule all of Creation, lumpen, a bit brutal, but unchanging, forever.</div> </div> </div> </div>My &quot;Ten pulls of a revolver with 5 chambers loaded&quot; is harkening back to the thought experiment I posited. How many flips do you consider it 'okay', to gamble all of creation, the suffering and misery of an entire world being slowly strangled and exsanguinated and burnt and tortured until finally everything lies dead? One? Two? Five? When is it &quot;okay&quot; to bet the house and risk losing it all, losing everything everyone ELSE owns, to you? Some people might not want to risk everything, absolutely everything, on that single d20 roll coming up 1-10 instead of 11-20 (if we're being nice and assuming it's 50/50) just because &quot;You're telling me THERE'S A CHANCE&quot;.<br /> <br /> My argument is opposition to the fact that there's a lot of Bronze Condemner players who say &quot;Oh, the odds aren't stated so OBVIOUSLY they were like, 99,999,999/100,000,000 that the Vision of Gold goes off without a hitch and only one in a hundred million that something goes wrong and Vision of Darkness happens&quot;, arguing that it should be assumed that it felt like a sufficient gamble, gambling away everything <i>for everyone else in Creation</i>, to not be worth it for most of the Bronze Vision supporters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":756,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","user":"Meianno Yuurei","date":". 07-22-2018, 01:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-21T22:53:24-07:00","id":1237695,"title":"","content":"Honestly I didn't see this as having much to do with edition at all. This seemed more to come from the old thing of there being this argument that the Usurpation and the Sids had to have been the bad guys, not because it was a betrayal, not even because of the stuff lost with the end of the first age, but this argument that &quot;Well the Gold Prophecy meant there was a chance for everything to be better and they went against it.&quot; Which takes away so much to try and play that as black and white. Not to mention I've never liked people arguing that The Great Contagion and The Balorian Crusade are proof that the Bronze faction was wrong, that its so often treated as a thing they should have seen coming just because they played up to events that happen outside of Fate and a Sid's ability to predict the future.<br /> <br /> It goes back into an argument SF Debris had against one of the factions in the Foundation series, basically the main character decided to side with this outside group positing that if aliens exist they could be dangerous and if they showed up and were hostile then none of the other factions would be prepared to deal with these aliens. Except there weren't any hints of aliens existing in the setting, so this guy is treated as right because he's supporting his premise on a possibility that has nothing backing the idea that it would happen. The only advantage the argument has with relation to the Sids is they at least knew Rhaksha and the Underworld were things that existed and even that doesn't really excuse that they should have gambled the world's future based on possibilities that had no real precedent for happening based solely on not being able to definitively say such things wouldn't happen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T06:00:11-07:00","id":1237739,"title":"","content":"It does have to do with edition because a lot of things subtly changed. First edition, treated the GC as a conclusion that had be reached. Second edition went with, kill the Gold faction so they can't ruin the plan. Which completely changes the setting politics that 1e set up. First edition, gave a lot of reasons for why sidereals don't kill each other. While 2e, just had it casually happen any time it could. Also, Rhaksha and the Underworld wouldn't have changed a thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T06:08:52-07:00","id":1237741,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237739#post1237739\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While 2e, just had it casually happen any time it could. Also, Rhaksha and the Underworld wouldn't have changed a thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that's really true. The 2e Sidereal book had them all working together, with bronze and gold supporters on missions together, and there was only one example of a Bronze Faction member killing a Gold Faction member, which she had to keep secret.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T06:30:00-07:00","id":1237750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237741#post1237741\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think that's really true. The 2e Sidereal book had them all working together, with bronze and gold supporters on missions together, and there was only one example of a Bronze Faction member killing a Gold Faction member, which she had to keep secret.</div> </div> </div> </div>Off the top of my head, the Usurpation and Cobra style and a few other situations I can't remember come to mind. The edition didn't have a problem treating them as every other exalt who have no problem killing each other. Sad Ivory, is supposed to be a major exception, not just one of many. In the first edition, it took a lot before they even considered trying to kill Ronin sidereal. Especially with Thulo being a thing now, and the E3 devs saying they're going to go with 1e over 2e. I hope Sidereals go back to being the Fellowship they used to be portrayed as.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T16:12:05-07:00","id":1237833,"title":"","content":"First,<br /> I never thought that &quot;The Bronze should have foreseen the Great Contagion/Balorian Crusade&quot; arguments were compelling. I always took the stance that, logically, *anyone* should have not prognostically, but common-sense-wise foreseen that <b>Some Sort of Vast, Creation-Totality-Threatening Grave Existential Threat</b> was the inevitable result of pink-slipping 6/7ths of the Celestial Exalts. Trying to 20/20-hindsight-blame the Bronze for not foreseeing the specifics didn't hold a lot of water, to me.<br /> <br /> Second, I took it as a single coin-flip. Heads, Creation goes down in flames. Tails, problem solved, and Super-Utopia arrives. I draw the linkage that the Bronze's unwilling-to-flip the coin is the <b>exact same thing</b> as saying &quot;In exchange for preventing total annihilation, we <b>knowingly accept</b> <b>that things LIKE</b> the Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade will happen.&quot; I've always felt like Bronze Faction proponents believe the level of &quot;lessening&quot; the Bronze thought they were buying was represented by the Shogunate, and the Contagion/Crusade came out of left field. I take the stance that logic, entirely independent of the ability to foretell the future, made it well understood such things would happen, and THAT was the price they chose to pay.<br /> <br /> Where do I get this from? The fact that, consistently, Chejop Kejak has ALWAYS been shown to never have reconsidered the Usurpation, not for a moment, even after the Contagion and Crusade. If those events were not cause to reconsider what they did, he considers them acceptable, anticipated, collateral damage.<br /> <br /> THAT, I consider more monstrous than one coin-flip for the fate of Creation.<br /> <br /> Edit: It goes deeper than that though. Part of the reason I find Nazi's Independents so compelling, is that they take the position <b>It is not the place of Sidereals to decide Fate, it is their job to execute Fate.</b> Basically like how the traditional conception of the Grim Reaper doesn't decide who dies, he makes sure that those who are &quot;supposed&quot; to die, do. That the Bronze and Gold have both become so caught up in Pro-Solar/Anti-Solar agendas, they consider the original job the Sidereal Exalted were created for the secondary focus, and their Solar-centric agendas their primary focus. Whereas Nazri proclaims that, IF there must be Pro-Solar/Anti-Solar agendas, they should clearly be something secondary to the Sidereal Mission Statement. NOT something that divides the Five Score Fellowship to the point of betraying that Mission Statement. Nazi looks at issues like the political war being waged over the Chair of the Convention of Air on purely Bronze/Gold lines, and fears for the Sidereal Exalted as a whole.<br /> <br /> I also took things like the 1E Cult book saying Ghataru was ready to go off the reservation and deploy the Aerial Legion in defense of the Cult's Solar-training camps as implying there was reason to believe the Bronze would attack such camps if they could find them, and throw down with the Gold Sidereals present if necessary to get at the Solars. The entire implication there is one of violence ready to kick off, minus the logistics of one side not knowing where the battle-to-be was yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-22-2018, 07:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T16:31:27-07:00","id":1237837,"title":"","content":"Because the Solars were getting that bad. You are not realizing that the book is a little nebulous on it but from all indications, the solars were becoming unrepentant monsters and the only thing to do with somehow fix their madness, which has a .0001% chance of happening. As far as Chejop believes, letting the solars rule again will just turn creation into a living hell. Which is much worse than anything that's happened so far.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T17:03:29-07:00","id":1237840,"title":"","content":"See that right there,<br /> Having just read the Gold Prophecy material that someone just helpfully quoted, I don't get anything CLOSE to &quot;.0001%&quot; from that. The crux of the matter (for me) is this: Chejop Kejak says &quot;In exchange for a 100% guarantee Creation won't die, we guarantee it will nearly be destroyed, again and again, but it'll continue to limp along, so that's a fair trade.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's no secret by now I don't care for the Bronze/Gold divide as some philosophical difference that somehow doesn't get in the way of the Five Score Fellowship's solidarity. I find much more story potential in two sides who both believe the other are guilty of unpardonable wrongs. If you are Gold, you believe the Bronze Sidereals developed such a mono-focus on avoiding Creation's total destruction, they willfully sanctioned piecemeal destruction that doesn't add up to total devastation. If you're Bronze, you believe the Gold Sidereals are unconscionable gamblers, both willing to risk the total devastation of Creation, and now colluding with the future architects of Creation's annihilation. If you're Independent, you're horrified most of the Fellowship has abandoned its mandate in favor for an assumed role as Architects of Creation's Destiny they've no right to.<br /> <br /> If you have Bronze and Gold Sidereals as Sidereals First, Faction Members Second, it weakens the meaning of the division to me. It imposes an arbitrary unified identity, without good reason for why the UTTER dichotomy of their respective beliefs aren't tearing any such possible bonds asunder. Beyond that, no other Exalt-type has a setting-point-based unity that overcomes their personal differences. The Dynasts scheme against each other, the Lunars prioritize wildly different definitions of &quot;Stewardship&quot; (though I AM glad to see the mono-focus on Protecting X going away for Lunars, Solars are all over the place, Abyssals are separated by their Deathlords competing against each other, and Infernals by their respective Yozis. I think, at some point, the Bronze being so united because they're all deeply invested in the Realm, became conflated with &quot;All Sidereals are THE Secret Masters&quot; so therefore, Faction should be something they should be able to overcome.<br /> <br /> Edit: Unless the Getimian(sp?) angle, and this Thulio's War against Heaven provides a &quot;We must tolerate and work together against our more immediately pressing common enemy, despite what villains you Other Faction members are&quot; sort of angle. I could get behind something like that, so long as it leaves room for the Solar Return to keep increasing the pressures trying to rip the Faction divisions wide open.<br /> <br /> Double Edit: As for 2E: You're talking to an old-school AD&amp;D player (1E was on the way out, with its bullshit-OP Assassins, when I began). To damage a D&amp;D hardback through anything other than page-flipping wear and tear was abominable heresy. To the point my hands shook while doing it, but I STILL cut the Lillun comic and the rapist-rape brothel out of my books. I don't have any tolerance for &quot;Edgy&quot; when Edgy = Child Porn, Rape for Rape's Sake, and any other deviant for deviancy angle meant to create a shock-boost in sales. I believe such things DEFILED Exalted, and I LOVE Exalted. To the point I am glad I never met the owners' of the twisted minds who thought drawing and writing such, for supposed entertainment, and to make MONEY was acceptable. My fury ranneth over when I found such in books I'd eagerly anticipated.<br /> <br /> I'm glad such horrors are a thing of the past. I just hate how they tarred an Edition with great story....albeit deplorable mechanics, and ill-conceived Charm Trees in the first half of the Edition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-22-2018, 08:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T19:03:12-07:00","id":1237861,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237840#post1237840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See that right there,<br /> Having just read the Gold Prophecy material that someone just helpfully quoted, I don't get anything CLOSE to &quot;.0001%&quot; from that. The crux of the matter (for me) is this: Chejop Kejak says &quot;In exchange for a 100% guarantee Creation won't die, we guarantee it will nearly be destroyed, again and again, but it'll continue to limp along, so that's a fair trade.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I will point out that the description you read also says that the Gold was so fracking unstable and likely to go in any direction that it took weeks for a group of people who specialize in reading prophecy to realize that it was the same one. I will also point out that it's not like Kejak was the leader back then, he's considered one of if not the de facto lead for the Sids because he's he oldest and most experienced of them all, back then he would have been one among a many.<br /> <br /> Except its not really like there were good odds on the Gold either and its not like the Bronze told there would be lots of apocalyptic events. Heck technically speaking there weren't even that many after the usurpation. In approximately five-thousand years there were two, except the 2nd happened during the first one and was basically brought about by the first. (Meaning one could technically make an argument the Balorian Crusade and Great Contagion are facets of one apocalypse like several of the conflicts in the Cold War.) So I mean not like you can make an argument of, &quot;Almost get wiped out over and over and over again.&quot; Basically the world is less able to defend itself against apocalyptic threats and the remnants have to work harder to make up for the lost power. Its kind of what they expected.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237840#post1237840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's no secret by now I don't care for the Bronze/Gold divide as some philosophical difference that somehow doesn't get in the way of the Five Score Fellowship's solidarity. I find much more story potential in two sides who both believe the other are guilty of unpardonable wrongs. If you are Gold, you believe the Bronze Sidereals developed such a mono-focus on avoiding Creation's total destruction, they willfully sanctioned piecemeal destruction that doesn't add up to total devastation. If you're Bronze, you believe the Gold Sidereals are unconscionable gamblers, both willing to risk the total devastation of Creation, and now colluding with the future architects of Creation's annihilation. If you're Independent, you're horrified most of the Fellowship has abandoned its mandate in favor for an assumed role as Architects of Creation's Destiny they've no right to.<br /> <br /> If you have Bronze and Gold Sidereals as Sidereals First, Faction Members Second, it weakens the meaning of the division to me. It imposes an arbitrary unified identity, without good reason for why the UTTER dichotomy of their respective beliefs aren't tearing any such possible bonds asunder. Beyond that, no other Exalt-type has a setting-point-based unity that overcomes their personal differences. The Dynasts scheme against each other, the Lunars prioritize wildly different definitions of &quot;Stewardship&quot; (though I AM glad to see the mono-focus on Protecting X going away for Lunars, Solars are all over the place, Abyssals are separated by their Deathlords competing against each other, and Infernals by their respective Yozis. I think, at some point, the Bronze being so united because they're all deeply invested in the Realm, became conflated with &quot;All Sidereals are THE Secret Masters&quot; so therefore, Faction should be something they should be able to overcome.<br /> <br /> Edit: Unless the Getimian(sp?) angle, and this Thulio's War against Heaven provides a &quot;We must tolerate and work together against our more immediately pressing common enemy, despite what villains you Other Faction members are&quot; sort of angle. I could get behind something like that, so long as it leaves room for the Solar Return to keep increasing the pressures trying to rip the Faction divisions wide open.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that doesn't really make sense to have most Sids blaming the others or holding the Bronze Gold divide as this ancient principle thing. I think the count was less than 10 Sids who were around during the event,that still live, so for most of them this would be like me holding a grudge against people who voted for Thomas Jefferson because Jefferson was responsible for altering the United States electoral system to make it where the runner up was not the Vice-President, and then being pissed off at other people because they're the descendants of people who voted for Jefferson. Its an issue now because there are Solars in large numbers again, and that is a lot of power, and a ton of work, and my gods have you the seen the horrors of the job that need to be done by Sids already? Of course there's going to be debate, sometimes it interferes with work, but a lot of times it isn't relevant to the job. I mean whether or not Chuck is for or against disposing of the returned Solars is probably not a huge deal when the current job is auditing the fact that the fellowship has received a crap ton of complaints over this being the umpteenth time the river in Great Forks has been stopped because one of the priests left the river god.<br /> <br /> Although yeah, retrieving stolen exigence before they get used to empower serious problems by other horrible problems and dealing with the Get are probably significant factors this edition in most Sids having adopted a policy of, &quot;I don't care how much you hate what they believe. We have enough crap to handle without any of you idiots making more for all of us.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T20:02:56-07:00","id":1237870,"title":"","content":"Something missed with folks who say the Bronze Faction should have seen the Great Contagion that they forget is that despite the destruction and pain it caused, Creation did survive, lessoned but stable, as a world without gold and silver. I have generally assumed a lot of the current Bronze-Gold debate, in 3e especially, is whether the Contagion meant what &quot;lesser&quot; was was well...lesser than expected, or whether they should have backed off. The youth of the Gold fits this too, with few being around in the Great Prophecy, and never having seen Solar tyranny first hand that was centuries ago.<br /> <br /> It's also worth noting the Great Prophecy only applied to the tiem it was done, and the protents going further, such as the continuation of the Wyld Hunt or the support of Solars, isn't so clear fifteen centuries later. Which you know, drama and shit rises from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":353,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","user":"Blaque","date":". 07-23-2018, 12:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-22T21:08:31-07:00","id":1237879,"title":"","content":"Another amusing point, didn't 2E's version of the Bronze prophecy imply that it only counted deposing the Solars, not imprisoning them? Cause I recall the mention of the forever war aspect and always wondered if that was an interesting try of the Sids to get ahead of the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T00:49:51-07:00","id":1237910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nalak42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237861#post1237861\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In approximately five-thousand years there were two</div> </div> </div> </div>Are you saying that the setting portrays it as having been five millenia since the Usurpation?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T01:21:52-07:00","id":1237913,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237910#post1237910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Are you saying that the setting portrays it as having been five millenia since the Usurpation?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm a bit sketchy on the specific time table I'll admit, but its not exactly a short one if we aren't using geologic time scales. As I understand it Kejack was a young Sid at the time of the Usurpation. Not necessarily a freshly exalted, just doubt he was over a thousand yet. Since Sids have a 5 thousand year lifespan, barring something killing them of course, and part of the Sid story conflicts in 2nd suggested dealing with the fact that Kejack has almost reached the end of his lifespan that would imply the time frame as likely being in the upper part of 4 thousand years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T01:31:03-07:00","id":1237914,"title":"","content":"The guy known to have orchestrated it all, including the *murders* of those who disagreed with him is still there in Heaven though. The woman who carried out his bloody-handed plans, Anys Syn, still has the same job. The fact that the man who killed the predecessor of yours who initially disagreed with him, and then used the fact those murders happened as an opportunity to indoctrinate their successors, who then has the GALL to have any Sidereal who doesn't want to be part of his murder-conspiracy anymore a &quot;traitor&quot; is still sitting there.<br /> <br /> While Ayesha Uru isn't Tammiz Ushun, remembering what happened to Tammiz caused her &quot;to lay awake at night, struggling to contain <b>the hate she felt for the man beside her</b>.&quot; Now, they can hand-wave that out, but that was a case of the successor taking the predecessor's fate quite personally. While the books don't focus on it nearly as much, the mention that Ayesha went to the few Golds remaining was moving to me. To me, drawing a curtain over the past, and &quot;not judging past actors&quot; is a moral whitewash. They're building the Getimians as this War in Heaven thing, and the issue of Black Market and Dirty Bomb Exigents provides MORE than enough fodder to explain why the Factions can't simply start killing each other. That's enough Sidereal Solidarity for me.<br /> <br /> The fact that we can argue the whole Prophecy of Bronze versus Gold here is GREAT. As things stand, things are great. The Bronze has more power, the Gold has the moral high ground. To me, championing that things be rewritten to make it harder to cast the Bronze as the bad guys is a partisan decision. You can be the guys who won the war, or you can be the guys with the higher intentions. Wanting your Bronze to have BOTH the power in Heaven/the Realm, AND a claim to moral high ground (more than what can be argued already, anyways) takes away from the Gold.<br /> <br /> Saying &quot;Why can't they be more reasonable, and less absolutist in their thinking&quot; misses the point. It IS an absolute they're arguing over. Also, you guys keep going back to the prognostication, but I'm trying to say it's simply valid without any prognostication to say that getting rid of the other 6/7ths of Celestial Exalts was going to have an inevitable near-total devastation effect on Creation. We can argue the percentages of the Prophecies, but simple logic says if you need X # of Sidereals watching the Loom &quot;at all times&quot;, Y # of Sidereals Sifu'ing for the new guys (and that gets awful time-intensive during Year and a Days), and Z # of Sidereals trying to correct the causality errors caused by Outside Fate beings...all while you've got some of your oldest minding the Immaculate Dragon Blooded Control Mechanism, that the Bronze were basically saying &quot;We know the Dragon Blooded will basically be on their own in this post-Usurpation Creation most of the time, and we're confident they can adequately defend Creation.....Despite all evidence we've ever had to the contrary.&quot;<br /> <br /> There, right there, see that? Drawing a curtain over the First Age removes things like Gungimdap (know I mangled the name, the Fair Folk war-leader whose massive invasion triggered the Unfurling Horizons era, and left most of the Solars, Lunars and their Dragon Blooded armies out fighting in the Wyld, when the second-to-last free Primordial launched its attack on Creation.) With something like that in canon, the Bronze decision to get rid of the Solars and Lunars looks like outright madness. A single Primordial which had a few centuries to rebuild itself solely for warfare was more effective against the Exalted Host than ALL the other Primordials had been.....<b>and there was still one more out there.</b><br /> <br /> With THAT in canon, you can look at it and go &quot;Umm, if it took the full might of the Solars, the Lunars, the Sidereals, AND every Dragon Blooded we could muster to end this horror....What are we going to do if the last one shows up once the Solars and Lunars are gone?&quot; See, it didn't HAVE to be the Contagion or the Crusade. Look at the rationale behind the Gods choosing to let the Exalted in on the existence of the Sword of Creation. Unshaped were still a thing, and the Gods well understood that the Unshaped of today might get a hair up its ass and turn itself into a Primordial next century. The odds were infinitely small one would choose to do so, but in an infinity of Wyld, it turns into &quot;Any non-zero probability, given sufficient time, will eventually come to pass.&quot;<br /> <br /> Make the First Age into this nebulous blank-slate, with just &quot;The Solars were insane, and they had to go&quot; and you can throw out the counter-argument to the Solar threat. That, while the Solars were a grave, grave threat, Creation was always going to be faced with grave, grave threats, and a massive force-reduction in what could be brought to bear in Creation's defense was never going to end well.<br /> <br /> I'm FAR from a persuasive orator, and even less persuasive with the written word...but leaving room for there to be a moral high-ground intact element to the Gold Faction doesn't really diminish any player or Storyteller's ability to portray the Bronze as the Hard Men and Women Who Made Hard Choices. The argument that the Solars were too dangerous to be allowed to live is always going to be there for the Bronze. Making it so there wasn't initial dissent over the Solars DOES, OTOH, take away from the Gold. Don't mistake me, I am most definitely NOT trying to turn the Gold Sidereals into faultless angels. They are systematically brainwashing Solars to use them like the Bronze use the Dragon Blooded. Their means are almost essentially the same, and just as ugly. If you take initial dissent to the Solar Purge away, they aren't DIFFERENT AT ALL from the Bronze.<br /> <br /> A setting point of &quot;Gold Sidereals, As Bad As the Bronze, With 90% Less Power&quot; just fills me with....Ugh. The very idea makes me against spending hard-to-come-by money on their book when it lands. Initial Dissent to the Purge was the ONE morally pure spot in anything to do with the Sidereals. It was the single thing that at least partially redeems the methods they're using now, because if some of these presently-living Sidereals are the successors to people who never meant the Solars harm, stooping to employ the Bronze's tools NOW with the Cult is somehow less egregious to me. I don't want new canon to take that away, I just don't, and I never, ever will.<br /> <br /> I probably should have mentioned four pages ago this isn't at all about rationality, and is simply about a deeply beloved bit of canon I don't want yanked away, eh?<br /> <br /> Edit: It's 2E, so not the canon anymore, but DotFA did say that Chejop Kejak and Tammiz Ushun were the oldest Sidereals left alive. They're born just a very few years after the Primordial War ended, and by the time the Sidereals had finished the fifty years they spent cloistered away in debate, they were the senior two. Anys Syn is 33 years younger than the two of them....and I had to use a date that relies on elements from CoCD: Malfeas, Underworld and Blessed Isle to arrive at Syn's age. I can lay it out for you, but it takes awhile. They were very, VERY careful regarding Sidereal ages...<br /> <br /> I can give you an ESTIMATE on the four Gold Sidereals who died alongside Tammiz Ushun, but it has at least one contradiction problem preventing specificity. Suffice to say that out of Elder Sidereals at time of Prophecy, there was a reason Tammiz Ushun and his compatriots had to be cut down by surprise. Chejop Kejak had piles of manpower, but not necessarily the most powerful kung-fu guys. The obvious inference is that with the only other Sidereal approaching his power dead, and Kejak retaining the unwavering loyalty of the second-most powerful Sidereal remaining, the Bronze swelled not just with those who thought the Solars had to go...but those who went along because they didn't want Anys Syn to come after them and literally rip their arms off and use them as form-weapons to finish beating them to death.<br /> <br /> Anys Syn = First Sidereal to learn Infernal Monster/Hero style. Began the tradition of Sidereal Elders mining Malfeas for Infernal MA-styles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-23-2018, 04:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T01:49:39-07:00","id":1237916,"title":"","content":"It would also probably help if you didn't keep changing what your argument was cause this is the first time I'm seeing you try to say that you're objecting the idea that all the Sids were in favor of the Bronze, an idea that doesn't have any appearance to be an argument unless you're saying that every generation of Sids between the Usurpation and the return of the Solars not constantly bickering and fighting eachother over an event done ,possibly literal, ages ago counts as unanimous support for the Bronze. Cause until now you've been arguing that the thing Gold need to be try to redeem themselves for their actions in the past that all the Sids need to be holding grudges and ready to kill eachother over this <br /> <br /> Plus your argument of, &quot;Any non-zero chance in the Wyld means is a sure thing given enough time&quot; as a justification for why the Sids were fools for diminishing their forces by dealing with the Solars is well stupid. By this logic the Sids shouldn't have been trying to stablizie Creation post Usurpation, they should have been mobilizing and fortifying Creation for the inevitable war against the new wave of Primordials that are coming who've all purposely designed themselves to war against Creation, because there's no evidence that such a thing could happen outside of Primordials existed, but well its an idea they could have and there is the Wyld meaning its only a matter of time. Hell by this logic the First Age shouldn't have happened for the same reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T02:56:56-07:00","id":1237922,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nalak42</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237913#post1237913\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I understand it Kejack was a young Sid at the time of the Usurpation.</div> </div> </div> </div>In First and Second Edition, Chejop was generally written as having been born at the beginning of the First Age.<br /> <br /> None of the three Editions portray the Second Age as being longer than 1500 years.<br /> <br /> Think about it; if it had been five thousand years since the First Age, why are there so many Lunars who lived during it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Any non-zero probability, given sufficient time, will eventually come to pass</div> </div> </div> </div>Now I'm no expert on statistics, but I'm pretty sure that they don't work like that; that it's entirely possible for an astrologically unlikely scenario to never occur.<br /> <br /> Even if it's not, it's a bad thing to base any kind of policy on.<br /> <br /> As for the idea of a rogue Primordial, that's not really an explicit thing in the current setting and probably never will be. Still, I do actually think that it vaguely approximates a point about how ready the world might be for a major threat, so it really comes down to how much agency one wishes to afford the non-Solar Exalted.<br /> <br /> The thing about the Usurpation is that while it's important, it can't be the be-all end-all of the setting. If one doesn't subscribe wholly to determinism (in which case moral arguments such as this are kind of irrelevant anyway), it can't be that the things that did happen after the fact were inevitable and indelible; the setting as described cannot be the sole outcome of the Usurpation.<br /> <br /> What did end up happening was far from ideal; the Sidereals possibly withdrawing too far, the Dragon Blooded turning on one another, the Lunar Exalted adopting a certain savagery. Plus some stuff with angry ghosts, but that's largely off to the side. What's important is that, sure, all kinds of bad choices were made in the aftermath of the Usurpation, that have ultimately placed Creation in the state of jeopardy that the Solars are returning to.<br /> <br /> They didn't do those things because they're evil; it's emergent from a number of things, some of them well-meaning but insufficiently thought out, some emerging from selfish agendas, some that kind of amount to stupid accidents as a result of too much complex intersections. It's an epic tragedy to be sure, but to articulate it in simple terms of &quot;some evil Sidereals did evil things, and the game is about the few righteous Sidereals teaming up with the good Solars to defeat them&quot;, now <i>that</i> is some actual reductionism.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T03:18:02-07:00","id":1237923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237922#post1237922\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They didn't do those things because they're evil; it's emergent from a number of things, some of them well-meaning but insufficiently thought out, some emerging from selfish agendas, some that kind of amount to stupid accidents as a result of too much complex intersections. It's an epic tragedy to be sure, but to articulate it in simple terms of &quot;some evil Sidereals did evil things, and the game is about the few righteous Sidereals teaming up with the good Solars to defeat them&quot;, now <i>that</i> is some actual reductionism.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean it wasn't insufficiently thought out. That was kind of the point of spending almost a century in debate over it. Granted it was more of a 2e deal. So we'll have to see how 3e portrays it but it was never portrayed as a spur of the moment deal. And 1e and what little of 3e hasn't portrayed it as an unnecessary thing. We'll just have to see how far that extends.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T03:18:32-07:00","id":1237924,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237914#post1237914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Edit: It's 2E, so not the canon anymore, but DotFA did say that Chejop Kejak and Tammiz Ushun were the oldest Sidereals left alive.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's wrong. DotFA still has at least one Sidereal from the Primordial War, Hadeon gets a full write-up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Anys Syn is 33 years younger than the two of them....and I had to use a date that relies on elements from CoCD: Malfeas, Underworld and Blessed Isle to arrive at Syn's age. I can lay it out for you, but it takes awhile. They were very, VERY careful regarding Sidereal ages...</div> </div> </div> </div> Is this headcanon? Any Syn's mentorship under Ni Zhang makes her seem considerably younger. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T04:53:31-07:00","id":1237933,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237923#post1237923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean it wasn't insufficiently thought out. That was kind of the point of spending almost a century in debate over it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Leaving aside the fallacy inherent to this position, obviously when I say &quot;it was a number of things, some of which were insufficiently thought out&quot;, I'm referring to a single thing that was definitely insufficiently thought out, regardless of any evidence to the contrary.<br /> <br /> For my next puzzle, I'm going to write out six lines, and you need to figure out what in them can be used to hang me. Good luck!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T06:39:01-07:00","id":1237944,"title":"","content":"While it might change, it's worth noting that in 3e, assassination of the Golds like Tammiz Ushin wasn't a thing according to the previous devs. There were Sidereals who would have preferred the Gold plan, but due to the fact that if theyw ere split, <i>neither</i> plan would work, they picked one (Bronze) and stuck to that to my udnerstanding.<br /> <br /> Note that this united front, and the Gold Faction being a newer emergent thing int he results of the Contagion and the return of the Solar Exalted. This will do a lot I think in how the Sidereal internal politics work and the roles of people like Chejob.<br /> <br /> And it's worth noting that in all of this, Chejob ultimatley is motivated by his very deeply held consequentialist view of the world. He still thinks he's doing what's right and that's what is meant to make him a compelling antagonist that i Think is getting lost in some of Wyldwraith's points to try and make him the setting's Stalin or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-23T23:46:32-07:00","id":1238149,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1237944#post1237944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While it might change, it's worth noting that in 3e, assassination of the Golds like Tammiz Ushin wasn't a thing according to the previous devs. There were Sidereals who would have preferred the Gold plan, but due to the fact that if theyw ere split, <i>neither</i> plan would work, they picked one (Bronze) and stuck to that to my udnerstanding.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ehhh.<br /> <br /> I honestly find it hard to accept that the Usurpation could be pulled off without the Sidereals having to murder some of their own members immediately after the Great Prophecy. And no, I don't think it could have been over the years of prep after they decided on a course of action - I think that <i>immediately</i>, as soon as the prophecy was finished, they would have realized there were people in the room who couldn't be safely allowed to leave alive. What they were proposing involved killing some of their closest friends (in some cases, spouses or lovers), starting an enormous war with an inevitably massive death toll, and overthrowing their entire society.<br /> <br /> I flatly don't find it credible that all 100 Sidereals could completely trust each other to go along with the plan - and all it would take is a <i>single one</i> who refused to accept the necessity. All Tammiz Ushin has to do is say &quot;welp, I informed twenty Solars, now the decision is set and we have no choice but to work with them!&quot; Note, he wouldn't even have to inform the Solars that the Usurpation was a potential thing - he can present the prophecy as something that the Sidereals and Solars will work together to fix, and the other Sidereals will immediately be <i>forced</i> to go along with his plan as if the Usurpation was never an option. He knows that. The other Sidereals know that. No Sidereal whose willingness to go along with the Usurpation was in question could be allowed to leave the room alive. (And it's silly to suggest that the Sidereals would hesitate to kill him in that situation when they were already preparing to kill so many others.)<br /> <br /> That said, I think I started to diverge from the old devs' views on Sidereals and the Usurpation when I started to seriously consider the narrative implications of allowing Sidereal prophecy to be <i>absolute</i> in the sense that it is 100% narrative-fiat perfect when used at that level and every Sidereal has 100% absolute narrative-fiat understanding of this perfection. Nothing in the real world works that way, and while you can say the same thing about a lot of stuff in a fantasy setting, I feel that taking that route with something so central to the motivations of an entire faction undermines their usefulness for storytelling. Sidereals who have that level of guaranteed certainty for the most important and controversial decision they ever made cease to be <i>people</i>, as I understand the term - they're just logic-robots. It also leads to <i>really</i> unfortunate implications when you start connecting it to any sort of comparable real-world hard decisions - like I mentioned earlier, everyone who ever seized power in a coup has given the exact same justifications about why it was Absolutely Necessary and how Terribly Hard it is to rule on their own etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> I feel like it's a position taken in response to a lot of bad forum arguments and the like, with the intent of annihilating particularly bad takes on the Sidereals using a blast from the authorial canon, so to speak.<br /> <br /> The reality is that it's fine for the Great Prophecy to be potentially fallible as long as the Sidereals were confident <i>enough</i> in it (and had sufficient grounds for that confidence) that their decisions made in response to it at least make sense given the potentially-catastrophic outcome it predicted. This doesn't even require the possibility of a literal &quot;oh it was just wrong&quot; failure - the Sidereals are the best prophets in the setting, but even if Fate is absolute it still requires interpretation, and there's always room for aspects you didn't see. I don't think anyone ever gets to know with <i>absolute certainty</i> that their interpretation is correct, and I think the Great Prophecy is more interesting if it was a process of continuous refinement and interpretation on their prophecies until they finally said &quot;all right, we know enough; it's never <i>absolutely</i> perfect, and yes, there's a chance there's something we're not seeing, but we have to make a decision based on the information we've gleaned.&quot; Because... that's how decision-making works, in reality. The urge to try and make their decision Absolutely Definitely Justified isn't healthy for the game or for storytelling in general; it just has to be a decision they could reasonably make at the time.<br /> <br /> Which touches on a lot of stuff in this thread, but it's also why I think it's important that, yes, there were a few Sidereals who, no matter how much time they spent refining and interpreting the prophecies and getting their accuracy closer to 99.99999%, were always going to say &quot;no, fuck that, fuck this prophecy&quot; and kick over the table at the end of it, because there's always <i>some</i> chance, however slim, that something has been overlooked and that they were willing to risk the universe on that chance rather than do what the prophecy implied they should. And the other Sidereals (who had worked with them for a long, long time) would have known who among them was like this and where it was headed, near the end.<br /> <br /> (I also think that this is honestly a better approach than the fixation on the Prophecy of Bronze vs. Prophecy of Gold thing. Having two competing prophecies doesn't even make as much sense, mythologically. I would generally <i>prefer</i> to frame the Gold Faction as Sidereals whose response to the Great Prophecy was &quot;fuck that, we'll find a better way anyway.&quot; This sets up an actual serious philosophical conflict between them and the Bronze Faction - who went with the safer option or who viewed the Gold Faction as insanely, dangerously idealistic / blinding themselves to the truth because they didn't want to see it. If you give everyone their own prophecy it's not as interesting and doesn't really compare as much to real-world philosophical / political arguments.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","user":"Aquillion","date":". 07-24-2018, 02:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T17:19:55-07:00","id":1238422,"title":"","content":"Whoa,<br /> You totally had my vote for Best Post of the Thread <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar517_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"517\" data-vbnamecard=\"517\">Aquillion</a> ...Right up until the One Prophecy idea. While the way YOU are presently articulating it is only moderately problematic, I can count the # of heartbeats before the interpretation of Bronze-inclined PLAYERS morph that into &quot;All the prophetic support was on the Bronze side, the Gold Sidereals had absolutely no basis for dissent, and the Great Prophecy proves they were just as crazy as the Solars they were trying to protect&quot; on one hand.<br /> <br /> Having re-read the entire thread, and seriously reexamined my position, this is what I'm left with.<br /> 1) If you keep powerful God/Goddesses from the time of the Usurpation active in modern-day Heaven, you always keep the Usurpation politics alive, to at least some extent. Unlike the Sidereals who are moving on to new lives, and may develop wildly different political stances from life to life, a deity's politics are only going to change if they are given reasons to change them so compelling as to be equivalent to convincing the deity to alter its very nature. The relatively inflexible, unbroken memory-possessing, examples of extremes related to their domain coloring their views of everything they view through a pretty mono-focused lens has been a staple of Exalted forever. Uvanavu, God of Health, is rampantly pro-Solar because the level of good health among all living things in Creation is vastly lower than it was under the Solars. Even the wildlife was healthier, because optimum weather and such benefited them. Unless he's persuaded some force besides the Solars can (and will) restore the abundant good health the living things of Creation enjoyed in the First Age, his good health-lens will always make his view &quot;I want Solars back, so we get the good health of the First Age back.&quot; To change this, without giving Uvanavu that alternative source of increased good health for Creation, you'd have to convince him the Solars will never raise Creation's health level as they did in the past. A daunting prospect, because you're trying to prove a negative, against the positive bias he associates with the Solars' First Age achievements.<br /> <br /> Any Sidereal (like Chosen of Serenity), who work in the Cerulean Lute of Harmony where he's a major player, are going to hear all about how the health of Creation was so much better in the old days, when the Solars ruled. As the God of Health, Uvanavu isn't just spitting rhetoric either. He's got the hard numbers for a detailed comparison/contrast of any year you'd care to name, with context-specific bias allowed for. The Chosen of Serenity can tune him out, but Uvanavu raising the issue in a hard-numbers way that checks out if the Joybringer wants to do any independent research in the Department's files, will certainly make the issue one that is real for the Joybringer, whatever side of the Solar issue they come down on. Even if they decide his position is irrelevant, because health is irrelevant when the Solars were piloting Creation towards total annihilation, this part of the Usurpation will be alive for the Joybringer in a way it wasn't for them before, because a living, breathing, sentient relic of the Usurpation-era is right there, co-signing reports on the latest plague that broke out in Town X, with a &quot;See, what did I tell you?&quot; expression.<br /> <br /> 2) If you don't like two competing Prophecies, and you don't like the absolute precision of Sidereal prognostication (which, incidentally, I agree with you about) make it one Prophecy, with wildly diverging probabilities entirely open to interpretation. The Bronze didn't need confirmation to take the stand that ANY chance of Creation's total annihilation was too large. Half of this thread has been wildly divergent opinions on the interpretation of the Factions interpretations. While I love me some quantification, if you quantify this, even vaguely like &quot;This result more likely, this result less likely&quot; it always seems to jump to players trying to nail down the quantification to a percentage. I would be perfectly fine if the Prophecy said &quot;The Solars may destroy Creation&quot; and have the rest be those who became Bronze looking about at the Solars current sins-in-progress, the general state of the world, and concluding &quot;May = Will.&quot; Then those who become the Gold <b>don't even weigh in</b> on the prognostication angle. Where the Bronze takes its stand on &quot;Any % threat to Creation in its entirety is intolerable&quot; the Gold goes &quot;Prophecy isn't an exact science, and beyond that, Exalts have always tangled Destiny into a mess, ESPECIALLY when you try to parse a totality of an Exalt group in one sweeping prediction. The not-insignificant margin-for-error means you would be committing mass murder, and plunging all Creation into civilization-cracking conflict for something you can't even be certain WILL happen!<br /> <br /> I agreed in spirit with pretty much everything you said in your most recent post <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar517_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"517\" data-vbnamecard=\"517\">Aquillion</a> , and I realize it sort of sounds like I'm disagreeing with you, while saying the same thing you are, but I always look at the fine-detail for abusable print. If a nuanced approach to the setting-point is shaped in such a way it can be brute-forced into an absolute, it will be. I've been guilty of that pretty much all week, and so has Epimetheus. We're essentially Exhibits A and B for why the Sidereal Prophecy needs to be one of the most carefully considered, reviewed and re-reviewed thing the Developers do for EX3. A bunch of people aren't going to be happy, whatever they do. Just like a bunch of people aren't going to be happy with the Lunar treatment, whatever it turns out to be (Because somehow it seems us Lunar fans want Lunars to be All Things to All People), but if they botch it, they'll be botching a lot more than the Sidereal splat.<br /> ,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T18:09:59-07:00","id":1238435,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1238149#post1238149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ehhh.<br /> <br /> I honestly find it hard to accept that the Usurpation could be pulled off without the Sidereals having to murder some of their own members immediately after the Great Prophecy. And no, I don't think it could have been over the years of prep after they decided on a course of action - I think that <i>immediately</i>, as soon as the prophecy was finished, they would have realized there were people in the room who couldn't be safely allowed to leave alive. What they were proposing involved killing some of their closest friends (in some cases, spouses or lovers), starting an enormous war with an inevitably massive death toll, and overthrowing their entire society.<br /> <br /> I flatly don't find it credible that all 100 Sidereals could completely trust each other to go along with the plan - and all it would take is a <i>single one</i> who refused to accept the necessity. All Tammiz Ushin has to do is say &quot;welp, I informed twenty Solars, now the decision is set and we have no choice but to work with them!&quot; Note, he wouldn't even have to inform the Solars that the Usurpation was a potential thing - he can present the prophecy as something that the Sidereals and Solars will work together to fix, and the other Sidereals will immediately be <i>forced</i> to go along with his plan as if the Usurpation was never an option. He knows that. The other Sidereals know that. No Sidereal whose willingness to go along with the Usurpation was in question could be allowed to leave the room alive. (And it's silly to suggest that the Sidereals would hesitate to kill him in that situation when they were already preparing to kill so many others.<br /> <br /> That said, I think I started to diverge from the old devs' views on Sidereals and the Usurpation when I started to seriously consider the narrative implications of allowing Sidereal prophecy to be <i>absolute</i> in the sense that it is 100% narrative-fiat perfect when used at that level and every Sidereal has 100% absolute narrative-fiat understanding of this perfection. Nothing in the real world works that way, and while you can say the same thing about a lot of stuff in a fantasy setting, I feel that taking that route with something so central to the motivations of an entire faction undermines their usefulness for storytelling. Sidereals who have that level of guaranteed certainty for the most important and controversial decision they ever made cease to be <i>people</i>, as I understand the term - they're just logic-robots. It also leads to <i>really</i> unfortunate implications when you start connecting it to any sort of comparable real-world hard decisions - like I mentioned earlier, everyone who ever seized power in a coup has given the exact same justifications about why it was Absolutely Necessary and how Terribly Hard it is to rule on their own etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> I feel like it's a position taken in response to a lot of bad forum arguments and the like, with the intent of annihilating particularly bad takes on the Sidereals using a blast from the authorial canon, so to speak.</div> </div> </div> </div>The choices sidereals had weren't two, there were three. The solars had gone mad and the sidereals could just navel gaze and do nothing. By doing this, creation would most certainly be lost. They looked into how to fix the solars, and found it to be astronomically slim. Then they saw what would happen if they removed the solars and saw their best chance at keeping creation alive. It wasn't just 3 paths, it was thousands of variations. They either do something or do nothing.<br /> <br /> The reason 100 sidereals completely trusted each other to go along with the plan was simple, if they fucked up creation would be destroyed. If they fought or were split then it would just be th same as if they did nothing. So despite Tammiz Ushin's love of solars, if he informed them at any moment before action was taken, all he would have done is destroyed creation faster. This was about the fate of creation, and everyone in it. It wasn't a rebellion or a small scale decision. Almost every version of fixing the solars ended with failure and the loss of everything sidereals have labored to protect since the primordial war. The entire point of the prophesy was that there was a chance to fix solars, but the chance was so small that it was near impossible. So people cast aside their doubts, and worked together to make sure that creation would survive regardless. The entire point of the prophecy is that they saw the choices together, realized what had to be done and did it. The vision of gold was something that was presented with very little chance of success. So little not even people who were for it could honestly decide to fuck everyone in creation just for 300 mad god kings.<br /> <br /> Also, due to the fact that they are following 1E, the old devs views aren't going to be much different than what we will eventually get. The reason the Prophecy is accurate, is because sidereals have the ability to see into the future and that should be somewhat useful. If the prophesy wasn't remotely accurate, then it would pretty much make every capability sidereals have, useless. Also, sidereals don't just look into the future, they also make it. When they saw into the future to see their options and they could have just done nothing. This would have almost certainly doomed creation. So instead they said fuck that and took destiny into their own hand. Which is also something sidereals do. They don't just follow destiny, they take it into their own hands and make it. You are acting like the Vision of bronze was a foregone conclusion because of this, when it was 100 people actively choosing to change the future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T18:47:16-07:00","id":1238462,"title":"","content":"If your goal for a portrayal of the Usurpation is that no one in the playerbase can possibly force it to support them telling you &quot;No, obviously you're wrong&quot;, then you are demanding a presentation that unambigiously supports your side. There is no such thing as a nuanced portrayal that can't be selectively emphasized and de-emphasized into PROOF! by a fanatic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T19:26:43-07:00","id":1238479,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1238422#post1238422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Whoa,<br /> You totally had my vote for Best Post of the Thread <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar517_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"517\" data-vbnamecard=\"517\">Aquillion</a> ...Right up until the One Prophecy idea. While the way YOU are presently articulating it is only moderately problematic, I can count the # of heartbeats before the interpretation of Bronze-inclined PLAYERS morph that into &quot;All the prophetic support was on the Bronze side, the Gold Sidereals had absolutely no basis for dissent, and the Great Prophecy proves they were just as crazy as the Solars they were trying to protect&quot; on one hand.</div> </div> </div> </div>Players should try not to get that heavily attached to a viewpoint out-of-character. The point is what makes for good stories and an interesting setting.<br /> <br /> The Gold Faction as the Sidereals who were willing to say &quot;fuck destiny, we'll find a better way&quot; even in the face of a seemingly clear prophecy to the contrary is more powerful and evocative than them just being people who preferred a different prophecy. It establishes the two factions as having fundamentally different outlooks on fate and necessity and on the role of Sidereals in relation to these things, rather than just having different views on Solars.<br /> <br /> The way I see it is that the Bronze Faction are the Sidereals who believe that their role is to &quot;choose the best option out of the ones available.&quot; The Gold Faction believes that their role is to &quot;find the ideal option no matter what.&quot; These are similar but fundamentally irreconcilable views that make for an interesting philosophical and ideological conflict suitable for a heroic fantasy game. If you make it two prophecies then it ends up becoming &quot;do they like the Solars or not?&quot;, which is much less interesting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1238435#post1238435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The choices sidereals had weren't two, there were three. The solars had gone mad and the sidereals could just navel gaze and do nothing. By doing this, creation would most certainly be lost. They looked into how to fix the solars, and found it to be astronomically slim. Then they saw what would happen if they removed the solars and saw their best chance at keeping creation alive. It wasn't just 3 paths, it was thousands of variations. They either do something or do nothing.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not a narratively meaningful choice and you know it. &quot;Or you could choose to do nothing and everyone would absolutely, 100% definitely die!&quot; serves no purpose but to make the decision less complicated and interesting. Again, it's something that was wedged in order to protect the Sidereals from bad forum-arguments; but I don't think that that's what the book should be concerned with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The reason the Prophecy is accurate, is because sidereals have the ability to see into the future and that should be somewhat useful. If the prophesy wasn't remotely accurate, then it would pretty much make every capability sidereals have, useless. </div> </div> </div>Again, the prophecy does not have to be 100% absolutely definitely accurately interpreted in order to serve its purpose. It just has to be accurate enough that Kejak Chejop can credibly say that risking the entire universe on the hope that it's wrong (or spending years trying to find a better path while Creation falls apart around your ears) is insane. Most prophets in fiction are not perfectly accurate at reading and interpreting their prophecies, after all. And in the real world we make important decisions on much weaker information all the time. A prophecy that is 99.9999% accurate (with the 0.00001% chance probably being the possibility that the Sidereals have misunderstood it, rather than &quot;lol fate was just wrong&quot;) still has more than enough weight to make every decision the Bronze Faction made at least defensible. It's certainly not a <i>useless</i> prophecy.<br /> <br /> But it means that the Bronze Faction's decisions are also ideologically and philosophically significant. They had a choice, and a more interesting one than just &quot;pick between these two prophecies.&quot;<br /> <br /> Again, like I said, I feel that forum arguments have turned it into &quot;either fate and the Sidereal reading of it is 100% perfectly accurate in a way that completely justifies everything the Sidereals did fullstop, or the Sidereals are doofuses whose actions are stupid and dumb.&quot; That's a false dichotomy. There's room for a setting where the Gold Faction can say &quot;they should have taken the risk; they should have been willing to keep looking for a better future&quot; and the Bronze Faction can say &quot;no, that's too idealistic; at the end of the day we had to take the information we had and act on it in order to avert the worst possible outcome&quot;, and have both arguments be defensible and interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","user":"Aquillion","date":". 05-20-2019, 03:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T20:02:33-07:00","id":1238488,"title":"","content":"Why? Why do both sides have to be defensible in the past? All it does is create this, what if scenario bullshit. The great prophesy was just a means to an end. It made sense to follow it before the contagion but then the split occurred. Now solars are back and the issue has nothing to do with the past and everything to do with the present.<br /> <br /> Personally, I'd rather 3e remove the prophesy and just keep the solars went mad so the sidereals and dragon blooded killed them. It removes all this what if bullshit.<br /> <br /> Edit- Since I'm grammar mistakes from posting on my phone. Let me remind you of the totally ambiguous history of how the exalted murdered the poor primordials and then murdered billions of their created races. There was absolutely all the moral ambiguity on how they could have just talked to the primordials and could have gotten the gods a bunch of free days. OH WAIT. That didn't happen one fucking bit. This argument is so pointless. I'm sure individual gold and bronze faction members will debate on it but the past is the past. I don't see why it needs to be presented as anything but what it was.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":116,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","user":"Epimetheus","date":". 07-24-2018, 11:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T20:33:57-07:00","id":1238494,"title":"","content":"Because the decision to go with the Usurpation is what defines the Sidereals in general and the Bronze Faction in particular in the present day. If it wasn't a meaningful decision then it's not doing a good job of that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T20:55:05-07:00","id":1238499,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aquillion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1238494#post1238494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because the decision to go with the Usurpation is what defines the Sidereals in general and the Bronze Faction in particular in the present day. If it wasn't a meaningful decision then it's not doing a good job of that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm just going to restate, the prophesy has always been a means to an end. They could have rejected it. They could have decided to ignore their choices and go with something new. Whether it's 100% right or wrong, doesn't change the fact that they did it. It's presented as a fact because it's history at this point. The solars were mad, the sidereals looked into the future, and they decided to kill them. Considering how much the 1e book goes about how sidereals are masters of their own fate. It should have been obvious that they chose what to do in the end. They did it as a unified group. You don't lose out on any of the conflict by presenting it as otherwise. In this edition, the gold saw the contagion and had a change of heart. Sometimes what feels right to do then, doesn't feel like it was right after. Or maybe to them it was right at the time, but solars are back and they're young and they can be molded.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T21:36:44-07:00","id":1238507,"title":"","content":"I think a thing Epimetheus points out is also important to remember: Whatever happened, they went with Bronze. Whatever else was around the generation of the prophecy ultimate is moot. Whatever number of Sidereals needed to do it, did it. If murder had to happen or not, it's kind of moot actually. And seems often used anyhow just to make CHejob &quot;Actulaly such a horrible guy...&quot; as this thread shows.<br /> <br /> How many Sidereals sided or not, whether they had to be gotten rid of or not, anda ll that, is kind of the thing that's just not best dwelled on. Because at the end of the day, no one told the Solars, and the ones running the Gold now weren't there when it happened anyhow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T22:06:48-07:00","id":1238508,"title":"","content":"The problem with treating it as a completely dead issue is that the setting <i>has</i> characters on both sides who lived through it -- thanks to the Elder Lunars. Even if the Sidereals are satisfied with &quot;We all thought it was for the best, but now some of us are having second thoughts&quot;, the Lunars certainly aren't. Whether the Usurpation was heroic or villainous needs to be something debatable for setting purposes, or else you're declaring the default/majority faction for a PC splat to be unambiguously wrong.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T22:34:26-07:00","id":1238515,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1238508#post1238508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with treating it as a completely dead issue is that the setting <i>has</i> characters on both sides who lived through it -- thanks to the Elder Lunars. Even if the Sidereals are satisfied with &quot;We all thought it was for the best, but now some of us are having second thoughts&quot;, the Lunars certainly aren't. Whether the Usurpation was heroic or villainous needs to be something debatable for setting purposes, or else you're declaring the default/majority faction for a PC splat to be unambiguously wrong.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but Lunars as a whole probably don't need much more epistemic clarity on how conflicted Sidereals were than we or Solars do. Just that the Bronze solution said &quot;without gold or silver' and Lunars got the short end of the stick by being knocked out of their spot as (to them) rightful heirs to the throne, so to speak.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-24T22:35:04-07:00","id":1238516,"title":"","content":"And I would add, many Lunars at this point probably aren't in a mood for hearing excuses for the &quot;Sorry man, you had to go too.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-25T01:00:42-07:00","id":1238526,"title":"","content":"If you do want a single prophecy, and say that Sidereals reacted differently in their confidence in prophecy, then one thing with that seems that there are at least a couple more way to respond than choosing to reform the Solar Exalted ...<br /> <br /> ... maybe some of the Bronze were audacious dreamers who thought &quot;Fuck that, we have to get rid the Solars right now, but the accuracy of this prophecy about a world without them being lesser? If our prophecy is untrustworthy enough for the Gold Faction to discard, let's ignore and discard the parts that say our world must be less without these tyrants, and believe in our ability to make a better world&quot;.<br /> <br /> ... while maybe some of the Gold would disregard the Great Prophecy out of the belief that &quot;If our prophecy is untrustworthy, why not follow our betters and obey the status quo?&quot; (This is the response that would be most similar to the the head in the sand, do nothing prophecy in canon).<br /> <br /> That is, between obedience with self skepticism, and self belief with the willingness to rebel.<br /> <br /> The three prophecies of canon (whatever their problems) as Epimetheus presents above doesn't really have the same underlying multiplicity of responses; there simply are three routes, not the wide variety of human responses that can be born from an unknown future, and which do not align much with how much people favor deference to anyone like the Solar Exalted.<br /> <br /> (Generally, the Great Prophecy does limit and constrain many things around the wider setting. It's a hard setting element to deal with and I wonder if there aren't setting problems from putting all of the Sidereals credibility at prophecy on a single high stakes bet (so to speak)! I've no desire to get into the wider debate but Wyldwraith does seem correct to imply that it's hard for the path of Bronze to retain capability if it is obvious that threats from out of Creation that the Sidereals have no special ability to predict are constantly threatening Creation since the First Age! I assume that the setting, as originally envisioned, did not have this feature, and that when introduced in later work, not much thought was given to how the GP making sense constrained introducing First Age &quot;Out of Context&quot; threats).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":929,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","user":"Ghosthead","date":". 07-25-2018, 04:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-25T04:18:25-07:00","id":1238550,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1238508#post1238508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with treating it as a completely dead issue is that the setting <i>has</i> characters on both sides who lived through it -- thanks to the Elder Lunars. Even if the Sidereals are satisfied with &amp;quot;We all thought it was for the best, but now some of us are having second thoughts&amp;quot;, the Lunars certainly aren't. Whether the Usurpation was heroic or villainous needs to be something debatable for setting purposes, or else you're declaring the default/majority faction for a PC splat to be unambiguously wrong.</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering how emphasized it's been that solars were going mad. I'd say the debate should be on approach.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-27T01:00:06-07:00","id":1239221,"title":"","content":"I was paraphrasing a quote, when I said &quot;Any non-zero probability, rises to a 100% certainty, given an unlimited amount of time.&quot; It was said by a Nobel Laureate, in the context of talking about the nuclear arsenal, and how many close-calls to nuclear deployment have occurred due to errors in launch-detection systems. A scandinavian rocket launched near Antarctica was mistaken by Russian early warning systems with an (at the time) recently inspected and quantified error-level of .9% He was saying that since the error-level is non-zero, and since nuclear deployment can occur based almost entirely on what the Russians computers are telling them, eventually the point nine percent of the first level of redundancy will line up with the fractions of a percentage error-levels in the second and third layers of redundancy, and how even if it takes ten centuries, so long as the nukes stay pointed the way they're pointed, eventually BOOM.<br /> <br /> I was drawing a parallel between that, and the vanishingly small chance of a being equivalent to a Primordial rising up once more out of the Wyld. The whole point of THAT being the Solars, Lunars and Sidereals were all, at least at one time, very concerned about such a possibility. A possibility that worried the Gods as well, which informed their decision to tell the Exalts about the Sword of Creation at all. Which was part of my pointing out how drawing a curtain over the OTHER threats Creation had faced during the First Age serves by default to strengthen the Bronze argument. Ie: If you remove the existence of any credible threat to Creation in its entirety BUT the Solars, that in itself is story-reductive.<br /> <br /> Also, the &quot;This is canon, and that's that&quot; argument has never been a terribly persuasive one. Stupidity has a long history in Exalted, and trust me, we haven't seen the end of it just because a new edition is arriving. Anything that 1) Doesn't make logical sense, 2) Isn't considered fun by the majority of players who look at it, and 3) Storytellers find more reductive than additive generally gets binned.<br /> <br /> As Aquillion said, the elder Lunars are still there. Add to that, the GODS who lived through it are still there (and Gods without agendas aren't Gods). You can't just say &quot;Event X Happened&quot; and leave it at that, and I think you know that Epimetheus. Nice attempted deployment of the turn-what's-a-question into a statement, however.<br /> <br /> The developers ARE going to have to do better than the EX3 Core blurb on the Usurpation when they get to the Sidereals book. Word-count and book focus explains a general statement in Core, but once you reach the higher Yu-Shan resolution of the new Sidereals book, it's going to take more. It's not just explaining why the Sidereals would be united on the Usurpation. You have to explain what created the same unification of conviction among the divinities as well. They'll need to nail down what really triggered the point of dissent, etc etc etc.<br /> <br /> To me, making the Bronze position the only logically defensible one by, as Aquillion stated, a blast of the authorial cannon would do the setting an incredible disservice. It's an approach with an inherently lower utility.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-27T06:37:55-07:00","id":1239277,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1239221#post1239221\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A possibility that worried the Gods as well, which informed their decision to tell the Exalts about the Sword of Creation at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>What a conversation that must have been - &quot;Hey, remember that gun you built? I wasn't going to tell you at first, but <i>you can use it to shoot at things!</i>&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 07-27-2018, 12:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-27T08:59:14-07:00","id":1239327,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"24378\" data-vbnamecard=\"24378\">Wyldwraith</a> The gods can't do anything in creation but provide blessings and they haven't had anything to do with the sword of creation. Not only that but they have no ability to effect it or punish the people who abuse it. That's what the entire creation ruling mandate was about. Now a number of gods are breaking that rule but that still doesn't mean they have any say to it. The gods don't even know how in depth the sidereal were responsible for the usurpation. They just know a usurpation happened. At this point, expanding on the great prophesy is a waste. There was a decision, the prophesy was a tool to help them get to that decision. If you need to know specifically how much of a baby eating god monster solars were becoming that's really unnecessary. We don't need to know specifics. We just needed to know that the solars were going mad and they had to be put down. Just as, again, we didn't need to know what the primordials were doing, just that they were alien beings who were doing whatever they wanted to creation. You can use your imagination.<br /> <br /> If solars going mad, isn't enough then there's something wrong with your perspective. You clearly think of solars as perfect beings who can do no wrong in that case. So we are never going to agree. It's fine enough for the book to portray the solars going mad from an objective point of view because anyone who wasn't a solar was getting abusing. This has been a thing noted in all three editions. Even the most ardent, solar supporters during that time, must have clearly realized it wasn't going to be possible to save them. So the only thing they could do was kill them. I don't really get why this is a hard concept to wrap around.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-27T15:19:14-07:00","id":1239449,"title":"","content":"Whoa,<br /> Never, at any time, have I remotely conceptualized or characterized the Solars as &quot;perfect beings who can do no wrong.&quot; In fact, here's MY Meta-perception of the First Age. The Solars instability was exponentially aggravated by them being in charge of everything. The Sidereals were absolutely justified in mass-resetting them, because clearly Solars died on a regular basis, in ones and twos all throughout the First Age, and the incoming new Solars were rapidly corrupted by the still-living long-lived Solars running the show. Triggering a mass-reset, in order to break the political and cultural inertia which had corrupted all the Solars Exalted since the end of the Great War in a FRACTION of the time it took the War's survivors to &quot;fall&quot; was justified.<br /> <br /> Where I take issue is with the Jade Prison, KEEPING the Solars gone. Coupling that with turning on the Lunars simultaneously wasn't smart. Leaving the Jade Prison, that shouldn't have existed, unguarded WITH A PILE OF SOLAR GHOSTS SURROUNDING IT well-exceeded the point of insanity.<br /> <br /> What you don't seem to get is that saying that none of the Celestial Exalts alive at the time of the Usurpation were aware of threats to Creation other than, and contemporary to the Solars is BORING. Ancient threats which precede the currently &quot;dominant&quot; mythos, like the Titans preceding the Olympians is why we have the Yozis, and that's because that's about as &quot;mythological&quot; as it gets. Saying Creation had been so tidied up that no Sidereal could reasonably argue &quot;You know, over the millennia of Solar rule since the Great War, there HAVE been emergent dangers (dealt with by the Exalted as a whole) I find it impossible to imagine the Dragon Blooded effectively defending Creation against with only us (Sidereals) to help them&quot; has implications for the setting well beyond the Usurpation itself. It makes it look like no one could have possibly imagined that pink-slipping nearly all the Celestial Exalts could have grave consequences. It reduces reasons for dissension with such a sweeping resolution.<br /> <br /> Just because I hate the idea of Absolute Sidereal Hegemony at the time of the Usurpation doesn't mean I'm some Solar Paragon Fanboy. If you want the truth of it, I haven't played a Solar in years. I'm a Lunar guy, and rampantly so. I'm the kind of guy who finds myself gritting my teeth when I read that &quot;Lunar taming leash&quot; Solar Charm.<br /> <br /> I know this might come as a shock <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"116\" data-vbnamecard=\"116\">Epimetheus</a> , but it's quite possible to view the Faction who created something as singularly evil as the Realm, and <b>didn't even TRY</b> to do better than &quot;The Dragon Blooded are Princes of the Earth, no matter WHAT they do, there is no point at which we will decide the misery and deprivation they create in ALL Creation is too much&quot; as the bad guys, without being a &quot;The Solars are totally rad! Everyone should've ignored the garden of human agony, and the Prefecture where a lunatic God-Queen turned every bit of living matter into animate mineral, going so far as to trap the SOULS of the mortals in crystal golems. They made sure everyone had piles of Magitech!&quot; Fanboy.<br /> <br /> The fact I object to a mono-focus setting point without the slightest resemblance to actual human decision-making couldn't POSSIBLY be that I find a group of incredibly powerful individuals, cursed by ancient dying Cthonic horrors with vast hubris, discussing Ending the World as They Know It, universally arriving at a single result umm...retarded? I would really, REALLY like to know where ANYTHING I said indicates so much as a contention the Usurpation shouldn't have happened.<br /> <br /> No, it isn't <b><i>&quot;fine enough&quot;</i></b> for the book to say it was a universal point of view. That kills something called nuance. I treasure things like the Solar Queen of Ondar Shambal NOT being the lunatic most of the OTHER Solars had turned into, to the point her Dragon Blooded fought their own in her defense <b>precisely BECAUSE it was an OUTLIER!</b> That the tragedy of the Usurpation was compounded by the fact not every Solar was a mad monster, deserving of the rabid dog's fate <b>MOST</b> of them deserved!<br /> <br /> My OOC sympathies lay with the Gold Sidereals, because they have not given up on a better Creation. Whereas the Bronze looks around and says, much as you do &quot;Eh, it's <b>fine enough</b>. The Realm is an acceptable means of directing Creation. Let's pay no mind to the fact we've actually introduced evils Creation didn't suffer from before, like the hosts of sentient beings now enslaved, or the economic deprivation that makes Luvilla, Goddess of Prostitution so much happier than she was in the First Age, because equal throngs now sell their bodies and their dignity to make ends meet.&quot; The Gold Sidereals have situated their goal-posts such that they're actually taking into account the QUALITY of mortal life, as opposed to the Bronze simply considering the PERPETUATION of mortal life &quot;fine enough.&quot;<br /> <br /> Making it impossible to say a reasonable Sidereal could not have disagreed with the Usurpation, is to license absolutism. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar517_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"517\" data-vbnamecard=\"517\">Aquillion</a> has made far more eloquent arguments than I ever could, as to why it doesn't make sense. The fact you had to resort to attempting to jam words down my throat <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"116\" data-vbnamecard=\"116\">Epimetheus</a> , in addition to <i>ad hominem </i>attacks is disappointing. I've come on strong, more than once, in this thread. I clashed with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> earlier, but neither one of us went there, despite Isator reaching a similar point of feeling there was nothing more productive to be exchanged so far as ideas on the topic under discussion at that point. I may rip into an idea that aggravates me, a position that makes me want to beat my head on the desk, but I haven't tried to insult anyone personally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-27T21:02:45-07:00","id":1239548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1238508#post1238508\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with treating it as a completely dead issue is that the setting <i>has</i> characters on both sides who lived through it -- thanks to the Elder Lunars. Even if the Sidereals are satisfied with &quot;We all thought it was for the best, but now some of us are having second thoughts&quot;, the Lunars certainly aren't. Whether the Usurpation was heroic or villainous needs to be something debatable for setting purposes, or else you're declaring the default/majority faction for a PC splat to be unambiguously wrong.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Elder Sidereals are an even more important issue. As a Sidereal &quot;what was this choice, why was it made, and what does it mean&quot; is often going to be a vitally important question because it still defines the Sidereals in the present day. It's meant to speak to their themes in the same way the position of their previous incarnation in the First Age speaks to Solar themes, or the Shogunate speaks to Dragon-Blooded themes. Is it going to be relevant in every single game? No. But it's absurd to say &quot;oh, it's just a dead issue.&quot;<br /> <br /> Here in the real world, with people who live far shorter lives, no magical reincarnation, nobody still alive from that era and so on, there are <i>still</i> plenty of people who care very very intensely about events that happened hundreds or even thousands of years ago. I just don't see how you can say that &quot;what sort of choice did the Sidereals have, exactly, and what did it mean to them?&quot; isn't an important question.<br /> <br /> And, on top of that, as a Sidereal you have to answer the question of &quot;what did my previous incarnation think of the Usurpation?&quot; Disagreeing with the choices your past incarnation made are pretty common for the Exalted, but I still think it is absolutely, 100% <i>essential</i> that PCs have the option to say &quot;no, my previous incarnation didn't go along with it.&quot; Establishing that there were <i>no</i> Sidereals who rejected the Usurpation takes that option off the table and therefore has serious implications for any Sidereal PC who cares a lot about their previous incarnations - and sets a meaningful constraint on what stories you can tell.<br /> <br /> (Thinking about it, I suspect the latter issue is the main reason the books unambiguously established that there were Sidereals who didn't go along with it.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/517-aquillion","name":"Aquillion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar517_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1341"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-27T21:17:00-07:00","id":1239554,"title":"","content":"It kind of depends on how many elder Sidereals there are in total and how many of them are pretty much, &quot;Yeah, I did it, what?&quot; and move on. I mean, even assuming the ones who might have leaked were killed, this still means that the ones who are here were pretty on board with the Vision of Bronze and as far as they were concerned, it was the right choice at the time based on what htey knew probably.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-27T21:27:09-07:00","id":1239557,"title":"","content":"A major reason that I like the premise of the Gold Faction as a relatively new thing, under the terms that writers have often referred to the idea, is that I like the idea that the condition of the prophecy in which a saved Creation would be diminished is something that has been examined and questioned within the setting, and ultimately judged to be not worth it.<br /> <br /> In retrospect, I feel as though when the only voice for the Bronze ideology are people that more-or-less accept the diminishment whole-heartedly, it makes the whole situation look obtuse and incompetent, particularly in the face of things like the Great Contagion. I'm not put off by that <i>solely</i> because it prompts people to form conclusions that the Bronze Faction is basically evil (and stupid to boot), although those are a significant part of it.<br /> <br /> Mostly, I think the Gold Faction is more interesting if it consists not of people on the sidelines tutting at their colleagues and repeatedly remanding them with &quot;I told you so&quot;s, with the security of a clearly defined alternate path to which they've been attached for a thousand years, but of people who once went along with the party line, some out of genuine belief and some out of group solidarity, who have seen the results, and come to the conclusion that no, a diminished Creation is not an acceptable end state.<br /> <br /> That's not to say that no part of the prophecy had the high-risk idea of helping to redeem the Solars.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T01:40:55-07:00","id":1239601,"title":"","content":"Obviously, I have to disagree: Again and again, all this EX3-return-to-1E talk describes not judging the past actors, but that's always been at the heart of the Sidereals. Unlike Solars and Lunars, where it's not at all a given all their peers are going to know who they used to be, its always been universal that all 100 Sidereals not only know who every new Sidereal was, they're all as much into the details/gossip attached to someone's past life as long-time co-workers of the now-deceased individual. Legacy conceptually is Sidereal first, any other Celestial Exalt second.<br /> <br /> Making it so those past details MUST BE &quot;X&quot; doesn't rehabilitate the Bronze voice. The two diverging PoVs, leading into later third PoV simply guarantees voice to everyone. To tell the absolute truth, if you gave me unrestricted fiat over continuity, I don't think I'd change what we have presently. As much as part of me might be tempted to turn the Bronze/Gold thing into a 50/50 deadlock, it wouldn't be good for Sidereals. I LIKE that the setting-point is so controversy-provoking it goes so far as to engender out-of-game emotion on the subject. If it can do that, it's certainly creating high drama and a deep well of potential conflict to drive story. Objective fact over what &quot;should&quot; have been done regarding the Solars is poison to the Sidereals conceptually.<br /> <br /> If you alter the setting-point so it can be objectively said &quot;The Bronze was right&quot; or &quot;The Gold was right&quot; ..how can that NOT weaken the splat? There are much better ways, and much better places to demonstrate the Bronze is not solely &quot;Better a horrible ending, than horrors without end&quot; with regard to the GC/Crusade than the setting point decision of reaction to the Usurpation. Changing the write-ups for canon Bronze members LEAPS to mind. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> You may not believe me, after other things I've said, but I am sympathetic to the notion of your wanting canon support for all the Bronze not being equated as the heartless calculating-machine Chejop's comic-appearances characterize the Bronze members as being. It IS heavy-handed, having Naro-O point-blank ask essentially &quot;So, knowing about the GC/Crusade, you still haven't for a moment reconsidered the Usurpation?&quot; and having Chejop Kejak not even need to think about it, not express really anything besides &quot;No, the Solars would have destroyed everything.&quot;<br /> <br /> I'm fine with <b>Chejop Kejak</b>, and maybe Anys Syn being hardassed like that...but giving the same treatment to Righteous Tsunami, who they call out as being the youngest Sidereal to Chair a Directional Committee (Water), by having Righteous Tsunami actually be the one who actually SPOKE the famous Bronze quote, yes, I see where you are coming from. More these are real people, not logic-bots simply tallying lives saved vs. lives lost, adds nuance, and I've been championing nuance for seven pages now. It'd be rather hypocritical to say Nuance is good for only the elements I sympathize with. How about some younger Bronze Sidereals who have actually had some negative experiences of their own with the returned Lawgivers? How about some Bronze members making the argument Abyssals and Infernals only show how easily Solars can be corrupted, and THAT is what makes them a danger?<br /> <br /> Taking away from the Gold, and I'm sorry, but I agree with Aquillion here, it is taking away from the Gold to say no reasonable Sidereal could have (and did) differ over what should be done concerning the ancient Solars, is not the way to positively alter the Bronze's image. Leaving the Original Issue open to the interpretation of individual Sidereals will always drive story better than a One True Result. I got an earful for using the term Objectively Evil, and everyone describes One True Build as bad mechanically. How can One Objective Truth be any different?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T04:37:26-07:00","id":1239625,"title":"","content":"Hmm.... There are a lot of things I disagree with here in the notions that the Bronze Faction chose an unjust society, that they are complacent about an unjust society, that a just society is not possible without the Solar Exalted - indeed I think it is possible for mortals without any contact with any Exalted whatsoever!<br /> <br /> I'm comfortable seeing the Bronze Faction as having unwillingly sacrificed a level of magical grandeur and material prosperity for a more secure survival (though they have to actually have that more secure survival be more likely than with the Solars to cash that cheque and be viable! So &quot;Well, it could be that loads of random threats pose more of a difficulty to Creation's survival than the Solars ever did&quot; is out).<br /> <br /> But I don't think it's a good idea to present them as people who are complacent about quality of human life, who have ceased striving for a more just society, and even ultimately the perfectly just society, rather than people who continue to strive in that direction within the greater material constraints of the limits of grandeur they accepted for more safety and survival.<br /> <br /> (Of course, I may be just running up against a contradiction of one of Exalted's core themes, which may be that a more just society is for some reason not possible without the Solar Exalted. In which case, I still don't believe it, but it may be more of a &quot;Exalted's setting no longer works for me&quot; thing, that something amendable within the confines of what Exalted's setting allows).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T12:31:26-07:00","id":1239757,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I am sympathetic to the notion of your wanting canon support for all the Bronze not being equated as the heartless calculating-machine Chejop's comic-appearances characterize the Bronze members as being</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br /> <br /> Your sympathy is not being extended to me; it is being given to a caricature of your own invention.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-28T16:59:09-07:00","id":1239843,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a><br /> You equated my saying the Bronze shouldn't be equated to heartless calculating-machines as a whole, because I consider the Chejop Kejak we've been given one, to believing I hold a caricatured viewpoint of the Faction? I thought I made a fairly compelling case for reasons why younger Bronze Faction members wouldn't be absolutist slabs of ideology. You're making the same mistake Epimetheus did. Just because my personal interpretation of the material inclines me against the Bronze Faction (primarily because I tend to prefer the dynamic over the status quo, if we're getting right down to it) doesn't mean I have either personal antipathy for the Bronze Faction material, or a lack of nuanced understanding of the subject.<br /> <br /> That's unfortunate, because I genuinely do believe 2E was entirely too monolithic in its presentation of the Bronze. While the canon Gold characters include a more varied set of circumstances in how those characters came to be Gold, the canon Bronze characters often default to &quot;Began Bronze, stayed Bronze.&quot; A conspicuous lack of any former Golds moving the other way, as an example. The Cult of the Illuminated book's inclusion of Sapphire Sparrow, as a Sidereal less interested in ardent loyalty to the Gold, and more interested in experiencing all the possible viewpoints a Sidereal can hold, or Lupo's initial push-back against Ayesha Uru &quot;rejoining&quot; the Gold, demonstrative of a Gold-hardliner. When Bronze Sidereals got written up, on the other hand, the write-ups tend to focus on their cultural/Direction-background, or circumstances that make them personally unusual, or on marked differences between their present and past incarnations. MoEP: Sidereals did nothing to correct the trend, consistently causing me to need to build my own Bronze antagonists. Mustache-twirling is beyond boring, and bored players don't remain one's players for long.<br /> <br /> The fact the Bronze Faction doesn't, in essence, &quot;get its own book&quot; is a prime example of &quot;What the Bronze is about is implicitly understood, the Faction doesn't need a higher-resolution focus on it&quot; for that matter.<br /> <br /> I thought that, no matter how ardently, or loudly, I might have championed any position, an underlying position of desiring nuance has been present in my posts all along. The fact I don't believe the setting-point made up of the Sidereal reaction to the problem of the ancient Solars collective and growing instability is the appropriate element to wield to change the Bronze voice in the manner you describe (demonstrating it's not inherently unreasonable to be Bronze, increased competence, and that they aren't obtuse). I felt that canon Bronze Sidereal write-ups would be a far more appropriate point at which to improve the Bronze Faction treatment, beyond the monolithic 2E canon characters.<br /> <br /> Do you disagree with this <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a> ? If so, why?<br /> <br /> I'm sorry I've given you the impression I approve of a lack of nuance in any element of the setting. I explicitly said that'd be hypocritical, and went ON to say I wouldn't change the Bronze/Gold division in favor of the Gold, even if given creative fiat.<br /> <br /> For the record, yes, I consider the Bronze Faction inherently antagonistic. The Realm is a negative force, for pretty much everyone except the Dynasts and their patricians. It's a major driver of both the slave and hard drug trade, to the point it competes with the Guild. Bronze top-down control of the Immaculate Order gives them incredible influence over the moral authority (such as it exists) over the power-possessing tiers (political and economic) of Dynastic society, but they don't make any concerted efforts to move that society beyond the inherently parasitic economic model the Realm embraces. Ultimately, they place a premium on social stability, and on quantity of mortal life over quality of mortal life. An initial NEED for expediency, has developed over time into a PREFERENCE for expedient solutions. Dragon Blooded spiritual superiority as a cornerstone of Realm society is an expedient means of making the Realm easier to wield, so they double-down on that position.<br /> <br /> Is there anything factually incorrect about the preceding paragraph?<br /> <br /> The problem with the Bronze Faction isn't mine, it's that Chejop Kejak as written IS a caricature. He does nothing but be defined by a single choice, and continue to take action to defend that choice. The Bronze Faction being considered synonymous with Chejop Kejak in its presentation isn't MY problem. It's a lack of nuance in any canon Bronze Sidereal given. If you want the Bronze Faction's voice to improve, the Faction needs a meaningful identity separate from a single character's agenda.<br /> <br /> The Bronze Faction will lack nuance so long as that synonymous-to-Chejop Kejak association remains the same. Having him be right at the end of his lifespan isn't a meaningful separation of his identity from the Faction, when there isn't a younger &quot;heir apparent&quot; to Bronze ideology with meaningful reasons for adhering to the positions they've taken.<br /> <br /> Also for the record, I dislike anticipated setting-point proclamations. Exhibit A: &quot;When Chejop Kejak dies, Ayesha Uru is expected to dominate Sidereal politics.&quot; Really? That's the only rational outcome of the Elder Sidereal's death? There's no outcome where the inner circle of Bronze adherents manage to hold millennia of dominance together? There's a lack of nuance I take issue with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-28-2018, 08:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-29T07:54:52-07:00","id":1240048,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1239843#post1239843\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar158_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"158\" data-vbnamecard=\"158\">Isator Levi</a><br /> For the record, yes, I consider the Bronze Faction inherently antagonistic. The Realm is a negative force, for pretty much everyone except the Dynasts and their patricians. It's a major driver of both the slave and hard drug trade, to the point it competes with the Guild. Bronze top-down control of the Immaculate Order gives them incredible influence over the moral authority (such as it exists) over the power-possessing tiers (political and economic) of Dynastic society, but they don't make any concerted efforts to move that society beyond the inherently parasitic economic model the Realm embraces. Ultimately, they place a premium on social stability, and on quantity of mortal life over quality of mortal life. An initial NEED for expediency, has developed over time into a PREFERENCE for expedient solutions. Dragon Blooded spiritual superiority as a cornerstone of Realm society is an expedient means of making the Realm easier to wield, so they double-down on that position.<br /> <br /> Is there anything factually incorrect about the preceding paragraph?</div> </div> </div> </div>Out of setting, or in setting, a dichotomy between quantity and quality of human life, doesn't make much sense as a dichotomy. The First Age sustained much larger populations than the Second Age; economic factors that increase population size also tend to increase quality of life and vice versa. <br /> <br /> (Like the point about the Goddess of Prostitution favouring the Second Age because people would sell themselves into prostitution. Doesn't make sense; Second Age has subsistence economies, and we know from actual real world economics that prostitution is more common in the monetized, cash rich, market economies that exist today than the subsistence economies that existed in the past. A plot point designed by someone with no knowledge of the economics of prostitution, no doubt. The goddess of prostitution should be on the side of the Gold Faction, if anything!).<br /> <br /> There's no evidence that the Bronze Faction particularly want or support for slavery to take place, frankly, or want people to develop drug addictions. They don't have absolute control over the world and work with the realities of power. The Immaculate Order, as wielded by the Bronze Faction, is probably in opposition to hard abuse, though not for the purest of reasons, and it's stance on slavery probably as ambiguous as real world religions (I would guess reformatory, as in Islamic prohibitions about the correct treatment of slaves, rather than abolitionist. Since the structure of the religion is about regulating hierarchies in moral ways, rather than abolishing them).<br /> <br /> There's not a lot of evidence that members of the Bronze Faction work to reform the character of Realm society, true, but there is certainly equally little evidence that they do not try to work to make it more productive, peaceful, stable, where there is opportunity to intervene.<br /> <br /> Also inconsistent with the earliest presentation of the Bronze Faction in the first book about Exalted, where deposing the Solars is an action intended by the Sidereals, at least in part, to remove the &quot;<i>vast atrocities and terrible indulgences</i>&quot;, tyranny and abuses of power over mortals by the Solar Exalted.<br /> <br /> Even in the Sidereals First Edition Book, the prompt for the Sidereals was: &quot;<i>The Solar Exalted, who ruled the Relam wisely and well for so long, became corrupt and complacent in their power. They turned from the worship of the gods and encouraged mortals to worship them instead. They abused their power, and perhaps worst of all, they ignored the advice and wisdom of their Sidereal counsellors, preferring to keep thier own counsel as to how the Realm should be administered. The Sidereals were told, in no uncertain terms to mind their own affairs and to leave the job of ruling the world to their betters. Deeply concerned by what they saw happening within the Realm and what they saw reflected in the weavings of fate, the Sidereal Exalted gathered together in secret for a grand conclave...</i>&quot;<br /> <br /> This is also the version where they reject the vision where the do nothing to stop the Solars, and the world has a guaranteed existence, but becomes a World of Darkness, as &quot;<i>the most terrible of all</i>&quot;, which rejects a view of them as the survive at all costs faction.<br /> <br /> The people who choose this path at some point become the ones who are indifferent to quality of human life? They may well have done, though I don't think this is supported, but this is the starting point they are working from in the setting history.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-31T17:24:34-07:00","id":1241086,"title":"","content":"Well, the very first time we're introduced to Chejop Kejak, he's musing about how addled the mind of the Mouth of Peace is by the many, many mind-control sorceries simultaneously at work upon her. I'd say 1E's notion was pretty ardently &quot;The Bronze Faction wields near-absolute control over the Immaculate Order.&quot; Chejop Kejak bargained its influence to the Scarlet Empress as if it were a personal possession of his, which could be used to legitimize her rule.<br /> <br /> As for the Bronze Faction's stance on slavery: The Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade had JUST ravaged Creation, prior to the rise of the Realm. In the real world, without ANY effort by social reformers with any measurable power, the Black Plague all but ended serfdom throughout Europe, because the value of the now-scarce farmers had risen accordingly. With the optics the Sidereals possess, I am CERTAIN a point arrived in their viewing of the Tapestry of Fate as it was woven, where Fate would proceed down either a path where the scarce peasantry's increased value would lead towards a yeoman-like development (IF the Realm relied upon the paid labor of free men and women), or where the Realm would choose to embrace slavery on a grand-scale (driving down the value of the individual labor of the free, and returning non-slaves to the role of serf-peasants) in exchange for the fastest economic recovery possible. Expediently, slavery was embraced, with all the resultant consequences. The Bronze Faction was there at the Realm's outset, with a vastly superior understanding of the consequences of present actions on the future. Right there, just as the Empress rose, they COULD have headed the Realm down a different course. Having their Immaculates purport that the keeping of slaves created such great spiritual contamination, it could overwhelm even the all-but-perfect spiritual purity of the Dragon Blooded. They chose not to, because it would've pissed the Empress off, and required them to expend effort mending fences with her.<br /> <br /> I'll admit the Cynis drug trade would've been much more difficult to deter, with the IO as the weapon of availability. Immaculates could scream all they wanted about the damage to the pathway of spiritual enlightenment caused by hard drugs, but furtive, covert drug trafficking is sadly pretty easy. All the more so when the State doesn't actually try to do more than tax said trafficking. That said, this is where control of the Tapestry could have been wielded. A few storms wrecking ships loaded with drugs headed for the Threshold could've sent the early Cynis in a very different direction economically. It wouldn't have been a hard-sell to persuade Ghataru to free up some elementals for a little seaborne mayhem, given his pro-Solar/anti-Realm leanings, and giving the venerable Shogun the opportunity to lash out at the Dragon Blooded might've even been something the Bronze could've parlayed into closer relations with the deity in control of the Aerial Legion.<br /> <br /> I vehemently disagree that rejection of the Vision of Darkness equates to rejection of Survive at all Costs. The Vision of Darkness ending in <i>&quot;...the world would drown in corruption, smothered by darkness&quot; </i>was intended to say that's how Creation ends. It describes the lead up to that, and then those words. It was the most terrible vision of all, because first Creation suffered immensely, then the Sidereals themselves were corrupted, and finally it ended in corruption and darkness.&quot; Rejecting the Vision of Darkness is therefore simply rejecting a more painful way for Creation to end, and says nothing about the Bronze's position on Creation surviving at all costs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 07-31-2018, 08:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-31T19:36:48-07:00","id":1241123,"title":"","content":"Just as econd, things posted when they shoudln't have.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-31T19:58:34-07:00","id":1241133,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, the very first time we're introduced to Chejop Kejak, he's musing about how addled the mind of the Mouth of Peace is by the many, many mind-control sorceries simultaneously at work upon her. I'd say 1E's notion was pretty ardently &quot;The Bronze Faction wields near-absolute control over the Immaculate Order.&quot; Chejop Kejak bargained its influence to the Scarlet Empress as if it were a personal possession of his, which could be used to legitimize her rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a bit where edition shifts are important. The Order seems in 3e to be a lot more autonomous this time around. Chejob probably still talks with the Mouth of Peace, but I think a lot of the original depcition of her was in a context of Celestial Exalted having a tier of power above Terrestrials that's not meant to be the case this time around. There's a lot more of Dragon-Blood autonomy in general through history this edition and Chejob is still thinking himself the shit but also the Empress and the Order's structure sitll has some comrpomises in it as a result.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the Bronze Faction's stance on slavery: The Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade had JUST ravaged Creation, prior to the rise of the Realm. In the real world, without ANY effort by social reformers with any measurable power, the Black Plague all but ended serfdom throughout Europe, because the value of the now-scarce farmers had risen accordingly. ...</div> </div> </div> </div>The Great Contagion is much more like the depeopling of the Americas than the Black Death I think. A big thing to remember is that the 9/10s lost there resulted in basically total economic collapse and ruin. The Black Death was surprisngly hlepful to Europe due to in part dumb luck. But the more similar to Creation event was very much an apocalypse that never was recovered from. Other comparable collapses include likely the Bronze Age Collapse or the period following the end of the Roman, Mauryan, and Han empires. The Black Death occurred in a time when Europe was an economic backwater globally and afterwards allowed them to participate in a pretty robust global economy. These other events fit the Contagion more in that they're a total collapse of major institutions or socieites and nothing really to fill in.<br /> <br /> It's also notable too that the rise of the merchant class and nascent capitalism arguably resulted in the rise of New World Slavery and European Colonialism, since there was also more money to go around and desire for some goods that those pracctices provided. So while yeah, serfdom ended, African chattel slavery practiced by Europeans kind fo also arguably rose form that too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... With the optics the Sidereals possess, I am CERTAIN a point arrived in their viewing of the Tapestry of Fate as it was woven, where Fate would proceed down either a path where the scarce peasantry's increased value would lead towards a yeoman-like development (IF the Realm relied upon the paid labor of free men and women), or where the Realm would choose to embrace slavery on a grand-scale (driving down the value of the individual labor of the free, and returning non-slaves to the role of serf-peasants) in exchange for the fastest economic recovery possible. ...</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a pretty big presumption actually. SIdereal prediciton isn't perfect and the economic forces that lead to slavery or paying folks is pretty fucking complex. It's an emergent, likely non-linearly determinable system like anything else economic. There's also all sorts of other factors probably in order that were more pressing, like prevent succession wars ala the Shogunate, rogue Lunar and Dragon-Blooded warlords in the Threshold, the geenral global ecnomic collapse that losing 9/10s your population cause (Again, think depeopling of the Americas or Bronze Age Collapse). Hell, as I note before, they're not even mutally exclusive, as arguably European relevance in the global markets due to increased indivdiual buying power caused the Atlantic Slave Trade to like...be.<br /> <br /> So you are kind of hitting three issues. The first is you are granting Sidereals predictive power via the Loom we are not really sure we can attribute to them. And even if we can, the butterfly effect on human economic behavior in a constantly revising non-determiend system like the Loom could predict this sort of macroecnomic activity on an empire the size of the Realm. And <i>even then</i>, that this is a concern the Sidereals have besides just keeping the lights on, metaphorically speaking.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">.... Expediently, slavery was embraced, with all the resultant consequences. The Bronze Faction was there at the Realm's outset, with a vastly superior understanding of the consequences of present actions on the future. Right there, just as the Empress rose, they COULD have headed the Realm down a different course. Having their Immaculates purport that the keeping of slaves created such great spiritual contamination, it could overwhelm even the all-but-perfect spiritual purity of the Dragon Blooded. They chose not to, because it would've pissed the Empress off, and required them to expend effort mending fences with her.</div> </div> </div> </div>You again, are assuming they had predictive power in that sense (The Great Prophecy was one-off) and that even then, it mattered in teh grand scheme when the world is still there and lessened. Life sucks for peasants. There is a world and lives for those peasants to have. And this is all assuming Sidereals could have just told the Empress, who at the time was an equal actor mostly and had her own plans, to do that. Or that even a cabal of people have that much power to personallys hape and guide an emergent state like the Realm when a lot of th epower is in a person who has her own agenda and not giving a shits.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll admit the Cynis drug trade would've been much more difficult to deter, with the IO as the weapon of availability. Immaculates could scream all they wanted about the damage to the pathway of spiritual enlightenment caused by hard drugs, but furtive, covert drug trafficking is sadly pretty easy. All the more so when the State doesn't actually try to do more than tax said trafficking. ...</div> </div> </div> </div>This exactly same logic applies to any major trade good, though. And in a world in need of labor and supplies, well, there's that. Drugs and slavery are also like, not the Realm's only major trade goods you know, right? They're an empire, the extract a shitload and push a lot more than just that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... That said, this is where control of the Tapestry could have been wielded. A few storms wrecking ships loaded with drugs headed for the Threshold could've sent the early Cynis in a very different direction economically. ...</div> </div> </div> </div>This again seems to assume a lot of the Sidereal's ability to, or giving a shit to, make the world the idealized post-colonial late 20th Century neo-liberal ecnomic setup. Especially when they are from a world that doesn't necessarily share our values, or priorities. And also their ability to know that like, <i>this kid is deifnitely going ot found a Great House</i> versus being another one of the Empress' kids who got rich. Sidereals are pretty good at forecasting the future, but not predicting it to the T. The thing about prophecies is they're kind of wiggly.<br /> <br /> And again, they have other issues like keeping demons out, fighting Rakan's bullshit, rogue Exigents, or other things. Creation exists, it goes on, is lessered withotu silver and gold.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It wouldn't have been a hard-sell to persuade Ghataru to free up some elementals for a little seaborne mayhem, given his pro-Solar/anti-Realm leanings, and giving the venerable Shogun the opportunity to lash out at the Dragon Blooded might've even been something the Bronze could've parlayed into closer relations with the deity in control of the Aerial Legion.</div> </div> </div> </div>By the time of the Empress, Solars had been mostly gone for centuries. By the rise of House Cynis, likely a millennium. Solars were never coming back, the Realm that ruled the world isn't who killed the Solars origianlly anyways.<br /> <br /> And you know, also 3e moving a bit away from I think DBs just being worst htan mortals in Heaven and gods thinking that stopping one society of them is sticking it to them, especially centuries already after they more or less conquered the world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I vehemently disagree that rejection of the Vision of Darkness equates to rejection of Survive at all Costs. The Vision of Darkness ending in <i>&quot;...the world would drown in corruption, smothered by darkness&quot; </i>was intended to say that's how Creation ends. It describes the lead up to that, and then those words. It was the most terrible vision of all, because first Creation suffered immensely, then the Sidereals themselves were corrupted, and finally it ended in corruption and darkness.&quot; Rejecting the Vision of Darkness is therefore simply rejecting a more painful way for Creation to end, and says nothing about the Bronze's position on Creation surviving at all costs.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's kind of moot since the Vision of Darkness kind of was avoided since something was done. It was the worst option, but well, it's the dodged one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-31T21:28:49-07:00","id":1241159,"title":"","content":"Well,<br /> The entire thread has been working off 1E determinations, because the lore-picture of Creation and Yu-Shan is by no means yet coherent in third. For 1E (since 1E has been described relentlessly as what third is hearkening back to) Sidereals absolutely DID have that kind of predictive power. Much of 1E's description of the Sidereals has them not only trouble-shooting Fate, but actively deciding those portions of Fate's shape not predetermined by one or more of the Maidens. The first place a Sidereal tried to change what the Elders of the Bronze Faction deemed an undesirable outcome was at the Loom itself. It was only when the event had already begun being &quot;processed&quot; into happening within Creation that the point of alteration shifted to boots on the ground.<br /> <br /> I don't dispute EX3 will change how Sidereals do things, but up until now, the Sidereal ability to predict events that didn't include a bunch of essence-usage was described as incredible. Chosen of Battles were assumed to know which side of a conflict was &quot;supposed&quot; to win. Chosen of Serenity were presumed to know when a princess was &quot;supposed&quot; to lose her virginity. Chosen of Endings presumed to know when a king's time to die had come. All of the planning related to value-judgments based on these givens, and the foreseeable problems which might arise. Everything that's been said in the thread to date precedes from a notion of superlative Sidereal optics on events.<br /> <br /> The very notion of Sidereal Astrology presumes an ability to alter destiny profoundly. Particularly when multiple powerful Sidereals collaborated on the same project. 2E weakened Sidereal Astrology, but in 1E, a half-dozen Essence 5 Sidereals, assisting an Essence 7 Bronze Elder could institute regime-change in a Threshold nation by Sidereal Astrology alone. It wouldn't happen as fast as sending a Chosen of Endings to simply kill the mortal ruler, but it would happen.<br /> <br /> I'm curious what level of predictive accuracy you think <b>should</b> be accorded to Sidereals? The basis of everything I was saying in the post quoted is predicated on Sidereal optics being excellent. Changing what's foreseen isn't as easy as foreseeing it, but determining exactly what's going to happen before it does is what they do. Down to the level of knowing Cart Driver X is supposed to be killed when his cart overturns the morning of Resplendent Wood 14th, RY 768, at one hour, three minutes past noon. A fate-error, where the driver is simultaneously alive and dead, when he's supposed to be dead, is a canon mission a Circle of Sidereals get sent to deal with.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","user":"Wyldwraith","date":". 08-01-2018, 12:31 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-01T07:53:15-07:00","id":1241305,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241159#post1241159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well,<br /> The entire thread has been working off 1E determinations, because the lore-picture of Creation and Yu-Shan is by no means yet coherent in third. For 1E (since 1E has been described relentlessly as what third is hearkening back to) Sidereals absolutely DID have that kind of predictive power. Much of 1E's description of the Sidereals has them not only trouble-shooting Fate, but actively deciding those portions of Fate's shape not predetermined by one or more of the Maidens. The first place a Sidereal tried to change what the Elders of the Bronze Faction deemed an undesirable outcome was at the Loom itself. It was only when the event had already begun being &quot;processed&quot; into happening within Creation that the point of alteration shifted to boots on the ground.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing as I gather is it's scatter-shot predicitve pwoer. It's often that it was through what was needed to know, what was important to keep things going and what ultimately was fungible for stability. It might very well be that the Realm being the slave-owning empire it is is what was the most stable path. It also just be not enough Sidereals gave a shit, because they are ultimately people in a largely high fantasy pulp setting and not 20th Century pro-democracy folks.<br /> <br /> My guess is that simply put, this might be something 3e moves away from since while it harkesn a lot ot 1e, it is willing to break where need be. We know for instance Sidereals <i>can't</i> trivially track Solar and Lunar Exaltations. Their power power to predict is strong, but it's not omnisicent and flawless. And there are some systems which are like weather and not really determinable beyond a point. The large scale economic activities of aglobal spanning empire ran by Exalts is probably not something you can make many predicitons of further than a week or two save in the very broadest, climat-elevel sense. &quot;The Realm might practice slavery&quot; is a broad prediction, but the details of when, how, why, or ways to prevent it are nuanced and complciated enough to be beyond singular prediction. Exalted is going back to 1e, sure, but it is also still being rooted in some pretty complex views of economics and human behavior.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241159#post1241159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well,I don't dispute EX3 will change how Sidereals do things, but up until now, the Sidereal ability to predict events that didn't include a bunch of essence-usage was described as incredible. Chosen of Battles were assumed to know which side of a conflict was &quot;supposed&quot; to win. Chosen of Serenity were presumed to know when a princess was &quot;supposed&quot; to lose her virginity. Chosen of Endings presumed to know when a king's time to die had come. All of the planning related to value-judgments based on these givens, and the foreseeable problems which might arise. Everything that's been said in the thread to date precedes from a notion of superlative Sidereal optics on events.</div> </div> </div> </div>Note though that things supposed to be the case isn't determiend to be the case. I always got a sense that these were generally forecasting, not prediction, prophecy, not dictation. Sidereals work through portents not declarations and so while something is supposed to happen, things often don't happen and it's because wht Heaven wants to happen isn't always what actually happens on the ground due to unconsidered factors. I think a lot of this is less even some outside force like Exalts and more folks have to work with models to understand predicitons and models often miss something. Sidereal prophecy is <i>accurate</i>, but that's not flawless.<br /> <br /> And again, the specific things you note? Not even accurate probably a lot save in the grossest sense that's impressive due to the data they have, but also probably still not useful save when relevant to story. \u201c...the best augurs are those who divine from the portents of the past. They compile phenomenal records.\u201d<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241159#post1241159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The very notion of Sidereal Astrology presumes an ability to alter destiny profoundly. Particularly when multiple powerful Sidereals collaborated on the same project. 2E weakened Sidereal Astrology, but in 1E, a half-dozen Essence 5 Sidereals, assisting an Essence 7 Bronze Elder could institute regime-change in a Threshold nation by Sidereal Astrology alone. It wouldn't happen as fast as sending a Chosen of Endings to simply kill the mortal ruler, but it would happen.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which again, if we're talking 3e is moot since we know here for sure astrology as a system is probably not working the same (it's probably mostly having a lot of effects rolled into Charms), Essence scaling works differently, and what htey do to fate in aggregate is as well. I think a thing to think is that if a power exists to make it so you don't have to do plot things, is it actually a good power? Likely instead of poking at the Loom as much we'll see more just well, enabling you to get in and kill the guy easier if it has some cascade eeffect you want.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241159#post1241159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious what level of predictive accuracy you think <b>should</b> be accorded to Sidereals? The basis of everything I was saying in the post quoted is predicated on Sidereal optics being excellent. Changing what's foreseen isn't as easy as foreseeing it, but determining exactly what's going to happen before it does is what they do. Down to the level of knowing Cart Driver X is supposed to be killed when his cart overturns the morning of Resplendent Wood 14th, RY 768, at one hour, three minutes past noon. A fate-error, where the driver is simultaneously alive and dead, when he's supposed to be dead, is a canon mission a Circle of Sidereals get sent to deal with.</div> </div> </div> </div>A big thing to remember with Fate errors is they're different from Destiny mismanagement. Random cart driver dying because of the events his day that caused his death is fate, and if he somehow lives due to like, gtetting speared by wagon axel but somehow delsn't die, a fate error occurred and the cascade is busted. If he just happens to not get run over and lived on his day, that's a problem for some planning ocmmittee that needs rectified wiht his deaht or things planned aroun dit. The first is a fray on reality, the latter is a management hedache which could have negative butterfly effect osnequences.<br /> <br /> Likely Sidereals have at best really good ability to portend what is going to be the fate of some object, given time and effort, but that prediciton shifts constantly like the weather. The future is determined by the current state of the present, and that state changes constantly. Note for instance this is why the Great Prophecy was described as a set of predicitons in categories, not actual Three Big Paths ala end of Mass Effect. Sidereals are probably still pretty good at this due to insights, but I doubt it's something that often expands much bigger.<br /> <br /> For Destiny, they probably have good records of what should be and how Fated events lead to that panning out and what happens when those don't line up. This is more administrative though for Sidreeals and fixing things before they cascade into stupid to lead to actual fate errors potentially happening is something that I think is part of what makes their storylines not just being a bunch of hyper-accurate prediciton shell games.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-01T18:36:24-07:00","id":1241613,"title":"","content":"With regard to slavery: I wasn't talking about altering the practice once the Realm was a &quot;globe spanning empire.&quot; I was talking about when the Realm hardly existed as yet. When the Blessed Isle was nothing but a (small) collection of mortals and the rare Dragon Blooded survivors, all shocked to still be alive. At THIS point in history, when the Empress was still coming to grips with the fact her writ essentially ran no further than she could fire the Sword of Creation without utterly wrecking the Blessed Isle, one hundred Celestial Exalts could have made a great deal of difference in the shape the Realm-to-be would take. There weren't complex multi-Directional trade influences at this time. There weren't powerful external economic drivers. At the time of the Empress's rise, there were basically three drivers of the Blessed Isle, the Empress, the several hundred Dragon Blooded who bowed their heads to her, and what was becoming the Immaculate Order. There wasn't even a Thousand Scales yet.<br /> <br /> It's disingenuous to say Chejop Kejak had concerns other than turning the Realm into a powerful, highly centralized, Imperialist society, from which power could be exerted into the Threshold to shape the Destiny of Creation as the Bronze Faction wanted it shaped. There is ZERO canon influence representing Chejop Kejak in ANY role as anything but a driven Utilitarian, whose overwhelming concern was making Creation's Fate into a) Something sustainable, and b) Something <b>controllable.</b><br /> <br /> I've said before, I truly believe EX3 needs to distance the Bronze Faction from this &quot;Creation's Survival at all Costs&quot; mindset. While ancient individuals like Chejop Kejak, and Anys Syn might be obsessed with the binary &quot;Creation Lives/Creation Dies&quot; because of their pre and mid-Usurpation experiences, that isn't a mindset likely to be shared by Sidereals who weren't even alive at a time when Creation as a whole was, or recently had been, threatened. Individuals like Sad Ivory would, for example, likely share a little of that (as someone born in the immediate aftermath of the Great Contagion), but anyone born say, a hundred years post-Crusade wouldn't have the same sensibilities. Not anymore than Gen-X and Millennials comprehend the getting-under-your-desk for nuclear drills specter of a Russian First Strike the 50's generation lived with.<br /> <br /> I will say this though: I was a tad discomfited you implied slavery is something you'd need to be a member of a democratically inclined modern society to consider a major evil. I think anyone with a functioning conscience would be able to at least understand that the forcible subjection of fellow sentients to lifetimes of uncompensated service, where the individual in question has certainly seen those slaves abused, if not sexually assaulted or killed for displeasing their owners, would be able to realize slavery is AN evil, even if their consideration of HOW evil it is differed markedly from our own.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-01T22:16:11-07:00","id":1241682,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241613#post1241613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With regard to slavery: I wasn't talking about altering the practice once the Realm was a &quot;globe spanning empire.&quot; I was talking about when the Realm hardly existed as yet. When the Blessed Isle was nothing but a (small) collection of mortals and the rare Dragon Blooded survivors, all shocked to still be alive. At THIS point in history, when the Empress was still coming to grips with the fact her writ essentially ran no further than she could fire the Sword of Creation without utterly wrecking the Blessed Isle, one hundred Celestial Exalts could have made a great deal of difference in the shape the Realm-to-be would take. There weren't complex multi-Directional trade influences at this time. There weren't powerful external economic drivers. At the time of the Empress's rise, there were basically three drivers of the Blessed Isle, the Empress, the several hundred Dragon Blooded who bowed their heads to her, and what was becoming the Immaculate Order. There wasn't even a Thousand Scales yet.</div> </div> </div> </div>The question ehre is whether the Sidereals give a shit enough about slavery to eliminate it here, when it existed in Creation before likely, and again, something that arises form complicated enough broad and complex socio-economic incentives and behaviors that in one end it happens and another don't due to indeterminate reasons that I wouldn't think it's something the Realm focused on beside syou know, helping this lady with the big gun and a bigger opportunity get shit together in general. Slavery in the Realm is an institution built over centuries. That it happened and shaped as it is is probalby just not something that's very predicatble.<br /> <br /> Again, slavery doesn't just happen overnight. It didn't on Earth and I doubt it'll do it in Creation.<br /> <br /> Note, the whole &quot;Using the Sword of Creation caused earthquakes&quot; was a 2eism to depower the Empress and generally introduce another &quot;DBs are just lesser&quot; thing. This wasn't in her 1e depicitons and isn't in her 3e one so far. She didn't use the sword more because she liked having people to rule via satrapies isntead of European-sytle colonializaiton. A palce where folks have a nic ecash crop to exploit them for is more to her use than something so blasted she has to build it forms scratch and the crop might be gone.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241613#post1241613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's disingenuous to say Chejop Kejak had concerns other than turning the Realm into a powerful, highly centralized, Imperialist society, from which power could be exerted into the Threshold to shape the Destiny of Creation as the Bronze Faction wanted it shaped. There is ZERO canon influence representing Chejop Kejak in ANY role as anything but a driven Utilitarian, whose overwhelming concern was making Creation's Fate into a) Something sustainable, and b) Something <b>controllable.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>This begs the question folks cared. People in power can be very callous for other long term goals and interests. Abolition took concerted will and effort form very brave and dedicated advocates. Often throughout history though they were drowned out by folks wiht vested interest in keeping things going and others who would rather focus on other stuff than that. And for Chejob, the Imperialist asshat conquering axial power seems to be for him, good and stable enough. And for a lot of folks growing up through the chaos fo the Shogunate and the Contagion, probably something they can be sold on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241613#post1241613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've said before, I truly believe EX3 needs to distance the Bronze Faction from this &quot;Creation's Survival at all Costs&quot; mindset. While ancient individuals like Chejop Kejak, and Anys Syn might be obsessed with the binary &quot;Creation Lives/Creation Dies&quot; because of their pre and mid-Usurpation experiences, that isn't a mindset likely to be shared by Sidereals who weren't even alive at a time when Creation as a whole was, or recently had been, threatened. Individuals like Sad Ivory would, for example, likely share a little of that (as someone born in the immediate aftermath of the Great Contagion), but anyone born say, a hundred years post-Crusade wouldn't have the same sensibilities. Not anymore than Gen-X and Millennials comprehend the getting-under-your-desk for nuclear drills specter of a Russian First Strike the 50's generation lived with.</div> </div> </div> </div>Generations tend to progress and change much less historically tahn the cohorts we have described these days. Note that for many cnetuires, the Realm was the sole global superpower. So like many do with the US today, folks will hold their nose at the exploitations comitted by it to get something out of it, and crimes of the past being redressed is easier said than done in context of other short and long term itnerests. And many Sidereals since the Contagion have had other shit to worry about, like Lunars, Rakan Thulio, Fair Folk, and whatever else Sidereals deal with which often cause agents of government agencies to often become accomplices to the hegemony.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241613#post1241613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I will say this though: I was a tad discomfited you implied slavery is something you'd need to be a member of a democratically inclined modern society to consider a major evil. I think anyone with a functioning conscience would be able to at least understand that the forcible subjection of fellow sentients to lifetimes of uncompensated service, where the individual in question has certainly seen those slaves abused, if not sexually assaulted or killed for displeasing their owners, would be able to realize slavery is AN evil, even if their consideration of HOW evil it is differed markedly from our own.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not a moral relativist if this is what you're tyring to accuse me of. Slavery ahs been identified as wrong, and morally abhorent for centuries. It was effectively dead, even, in Medeival Europe as we understand it in part due to the desires of the church.<br /> <br /> I just think that modern industiralized and liberalized democracy has a tendency to create an environment where slavery is not just ethically abhorent, but ecnomically detrimental long term. Many situations where slavery became prominent is becuae of a perceived (real or no) value in labor theft. And I think that we in the West are actually still pretty complcit with it than before, just give folks enough caveats to make it look like fair labor and they seem fine with it.<br /> <br /> Basically, people have (rightly) seen slavery as abhorent as long as htere is slavery. The ability for folks in power to be forced to stop doing it though, is much more recent.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-01T23:44:51-07:00","id":1241708,"title":"","content":"Its not like Chejop had that much pull with Her Redness when she was starting the Realm. I mean even when 2nd was doing its whole thing of somehow the only factions that weren't directly involved in her getting the weapon were the ones that didn't exist at the time the Sids only got involved by forcing the a destiny into existence that had a chance to save the world. So, its not like Kejack could have made demands with a reasonable expectation of her listening. The Empire was founded when her Redness started taking shots at Rhaksha during the Balorean Crusade with a doomsday weapon and made her little announcement of, &quot;Hi, I'm the Empress of well pretty much the world now you can be under my protection or on my list of people to fuck up once I'm sure the world won't end next Tuesday.&quot; <br /> <br /> The offer he made when he showed up and offered the assistance of the Sidereal and their work to making the Immaculate Order a major asset to her empire. That wasn't, &quot;Hey we'll make your little empire thing a global power, these are the assets we'll be willing to use and put at your disposal to do so, you know so long as you help with some of the things we want done.&quot; He was approaching a fledgling super power that was going to become global one way or the other and offered tools and allies in exchange for cooperation as opposed to trying to have her and her allies killed when they became problems and needing to be even more quiet when they manipulated her empire from the shadows. He certainly wasn't in a position where he could go, &quot;Also slavery is bad stuff, I want it stopped.&quot; With any expectations that she'd do so unless he could give some sort of proof that it would be potentially catastrophic or apocalyptic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T03:33:51-07:00","id":1241744,"title":"","content":"The Realm\u2019s slave trade might have been another best of a bad situation deals. House Cynis is pretty bad but the slave trading arm of the Guild is even worse because their business model involves feeding people to the Fair Folk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12844-bladewind","name":"Bladewind","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T04:32:17-07:00","id":1241756,"title":"","content":"I think reading the beginning of the Realm as a blank slate that the Sidereal Exalted were free to shape however they want is quite reductive, both in terms of their actual powers*, the degree to which they can affect the Terrestrial Exalted (all of those Shogunate influences don't just disappear entirely, even if a lot of the structure has been shattered), and political expediencies.<br /> <br /> The original narrative of the Realm was one in which the Scarlet Empress wound up in a position of authority over a large number of Dragon Blooded in a world that was still recovering and vulnerable, and terrified that they would all resume tearing one another apart.<br /> <br /> I don't subscribe to the belief that authoritarianism is a necessity in the face of disaster, but in assessment of fictional characters, I'm willing to grant that they fallibly believe in such as a basis for their motivations beyond being objectively evil.<br /> <br /> <br /> * People always always always bring in some variation of the term &quot;lots of Celestial Exalted&quot; with Sidereals, as if to imply that they should have the same kind of impact as Solars, when that's not what they're for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T06:29:48-07:00","id":1241797,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241756#post1241756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think reading the beginning of the Realm as a blank slate that the Sidereal Exalted were free to shape however they want is quite reductive, both in terms of their actual powers*, the degree to which they can affect the Terrestrial Exalted (all of those Shogunate influences don't just disappear entirely, even if a lot of the structure has been shattered), and political expediencies.<br /> <br /> The original narrative of the Realm was one in which the Scarlet Empress wound up in a position of authority over a large number of Dragon Blooded in a world that was still recovering and vulnerable, and terrified that they would all resume tearing one another apart.</div> </div> </div> </div>And again, predicting its long term behavior or what she wants for it is easier said than done, since the institutions that would work or last were things centuries off. Great Houses weren't even a thing for a few centuries,a nd for all we know, the Realm's take on salvery is an extension of some Shogunate Era practice. The context of how Dynastic slavery developed is not clear enough to say &quot;Chejob and crew could have fixed it.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241756#post1241756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't subscribe to the belief that authoritarianism is a necessity in the face of disaster, but in assessment of fictional characters, I'm willing to grant that they fallibly believe in such as a basis for their motivations beyond being objectively evil.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very true here. Like, there's the stereotyping Chejob as a hyper-Utilitaraina nd then tehre's him litereally coming out of a total system collapse crisis. There's a lot of specuation of what oflks think in that, and also how complacent folks are to stable status quos.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241756#post1241756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">* People always always always bring in some variation of the term &quot;lots of Celestial Exalted&quot; with Sidereals, as if to imply that they should have the same kind of impact as Solars, when that's not what they're for.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it's also said in a way to erase Terrestrial Exalted as significant factors in dictating things as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T07:25:52-07:00","id":1241812,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I certainly won't vouch for the version of Creation in which Dragon-Blooded are entirely incidental to <i>their world-spanning empire</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T09:38:25-07:00","id":1241883,"title":"","content":"I'd also point out that slavery was abolished... for Realm citizens.<br /> <br /> Realm citizens cannot be traded or sold by other Realm citizens. Nor can they even own slaves.* So it's quite possible that there was some kind of abolitionist idea in the early Realm, but as they grew, this got watered down.<br /> Kind of like how slavery disappeared in England because Norman priests thought it was wrong, but then later the English went &quot;well, other countries are making loads of money from slave-trading, and we don't want to lag behind... obviously enslaving civilised people like us is wrong, but enslaving Native Americans or Africans is okay.&quot;**<br /> <br /> *Only the state, in the form of the Empress, can own slaves. But then you get the thing where people can rent slaves from the Empress. And then, when people hire work-gangs for decades at a time, it becomes de-facto ownership.<br /> **I'm simplifying for the sake of the argument, of course.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 08-02-2018, 12:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T10:27:04-07:00","id":1241905,"title":"","content":"Creation is a place where slaves have always been a thing. Humans started as slaves. They then enslaved other races and people to work for them. The cerulean lute deals with the fate of slaves as well implying that it still happened during the first age when the solar deliberative enslaved the jadeborn and other beings that threatened them. It still happens everywhere in creation, not just the realm. It's an issue that dates to prehistory. So I would imagine it's so engrained in creation it would take a lot to remove it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T13:04:16-07:00","id":1242005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, the very first time we're introduced to Chejop Kejak, he's musing about how addled the mind of the Mouth of Peace is by the many, many mind-control sorceries simultaneously at work upon her. I'd say 1E's notion was pretty ardently &quot;The Bronze Faction wields near-absolute control over the Immaculate Order.&quot; Chejop Kejak bargained its influence to the Scarlet Empress as if it were a personal possession of his, which could be used to legitimize her rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like other have folks said, it's less that Chejop Kejak didn't have the power to make The Mouthpiece say whatever he wanted, but that they didn't have the power to avoid consequences, should they, say, decide to make her leap up and down, squawk like a parrot and declare the Dragonblooded Anathema.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the Bronze Faction's stance on slavery: The Great Contagion and Balorian Crusade had JUST ravaged Creation, prior to the rise of the Realm. In the real world, without ANY effort by social reformers with any measurable power, the Black Plague all but ended serfdom throughout Europe, because the value of the now-scarce farmers had risen accordingly. With the optics the Sidereals possess, I am CERTAIN a point arrived in their viewing of the Tapestry of Fate as it was woven, where Fate would proceed down either a path where the scarce peasantry's increased value would lead towards a yeoman-like development (IF the Realm relied upon the paid labor of free men and women), or where the Realm would choose to embrace slavery on a grand-scale (driving down the value of the individual labor of the free, and returning non-slaves to the role of serf-peasants) in exchange for the fastest economic recovery possible.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not sure about the social history, but... the Realm doesn't have agricultural slavery plantations really, or much like serfdom I don't think. Slaves are employed in &quot;<i>the grunt work of mining and construction to the relatively more pleasant tasks of attending lords and Dynasts</i>&quot;. Disease epidemics don't inevitably end slavery or serfdom either - why doesn't the bubonic plague end Islamic slavery or the Plague of Justinian end slavery in the Eastern Roman Empire?<br /> <br /> Though to give some context on the Realm as a slave society, in the Southern US prior to abolition in 1860, you have about 30% of the society as slaves. In the Realm, the figure is (quoting 1e again) &quot;<i>- about 1 in 50 people in the Realm is a slave</i>&quot;, so 2% (over an order of magnitude lesser).<br /> <br /> (Further, and this is an obscure point, IRL not sure there's much evidence of persistent wage or subsistence differences between societies at the low prevalence of slavery (something like the Realm's 2%), or societies in which they were absent. May not even be the case for much higher % slave societies; e.g. the Roman Empire's wage levels are not dispositive to this idea. Malthus rules OK!)<br /> <br /> As Epimetheus rightly says as well, slavery likely reaches back into the First Age as well, in most of the canon (can't remember how it was dealt with in the more modern-utopia version of the First Age from 2e?).<br /> <br /> It's less clear how favourably, or disfavourably, the Realm compares to states outside the Realm's aegis in the relative prevalence of slavery. (Like, the slave markets of Nexus aren't there because of Bronze Faction Sidereal choices?).<br /> <br /> But let's also take the idea you presented of how the Bronze Sidereals *could* have forestalled slavery:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1241086#post1241086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having their Immaculates purport that the keeping of slaves created such great spiritual contamination, it could overwhelm even the all-but-perfect spiritual purity of the Dragon Blooded. They chose not to, because it would've pissed the Empress off, and required them to expend effort mending fences with her.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, what kind of consequences do you think there could from of labelling a class of humans spiritually contaminating? And note that the Realm does have a class of people labelled worse off than slaves - the Disenfranchised, a group who can be subject to violence at will without consequences from the majority of the population.<br /> <br /> I don't point this last one out to be overly confrontational or knock you, but just as an indication of how I think you may be understating unintended consequences that might spin out from what you see as more effortful but otherwise obviously and straightforwardly better choices. (For PCs with a cooperative ST who wants to enable them, sure they can be in your game! But I don't see much benefit in interpreting the setting in general that way.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T13:19:57-07:00","id":1242023,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242005#post1242005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Like other have folks said, it's less that Chejop Kejak didn't have the power to make The Mouthpiece say whatever he wanted, but that they didn't have the power to avoid consequences, should they, say, decide to make her leap up and down, squawk like a parrot and declare the Dragonblooded Anathema.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Not sure about the social history, but... the Realm doesn't have agricultural slavery plantations really, or much like serfdom</div> </div> </div> </div>I think most people in the Realm are essentially serfs. I'd have to check, but I don't think Realm peasants are allowed to just go &quot;Being a farmer's rubbish, I'm moving to the city.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T13:23:47-07:00","id":1242026,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242023#post1242023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think most people in the Realm are essentially serfs. I'd have to check, but I don't think Realm peasants are allowed to just go &quot;Being a farmer's rubbish, I'm moving to the city.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably they don't, but they did not much do that in medieval China or Japan (or really most of the world where low agricultural productivity means relatively huge agricultural populations had to persist until quite recently) and I believe most specialists don't class those as economies with serfdom!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T13:28:29-07:00","id":1242029,"title":"","content":"What do you define as serfdom? <br /> <br /> If you really get into the history, you start finding that serfdom is a very vague term describing a variety of legal positions (in England we had multiple types of peasants with a variety of grades of freedom and responsibilities) and then you start running into &quot;there's no such thing as Feudalism/Serfdom/the Dark Ages.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T14:15:59-07:00","id":1242052,"title":"","content":"Hmm... Serfdom in general, very approximately, some kind of legal restrictions binding a person to service on the land. Legal bondage to the land. <br /> <br /> Rather than the situation for most agrarian peasants where you *could* go to the city... but:<br /> <br /> You'll leave the only wealth you have (in the form of a smallholding) behind and if you have any running debts (and agrarian life is full of mutual obligation and communalism and &quot;credit&quot;) it won't be good for your remaining family for you to walk out on them. You probably don't have a lot of urban artisan skills, life as a unskilled wage laborer is tough, and even the cheapest calories (likely some form of porridge) are more expensive in the city than the country and you're more likely to starve to death than in the country, even as a skilled worker. And let's talk about the cities' greater tendencies to high murder rates and disease as well...<br /> <br /> I mean, in reality, for most pre-modern societies there were societies that restrained by law the freedom for agrarian workers to leave the land they were born on, or migrate to cities or towns. But even those that did not, faced pretty major constraints in how possible it even was for significant amounts of the population to move or become a non-agrarian worker, so had an enduring character of a large peasantry. <br /> <br /> (Not sure which of these characterises the Realm best! As best I can remember, the Immaculates frown somewhat on too much social mobility, but I can't recall much in the way of legal bondage to land.)<br /> <br /> Societies that overcame these limitations often did so by combinations of good fortune and clever engineering (e.g. watching a great documentary on Angkor recently) that boosted agricultural productivity or trade (so local surpluses when achieved could be traded). Even powerful rulers who ruled huge empires, without those factors could only centralise smaller towns and cities into grand capitals, not really achieve a lasting reduction in the agrarian share of the population and an expansion in the urban share and non-agrarian share (the Persian Empires probably had vaster capitals than any city-state of classical Greece... but the degree to which the % non-agrarian of the population was greater is doubtful). And usually when these factors did work, when people did produce enough food to outrun the limitations, there was a time limit on how long they boosted non-agrarian share for, and a return to the old days.<br /> <br /> I think as moderns, we can overestimate the universality of the tendency to which historically people were kept in agrarian situations because of oppresive legal forces. After all, city life is great, and there's plenty of opportunity and extra wealth,right? So why wouldn't you move? But this is not the case through much of human history; the technologies and economic and institutional changes to allow this to happen were huge (and arguably remain precarious to a degree today).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-02T14:52:50-07:00","id":1242074,"title":"","content":"Manacle and Coin noted that the process of the Great Houses taking increasing control of the peasantry amounted to reducing them to serfdom, so one could see that there's at least a distinction in character between where they've been and where they're going. <br /> <br /> My read on the situation of the Realm's peasantry is that they have obligations to the state representatives, including needing permission for travel, but are otherwise relatively autonomous, and have concerns about the possibilities of being tied in fiscal responsibilities to the local Dynasts.<br /> <br /> Mind, European serfdom came in several forms and with varying nuances, and was not universally a terrible situation. You got Medieval peasants who would opt for serfdom over being Freedmen, because it could carry certain economic stability and responsibilities from the landlord. I would say that the Realm situation is more concerning because of the specifics of how Dynasts would relate to peasants that they have a more direct control over.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T05:18:10-07:00","id":1242305,"title":"","content":"Also when we talk about slavery in the Realm. In previous editions it was pretty clear that slavery doesn't exist in the Age of Sorrows because society went Mad Max after the Usurpation or the Great Contagion, it existed because <i>it never went away</i> even when the world was ruled by people who could build robot armies and alter reality to their will.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T05:23:28-07:00","id":1242309,"title":"","content":"Just finished reading Third:<br /> Realm peasants are serfs. They require documented permission to leave their area of residence. Most, if not all, accepted definitions of the term include an inability to range away from the place where you labor for the social strata above you.<br /> <br /> As for the &quot;stereotyping of Chejop Kejak as a hyper-Utilitarian&quot; I can honestly say I can't think of ONE bit of material that actually cites his thoughts or spoken words that doesn't characterize that NPC as a moral utilitarian. This may well be in the process of changing, but so far as 1E/2E goes, I am genuinely curious where I could look to find a counter-example on the character's behavior. Everything I've ever read has him doing the whole &quot;I regret and reconsider nothing, this was the way it had to be&quot; thing. Anyone who read the fluff-piece where he's drunk and talking to Nara-O can definitely be forgiven for thinking the character a Utilitarian, IMHO.<br /> <br /> So far as the Realm goes, it doesn't seem to me there is ANY canon evidence the Sidereals made any effort whatsoever to rein in the more negative practices of said state. I'm certainly open to views to the contrary, though. From my perspective, I've noticed there's a certain tendency to sort of view the Realm's excesses as solely the responsibility of the Dynasts, and I don't see how the Bronze Faction can simultaneously be pulling all these strings, and yet not be at a minimum indifferent to the negative things the Dynasty does.<br /> <br /> I did notice there is mention of initial dissent among the Sidereals regarding the Usurpation, early in the third Core.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T06:13:57-07:00","id":1242325,"title":"","content":"I want to remind people of the fact that I, personally, never said that there was no dissent, but that I read it as a standing agreement that the dissenters would still go along with the will of the majority. If Chejop had been in the minority side, he would be right there with his fellows trying to get the Solars back on the straight and narrow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T06:16:37-07:00","id":1242326,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242305#post1242305\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">it existed because <i>it never went away</i> even when the world was ruled by people who could build robot armies and alter reality to their will.</div> </div> </div> </div>That does raise questions about what kind of slavery they had, though. Large workforces of people in permanent bondage are a different thing than a system of personally bonded retainers to specific kinds of individuals. An itinerant society of small communities having a system of slaves in the form of a few bonded household servants is not the same thing as the trans-Atlantic trade.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T06:22:29-07:00","id":1242331,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242309#post1242309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just finished reading Third:<br /> Realm peasants are serfs. They require documented permission to leave their area of residence. Most, if not all, accepted definitions of the term include an inability to range away from the place where you labor for the social strata above you.</div> </div> </div> </div>Serfdom though as noted is such a multi-variant term that there are probably ones that do or do not capture Realm peasantry and why they are not called that. Serfdom for example has a sharecropping element that I'm not so sure the Realm has, and it appears that while peasants need permission to move, they can't be arbitrarily moved either at times.<br /> <br /> This might all be cleared up in <b>The Realm</b> mind. But I think Realm peasantry arne't serfs by the same definitions that Japanese or Chiense peasants weren't often called that. THey might very well still be based on defintiions,b ut the defintiions are so elastic as to make it kind of difficult to be of much use categorically save I think in context of saying when a country usually started or stopped being feudal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242309#post1242309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the &quot;stereotyping of Chejop Kejak as a hyper-Utilitarian&quot; I can honestly say I can't think of ONE bit of material that actually cites his thoughts or spoken words that doesn't characterize that NPC as a moral utilitarian. This may well be in the process of changing, but so far as 1E/2E goes, I am genuinely curious where I could look to find a counter-example on the character's behavior. Everything I've ever read has him doing the whole &quot;I regret and reconsider nothing, this was the way it had to be&quot; thing. Anyone who read the fluff-piece where he's drunk and talking to Nara-O can definitely be forgiven for thinking the character a Utilitarian, IMHO.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sort of confused what your stance is here. Earlier stuff mentioned you having a problem with him being a to-the-hilt utilitarain. You're saying this now, and then saying he's not in teh last sentence, so it's not clear what you are getting at?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242309#post1242309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So far as the Realm goes, it doesn't seem to me there is ANY canon evidence the Sidereals made any effort whatsoever to rein in the more negative practices of said state. I'm certainly open to views to the contrary, though. From my perspective, I've noticed there's a certain tendency to sort of view the Realm's excesses as solely the responsibility of the Dynasts, and I don't see how the Bronze Faction can simultaneously be pulling all these strings, and yet not be at a minimum indifferent to the negative things the Dynasty does.</div> </div> </div> </div>Bronze Sidereals support but don't control the Realm. <i>The Empress</i> controlled the Realm. There's also the fact that again, Sidereals bieng in power doesn't mean they have a post-colonial 20th-21st Century view of how to utilize that power. As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar7_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"7\" data-vbnamecard=\"7\">Lioness</a> said, the institution of slavery and the exploits of imperialism predate the Realm in Creation. A big issue with slavery in human history is that a lot of folks recognized it was bad but very few folks actually gave enough a shit to do anything about it, with many thinking that it'll just sort itself out anyhow (this is what the original view of it before the rise of cotton in the South was in the US by some accounts but you know, cotton).<br /> <br /> And you seem to think that the current state of affairs being bad as it is is not as bad as it coudl be. The Bronze Faction helped expand the Immaculate <i>Philosophy </i>explicitly to curtail some of the excesses of the Dyansty. But in the end, while Sidereals are powerful, they aren't on the ground running the government and they can't go and dicatet eh policy of a superpower ran by thousands of demigods.<br /> <br /> The Bronze just doesn't have all the strings. Sidereals are co-conspirators in teh government, not the secret masters, with regards to the Realm. Think like, CIA advisors in totalitarian regimes more than secret kings here. Despite the protests of conspiracy theoriests, secret cabals have a hard time actually directly controlling policy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242309#post1242309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I did notice there is mention of initial dissent among the Sidereals regarding the Usurpation, early in the third Core.</div> </div> </div> </div>Whether they actually interfered with it though is not said. Disagreeing and then goign along with things anyhow because there's no Option C is not uncommon in human history or life.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T17:01:59-07:00","id":1242529,"title":"","content":"I wasn't saying that Chejop Kejak isn't a totalitarian moral utilitarian. In the passage you quoted, I was referencing the LACK of counter-examples in the character's known canon behavior. I was also allowing for the possibility such a counter-example might well exist, that I am not aware of, and suggesting if anyone knew of one, I would be extremely interested in such as a broadening of my understanding of the character. A character who has a significant, yet rarely expressed, view is fundamentally different from one who has never offered such a view. Everything *I* know of the character Chejop Kejak is indicative of his considering the GC &amp; BC extremely regrettable, but ultimately preferable, to continued Solar rule...and personally, I'm giving the character the benefit of the doubt on his considering the GC &amp; BC extremely regrettable, because there ARE inferences one could draw to the contrary from the character's cited statements. I tend to think no one except Deathlords/Neverborn, and some of the hardcore true-believing, outright Oblivion-worshiping DKs could look at either catastrophe and not feel SOME level of horror.<br /> <br /> As for Sidereal influence over the Realm, I tend to believe they COULD have done more to rein in what would later become all-but-unstoppable cultural and economic forces, but early on, the focus was very much on whipping the Realm into the centralized power required to rapidly reconstitute the Wyld Hunt and assist the Realm in sinking its hooks into three of four Directions before humanity got back on its feet.<br /> <br /> Oh, and you guys REALLY preferred a Sword of Creation that operates optimally for a Terrestrial Exalted, to one she can get to work, but with consequences? Why? How does that improve the setting, in other than a &quot;DBs RULE!&quot; kind of way? If the Empress could just fire the Realm Defense Grid however she liked, as she liked, there's no reason for her NOT to have fired it at any target she wasn't going to get anything out of economically, but might pose a threat to Realm interests. The Skullstone Archipelago was a piracy problem long before the Silver Prince reemerged, as a shadowland it's essentially worthless as something to conquer, and obliterating it would likely go a long way toward reminding Coral whose boss. Firing on the exceptional conglomeration of Lookshy/Nexus/Confederation forces marching against the Thorns forces pinning the rest of the Confederation at Mishaka could have broken the East forever. Especially since the Lookshy forces had brought out a significant portion of their First Age goodies. Losing at Mishaka was a FAR more severe blow to Imperial power than even losing the Tepet/Linowan/Talinin/Roka-Jin forces would later be, after her disappearance. Limiting the Sword when operated by a non-Celestial was, IMHO, one of the best setting-point decisions 2E made. Someone as utterly ruthless as the Empress required there be a material reason the Sword didn't become the answer to thorny military problems.<br /> <br /> Since it's been mentioned several times though, how do you guys envision the Sidereal capacity for perceiving what's going on/will go on in Creation with the Loom in EX3? Specifically, stuff like tracking individuals by use of essence, determining significant events in area X in say, the next year, and with what level of precision locating and identifying damaging Out of Fate (or whatever demons/Fair Folk will be with regards to destiny now) beings?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T17:30:10-07:00","id":1242541,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242331#post1242331\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Serfdom though as noted is such a multi-variant term that there are probably ones that do or do not capture Realm peasantry and why they are not called that. Serfdom for example has a sharecropping element that I'm not so sure the Realm has, and it appears that while peasants need permission to move, they can't be arbitrarily moved either at times.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the Realm has just as much capacity to arbitrarily move peasants as the decentralised, poorly controlled states of medieval Europe.<br /> <br /> Also, serfs didn't originally use sharecropping. That was brought in later to replace the original system of having a set amount of days a year they had to come work for the Lord. But that was actually a rubbish economic system (obviously the serfs tended not to work very hard on the Lord's land, not to mention all the times of the year where they weren't working on it), so overtime it was replaced by sharecropping, which is essentially just a tax.<br /> <br /> (Some historians do describe China and Japan, among others, as having serfs. Others say that's completely misleading. It's a controversial thing, but ultimately one that's simply semantic)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This might all be cleared up in <b>The Realm</b> mind. But I think Realm peasantry arne't serfs by the same definitions that Japanese or Chiense peasants weren't often called that. THey might very well still be based on defintiions,b ut the defintiions are so elastic as to make it kind of difficult to be of much use categorically </div> </div> </div>This is true, I'll admit.<br /> What I was trying to say, when responding to &quot;I don't think the Realm has serfs&quot; with &quot;the peasants are serfs&quot; is that they are a form of unfree labour. They have to pay tax to the House or Dynast (or Patrician) that owns their land, and can't sell it themselves. They also have other legal restrictions on them, designed to keep the peasantry in place.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ave I think in context of saying when a country usually started or stopped being feudal. </div> </div> </div>Honestly, it tends to fade away, rather than being abolished (with exceptions like 19th century Russia), and &quot;feudal&quot; is a vague label anyway.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T17:33:02-07:00","id":1242542,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242529#post1242529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since it's been mentioned several times though, how do you guys envision the Sidereal capacity for perceiving what's going on/will go on in Creation with the Loom in EX3? Specifically, stuff like tracking individuals by use of essence, determining significant events in area X in say, the next year, and with what level of precision locating and identifying damaging Out of Fate (or whatever demons/Fair Folk will be with regards to destiny now) beings?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I'd prefer that it mostly be done though charms... but then, they obviously do use the Loom, so it shouldn't be entirely out. I would like a reasonably clear explanation of what they do with and what its limits are though, which I do think the previous book lacked.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-03T18:02:22-07:00","id":1242553,"title":"","content":"I'm really hoping the entire idea of Individual Essence-Tracking gets abolished, to tell the truth. With all the new types of Exalts, and particularly since a not-insignificant fraction of Exigents are as powerful essence-use-wise as Celestial Exalts, they'd need to INCREASE the resolution of Sidereal essence-monitoring to make it viable. Ie: Not only could they pinpoint spikes of essence-usage affecting threads #311-365, but ALSO somehow differentiate Exigents from Solars/Lunars/DKs.<br /> <br /> I liked the mention of a star flaring as a Solar is Exalted. The idea the Exaltation-event is something which can be identified-tracked-pinpointed ties in nicely with the way Wyld Hunts seem to descend in short order upon new Exalts, and the retained Lunar concern for rescuing their newbies. The notion of Sidereals pulling a file, and reading &quot;Harmonious Jade in on her way back to Nexus. If the bandits who attempt to ambush her two days shy of the Exile-Boundary don't shoot her horse out from under her (Reference Attached File #22-b for Probabilities of Mount Survival), she'll reach the city gates around noon of Descending Fire 11th&quot; makes me tired. (Really wasn't in love with 2E's notion of every Exalt having an A-Z life-path file, and that the Factions fought over sealing them from each other.)<br /> <br /> Something like Portent-Omen observation could be interesting, but desired visual resolution becomes a balancing issue to keep it from turning back into Exalt-GPS.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-04T04:05:40-07:00","id":1242677,"title":"","content":"Sorry for the double, but noticed something reading Arms of the Chosen just now, that in conjunction with what I read in the Core seems interesting:<br /> So far, I've noticed *five* mentions of what I would deem &quot;experienced Sidereals&quot; (they got sent to do something very important in Creation, example: In two cases, checking to see if Warstriders in old tombs were still there), plus two more who disappeared (one of which it says is presumed dead) that have gone missing between 763-768. Add that to the missing one from the Core.<br /> <br /> Do you think that's meant to imply anything about the current setting-climate, or just off-the-cuff fluff?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-04T04:38:36-07:00","id":1242685,"title":"","content":"It could be something to do with Getimians.<br /> <br /> On essence-tracking... I always felt the most appropriate thing to fit with the rest of the fluff would be, similar to what you were saying, that the Sidereals are alerted by astrological signs when and where a Celestial (of any type) is Exalted. And... that's it.<br /> <br /> So, when a new Solar or Lunar exalts, they can tell the Wyld Hunt that &quot;a Lunar exalted in the village of Gan, 17 miles east of Azure,&quot; but the Wyld Hunt will actually have to go there, investigate, and track down the Celestial (who probably left, or was spirited away by other Lunars) using normal investigation and charms.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-04T06:31:48-07:00","id":1242701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242326#post1242326\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That does raise questions about what kind of slavery they had, though. Large workforces of people in permanent bondage are a different thing than a system of personally bonded retainers to specific kinds of individuals. An itinerant society of small communities having a system of slaves in the form of a few bonded household servants is not the same thing as the trans-Atlantic trade.</div> </div> </div> </div> Well, the Dune People are probably going to make a reappearance.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-04T07:14:08-07:00","id":1242708,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242701#post1242701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Well, the Dune People are probably going to make a reappearance.</div> </div> </div> </div>They get two mentions in the Ex3 Core: we're told that they lie beneath the sand to attack; and that they believe that all memories washed from souls by Lethe are forged into weapons for the gods, for the great battle at the end-times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>He/him","edited":{"userid":177,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","user":"TheCountAlucard","date":". 08-04-2018, 10:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/177-thecountalucard","name":"TheCountAlucard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar177_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10857"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-05T09:23:30-07:00","id":1242963,"title":"","content":"I'm honestly a bit cold towards the origin of albinism as an alternative to fences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-05T10:04:07-07:00","id":1242971,"title":"","content":"Yeah, considering all the unearthly phenotypes and ethnicities in Creation, including panda-spotted and zebra-striped people, a race of people who look like some people do in real life doesn't really need to be explained as &quot;a Solar cursed them with albinism so they couldn't escape&quot;.<br /> <br /> But then, they can easily reappear without that first-age backstory, especially as &quot;these guys were created by an evil Solar in the First Age&quot; is a piece of setting information that I don't think this edition will really go for so much.<br /> Certainly, neither of the two mentions Alucard references talk about it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-05T10:39:43-07:00","id":1242979,"title":"","content":"It's been there since their first appearance in Exalted: the Lunars and helps make them more nuanced than (for example) Hushed Ones who have a similar aesthetic but are straight up monster manual fodder.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":7,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","user":"Lioness","date":". 08-05-2018, 03:03 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-05T12:51:01-07:00","id":1242997,"title":"","content":"They could be even more nuanced without cannibalism as a way of life.<br /> <br /> Either way, private pleasure slaves is still different from mass labour slaves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-05T14:23:37-07:00","id":1243024,"title":"","content":"I'm not really bothered by the cannibalism (I like to see cultures with unusual cultural practices), but I do wonder, in the desert, how many people there actually are for them to eat. Seems like it'd be more just a food for special occasions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-05T16:29:08-07:00","id":1243060,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243024#post1243024\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not really bothered by the cannibalism (I like to see cultures with unusual cultural practices), but I do wonder, in the desert, how many people there actually are for them to eat. Seems like it'd be more just a food for special occasions.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, that's why I added the qualifier of being a way of life.<br /> <br /> As far as I know, in any real life culture that engaged in cannibalism, it's something that has very specific connotations and purpose. Perhaps not <i>always</i> a thing done to foes as a process of honouring their strength, but that one features prominently. A lot of times, cannibalism has been reserved for one's <i>own</i> fallen, with implications of things such as recycling their power and spirit into the community, or otherwise making something very intimate of something like a feast to honour them.<br /> <br /> It's not something I've read much on in a while, and there are probably gaps in my understanding, but that's where I'm coming from.<br /> <br /> When you get examples such as the Dune People and those tribes on that Northern island who were featured in the opening comic of Manual: Dragon Blooded (but not with the Lintha, interestingly*), you get the much more fictional instance of a culture that is <i>all about</i> cannibalism, to the point of attacking people specifically for the sake of eating them. It's not always on a premise that doing so is their primary source of sustenance (and it's particularly absurd when it is), but it's often exaggerated in a manner that makes it a basic shorthand for a community of people that are basically monsters. To be sure, I don't recall offhand any instance in which you got a depiction of somebody from a society that was super into eating people who was really pleasant and friendly and admirable in their own context.<br /> <br /> There are a few issues with it. One of the most prominent to my mind is that it has a <i>really</i> ugly legacy of being used to other and marginalize people indigenous to parts of the world that have been traditionally colonised and exploited by European powers, so it kind of raises a red flag to me, if only for uncritically invoking something with a stain on its history.<br /> <br /> As the Dune People go overall, I feel as though they exist in a somewhat odd place where the game has kind of flirted with the idea of wanting them to be well-rounded and acceptable and sympathetic, but kind of messes up the landing with wrapping them up in so much that is coded as feral and alien. I feel as though having both in the same place doesn't work the best, and that one is more worthy of keeping than the other.<br /> <br /> Never mind the other issues with using albinism as a shorthand for somebody alien and sinister. Sure, it's a better treatment than most in that it remembers that albinism comes with increased risk of skin cancer, emphasized by putting them in an extremely sunny environment, but like... you could do that without them also being scary cannibal people.<br /> <br /> I've yet to read Exalted: the Lunars personally, so I can't yet say if the book's overall perspective on people coded to indigenous groups necessarily validates the manner in which it uses the &quot;cannibalism as a way of life&quot; trope. Still, from second hand accounts, I have gathered that the book evinces a rather odd blend of depicting such groups as actually being marginalized by powerful, centralized states against which they are in a constant struggle for survival, and taking at face value the narrative of the barbarian at the gates of civilization, constantly waiting to just roll over and send everybody into the Dark Ages (even if it's from an angle of being the barbarians at the gates). I guess it depends on whereabouts the Dune People fall.<br /> <br /> * People seem to tend to forget that, as depicted in Blood &amp; Salt, the Lintha only really ever cannibalised one another; they considered other humans as unworthy of the attention, that doing so risked contaminating their Lintha blood, and would get <i>super</i> mad if an outsider was ever to eat the flesh of a Lintha.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-06T09:04:20-07:00","id":1243223,"title":"","content":"I remember watching a program once about an explorer who went and lived with remote tribes in the jungle. He joined this Indonesian tribe of cannibals. Except, of course, they didn't seem to eat anyone.<br /> <br /> Eventually, after becoming close enough that he felt he could raise it, he asked them about their well-known cannibalism. His friend explained that they don't just eat anyone, because that's screwed-up. They only eat witches. Because it's the only way to get rid of their curses.<br /> <br /> The guy admitted he once ate a witch, and it was awful, traumatising, he felt like he was going to vomit. But he didn't feel as he had any choice, because, well, the other guy was a witch.<br /> <br /> So, my point is that essentially I think Isator is right; cannibilism is generally part of specific cultural or religious rituals, not something done any time. This tribe, which was infamous amongst locals as being cannibals, actually ate people so rarely that this particular cannibal had only done it once in his life.<br /> <br /> Of course, there are cases where people starving will commit cannibalism. But this tends to be something they stop once they acquire more food, it's not something that becomes part of the culture.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 08-06-2018, 12:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-06T17:43:28-07:00","id":1243340,"title":"","content":"I'll actually say that my own read on the Shogunate is also that it was a time in which government and business interests went in for the mass enslavements of entire extant cultures (as distinct from the possibility that the First Age had magically created cultures to serve a kind of slave function) for the purpose of providing labour to shore up failing infrastructure and providing an alternative to the kind of efficiency produced by Solar directive or ancient sorcery. Heck, I would even read the Sidereals as institutionally promoting and enabling such processes, in their function of trying to keep Creation up and running; of all of the things I would imagine them seeing as a downturn in Creation, mass exploitation of human labour as an alternative to powerful and useful magic that is no longer available seems like one of the more obvious.<br /> <br /> Sure, I would imagine that a significant part of many Sidereals coming around on &quot;the costs are too high, we need to go for the riskier option&quot; was witnessing the difference between the idea in the abstract and the actual human costs of it in action, and I would certain think that there would be economic, social and political processes going on with and complicating the process as it developed, but yeah, I'm all for the idea that human exploitation took a big upswing after the Usurpation, and that the Sidereals were more than merely silent observers in that.<br /> <br /> I personally have no interest in condemning the fictional characters over that, or of seeing it primarily in terms of them being basically bad people. That's not to say that I'd categorise those Sidereals as good people that are tragically required to do bad things for the sake of a greater good, just that they're people with varying characters, priorities and perspectives trying to deal with things as they crop up, and dealing with complex forces that they're not fully in control of.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T01:15:34-07:00","id":1243429,"title":"","content":"The major thing about tribal cannibalism is that, as a practical matter, tribal societies don't live in areas with the population density to maintain it as a regular thing anyway. Mathematics of population and food consumption are such that the only people who <i>could</i> be regular cannibals are members of small elite classes in settled agriculture-based civilizations. Cannibal tribes are never going to do it more than rarely and (as a side effect of the rarity as much as anything else) ritualistically.<br /> <br /> I mean, theoretically, sure, you could have a tribal society that lived next to a high-density settled population and raided it regularly for people to eat. Sociologically that's unstable; either the tribe will have the ability to take over and become a small elite leadership class in the settled society, or the settled society will raise an army and crush the tribe.<br /> <br /> And it's only going to happen if the settled society doesn't have access to large domesticated herbivores, which are cheaper and safer sources of meat. Cattle (horse, sheep, whatever) raids are going to be preferred to cannibal raids by tribes, and elites eating beef are less likely to provoke peasant revolts than eating people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T01:28:34-07:00","id":1243430,"title":"","content":"I'm not so sure as to whether the Shogunate or even Usurpation would represent a step up in mass enslavement. (I don't quite share the model of slavery as being likely to be driven by economic substitution; it's not how it happened IRL at all, ever, and I'd prefer to hew to those causal factors in the interests of my preferred version of verisimilitude).<br /> <br /> But I don't have any problem so much with it hashing out that the Sidereals were often on the side of accepting &quot;more slavery&quot;. (They're not &quot;my guys&quot; who I want to have clean hands!).<br /> <br /> It's more that I don't see this as motivated from any Bronze Faction specific factor of a preference for higher populations whatever the quality of life, or &quot;expediency&quot;, that can be drawn back to a decision that, in my view, was mostly about how to resolve Solar tyranny alone, rather than a general philosophical perspective on quality v quantity of life, or on potential v security.<br /> <br /> And it's not a decision which begins or ends at the desks of the Bronze Faction (still less their inner circle alone); Gold Faction, Lunars, Dragonblooded, gods, mortals, etc. have all made decisions that have led to the current state of things, and with similar motivations of maintaining real power. (It's not the case that the Gold Faction or the Lunars are or ever have been generally &quot;anti&quot; slavery, while the Bronze Faction is generally &quot;pro&quot;; both those parties are as equally likely to support it or use it in the interests of their clients and their own power politics). The influence of the Bronze Faction is only a sliver among those vast array of factors beyond their control.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T01:58:45-07:00","id":1243435,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243430#post1243430\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">it's not how it happened IRL at all, ever</div> </div> </div> </div>Real life never had human civilization originate with the presence of mystical demigods that were around for the vast majority of it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd prefer to hew to those causal factors in the interests of my preferred version of verisimilitude).</div> </div> </div> </div>I want to make a joke about the Usurpation happening, and all of a sudden there's a pressing need for cotton, but... that doesn't actually seem like an inaccurate take on it. The worldwide market is still huge, a lot of the old infrastructure has broken down, they need <i>some</i> means of harvesting that cotton.<br /> <br /> Unless it's to say that, for all of their immense power and variety of innovations, the preferred method for harvesting cotton in the First Age was to acquire massive work forces of slaves to do it?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T08:52:58-07:00","id":1243517,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1242997#post1242997\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They could be even more nuanced without cannibalism as a way of life.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd rather change that than their origin.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Either way, private pleasure slaves is still different from mass labour slaves.</div> </div> </div> </div> They weren't originally pleasure slaves, Exalted: The Lunars talks about them maintaining some underground machine that created water for the resort and guarding a temple.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T09:50:32-07:00","id":1243530,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243517#post1243517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd rather change that than their origin.</div> </div> </div> </div>Why; what's the attachment there?<br /> <br /> This is me assuming that it's related specifically to their origin in terms of why they have albinism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exalted: The Lunars talks about them maintaining some underground machine that created water for the resort</div> </div> </div> </div>That doesn't really make a lot of sense to me. That actually sounds like a conceit more suited to the Second Age, where limited understanding of the functions of a thing and resources to preserve it requires round-the-clock menial labour of the kind that people would assume is only available through force to keep up and running.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T10:00:29-07:00","id":1243532,"title":"","content":"I see your point, but I can equally see that a fantastically rich First-Age ruler of a kingdom might well be happy to have an elaborate machine that requires servile labour, because, hey, why not? You rule a kingdom, you have plenty of labour. It shows off your power to other Solars. Your kingdom is so rich and powerful that you can afford to have a hundred people working on your water-ritual-enchanted-bronze-heart or whatever, rather than working on something productive like agriculture.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T10:19:18-07:00","id":1243541,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243532#post1243532\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see your point, but I can equally see that a fantastically rich First-Age ruler of a kingdom might well be happy to have an elaborate machine that requires servile labour, because, hey, why not?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because you have magic?<br /> <br /> I mean, better magic than the Dragon Blooded, certainly. I kind of implicitly assume that they're not quite good enough to widely create things that can provide for their labour, or sharply limit them altogether. <br /> <br /> I don't know, I hear the idea of depicting the decadence of Solars in the form of them enslaving people despite no economic incentive to do so, and it feels a lot like the take on them being corrupted by the Great Curse that I dislike so for being so generically villainous.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T10:24:30-07:00","id":1243542,"title":"","content":"Magic that doesn't do the work of millions of men. And before you go, but automatons, those need constant maintenance and were often specialized luxury items. Ones that only exalted can fix in this edition, unless you make a race to do that. In most cases, basic laborer would produce easier results.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T10:49:25-07:00","id":1243548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243542#post1243542\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Magic that doesn't do the work of millions of men.</div> </div> </div> </div>Jesus, how many Dune People do you need to pump your water? How big is that resort?<br /> <br /> Yes I'm being facetious; considering that a million people are probably distributed around doing about ten thousand specialised jobs, I would presume that all of those jobs have different requirements.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">And before you go, but automatons</div> </div> </div> </div>Whaaaaaa\u2026<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ones that only exalted can fix in this edition, unless you make a race to do that.</div> </div> </div> </div>The design of thaumaturgy doesn't exclude the First Age from having methods for people to interact with certain machines.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epimetheus</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">In most cases, basic laborer would produce easier results.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm mostly coming from a place influenced by a time I read Geoff Grabowski say that the First Age was not dependent on utilising the labour of vast numbers of people. It was a premise that sparked my imagination and drives me to envision scenarios that fulfil it. That and a general sense of trying to picture the anthropology of the setting in which human civilization has always included the Exalted.<br /> <br /> So, as an example, I would imagine how the First Age, in its earliest stages, would have managed some great feats of ancient (or early modern but in a technologically distinct part of the world) engineering, such as canals across the length of China, or mountain road networks connecting the Inca Empire.<br /> <br /> In real life, these things entailed corvee labour; not quite slavery, but still a form of bonded servitude, making providing a bit of time and service in a major state infrastructure project an obligation of existing within the state.<br /> <br /> As an alternative in Exalted, I see a few Solars, some accompanying other Celestial Exalted, a few dozen associated Dragon Blooded, a sorcerously bound additional force of demons and elementals, and maybe a few hundred paid professional or volunteer mortal labourers, and they go at it with gusto.<br /> <br /> Sure, it would be easier on those Solar Exalted if they just commanded several hundred thousand people to work on it instead, but you weren't bestowed the Twilight Caste because you're work averse, and it's a pretty worthwhile thing to devote some of your creative talents to.<br /> <br /> The idea that the Solars as fully corrupted by the Curse actually do become the kind of indolent and lazy that would create a preference for the alternative is not my favourite, but is kind of plausible... but for them sitting on the legacy of several thousand years of an alternative work ethic. I presume that they've gotten in a fair amount of automation by then. Certainly enough that it can then do things like go violently out of control in response to the Solars being betrayed.<br /> <br /> It's not referred to as the Era of Light so Limited that you need Large Quantities of Bonded Servitude to Harvest your Cash Crops. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T11:01:15-07:00","id":1243550,"title":"","content":"{perspective} This is starting to look like one of those scenarios where I get way too hung up on my own highly specific, heavily detailed and probably idiosyncratic vision of the First Age, so I'm just going to drop the subject before I run off half-cocked and make (more of?) an ass of myself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T11:13:36-07:00","id":1243552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243530#post1243530\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why; what's the attachment there?<br /> <br /> This is me assuming that it's related specifically to their origin in terms of why they have albinism.</div> </div> </div> </div>To me it's a compelling part of why they're albino desert dwelling nomads. It really was that bad for the Solar created races in the Shogunate.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T12:28:40-07:00","id":1243564,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243552#post1243552\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To me it's a compelling part of why they're albino desert dwelling nomads.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know... considering the degree to which albinism is marginalized in real life* and has problematic representations in fiction, I feel as though an idea of giving it a background of some kind of curse or debility imposed by a person has some issues. It feels a bit too close to people coming up with some very distressing ideas about why black people have such high melanin levels.<br /> <br /> It also feels a bit on the nose.<br /> <br /> *Seriously, bad enough the inherent issues with visual impairment and increased risk of skin cancer, but the number of cultures that will treat you as a witch or a cure for AIDS...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It really was that bad for the Solar created races in the Shogunate.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the whole concept of &quot;created races&quot; could do with a bit of re-examination, but... wouldn't the worst things for them be a matter of active persecution?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T12:35:41-07:00","id":1243566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243435#post1243435\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Real life never had human civilization originate with the presence of mystical demigods that were around for the vast majority of it.<br /> <br /> I want to make a joke about the Usurpation happening, and all of a sudden there's a pressing need for cotton, but... that doesn't actually seem like an inaccurate take on it. The worldwide market is still huge, a lot of the old infrastructure has broken down, they need <i>some</i> means of harvesting that cotton.<br /> <br /> Unless it's to say that, for all of their immense power and variety of innovations, the preferred method for harvesting cotton in the First Age was to acquire massive work forces of slaves to do it?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not like it's a bad take, and I'm not objecting on common sense grounds or infeasability - what you describe is a staple of post-apoc, after all; you see it about in Mad Max to Fallout.<br /> <br /> I just have a personal preference for slavery emerging from the kind of mechanisms that it emerged from when practiced by real world slave societies, which were more in the phase of the waxing of societies; as the Roman Empire's machine allows it to conquer, they take slaves as war booty; as the Islamic World's economies allows it to export trade goods, members of Slavic tribes captured in internecine warfare are traded to them; as the Atlantic European economies expand, they can sell trade goods to people who have not much more to offer than slaves, and an opportunity presents itself in transporting human lives to a place where European workers die in droves. <br /> <br /> That is, as something that emerges from economic expanse and the lack of any real check on the institution, more than a substitution of labour effect<br /> <br /> As to the question of whether the First Age Solars would ever have had need of slavery, there is the character of general abundance about which would limit *need* to resort to slavery, but, at the same time, I would point to the stance that the institution emerges more because of a lack of a sufficient check and incentives than from necessity.<br /> <br /> And on whether the Solars would ever have had *need* of slaves, to use in their magics, to perform ritual labour that free people probably would not do of choice, I think that seems reasonably possible. Factory cathedrals, &quot;through a mirror darkly&quot;, suggests some images. Especially given the caprice that certain magics show for people who meet a particular intersection of qualities (&quot;This ritual must be performed by a member of this specific bloodline&quot;). And whether malice is intended, there is probably a fair bit of moral compromise about in the wars of Solar Empires and between them and other things. (Though of course, much of depends on how much your First Age is defined as one of grandeur vs one of justice.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T12:47:31-07:00","id":1243570,"title":"","content":"Factory cathedrals don't really exist anymore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T13:24:38-07:00","id":1243576,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243564#post1243564\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know... considering the degree to which albinism is marginalized in real life* and has problematic representations in fiction, I feel as though an idea of giving it a background of some kind of curse or debility imposed by a person has some issues. It feels a bit too close to people coming up with some very distressing ideas about why black people have such high melanin levels.</div> </div> </div> </div> This seems like an argument to drop Dune People entirely. Even if you made them peaceful explorers you're still using a real life genetic condition as a shorthand to create exoticism.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the whole concept of &quot;created races&quot; could do with a bit of re-examination, but... wouldn't the worst things for them be a matter of active persecution?</div> </div> </div> </div>They do experience active persecution. It's just that they went through a period in their history where civilisation really did turn against them and their relations with people since have been informed by that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T13:26:33-07:00","id":1243579,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243548#post1243548\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm mostly coming from a place influenced by a time I read Geoff Grabowski say that the First Age was not dependent on utilising the labour of vast numbers of people. It was a premise that sparked my imagination and drives me to envision scenarios that fulfil it. That and a general sense of trying to picture the anthropology of the setting in which human civilization has always included the Exalted.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point to bear in mind, though I'd add, if I remember correctly (and I can't find the quote), that this is something which he highlighted specifically in the sense of industrialisation* and the social changes that he viewed as springing from that, in terms of I think, democracy, the large middle class and political egalitarianism as consequences of industrialization (contra the world of Exalted, which mirrors a pre-modern world of very high inequality within constraints and elites who are close to the Inequality Possibility Frontier). <br /> <br /> The Order Conferring Trade Pattern and the dependence of good functioning of the world of vast quantities of mortal prayer also suggest that in certain ways, the Solars were never able to completely dispense with the need to organize vast swathes of mortal society towards activities which were somewhere between ritual and organic, and between centrally planned and spontaneous.<br /> <br /> (He's also not totally consistent to my mind on the implied economic basis of the First Age in his comments, here: <a href=\"http://exalted.xi.co.nz/wiki/Thus_Spake_Zargrabowski/RealmTechnologicalLevels\" target=\"_blank\">http://exalted.xi.co.nz/wiki/Thus_Sp...ologicalLevels</a><br /> <br /> On the one hand he notes, in response to a comment that the Dragonblooded would suppress printing on the basis that; Commentor: &quot;<i>Peasants are peasants and they shouldn't be allwoed to rise up. It distrupts the order of society and causes chaos and all that</i>&quot;, Grabowski: &quot;<i>I think that you take a little more cynical a tone than the average Dragon-Blooded would. Consider that you can go outside and see the ruins of multiple destroyed high-tech civilizations. Would it be hard to keep a straight face when pushing a society based on idealized peasant farming?</i>&quot;.<br /> <br /> On the other states &quot;<i>Society also has a different focus too. The self-envisioned idyll of the world of Exalted is, I think, very much like the funeral scene at the end of Kurosawa's &quot;Dreams&quot;. A few Exalted trouble themselves so that the masses might live in peaceable ignorance</i>.&quot; where the end of Kurosawa's Dreams having watched the thing, is, in effect, some kind of idealized agrarian village!)<br /> <br /> *More on the tangent about industrialisation, even then, for Grabowski's statement, maybe refers to a personal theory of industrialisation where industrialisation was driven front end by social changes to recruit large amounts of the population in projects that societies viewed that they *needed* to achieve. <br /> <br /> My understanding is that's probably valid to some degree in later industrialising societies, who viewed it as a need to remain competitive in a dangerous international system (it probably tells you about German or Japanese or Soviet industrialisation) and engaged as a more top down process.<br /> <br /> But possibly less valid for primary industrialisation, in which putative theories revolve around where increases production of secondary goods (and so machines to do that, e.g. industrialisation), are a more organic aftereffect of things like a high agricultural productivity and a large share of goods production, which builds high demand, and a high wage level, which together with high demand and disposable income for non-food goods makes machine substitution and mass production attractive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T13:46:28-07:00","id":1243589,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghosthead</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243566#post1243566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's not like it's a bad take, and I'm not objecting on common sense grounds or infeasability - what you describe is a staple of post-apoc, after all; you see it about in Mad Max to Fallout.<br /> <br /> I just have a personal preference for slavery emerging from the kind of mechanisms that it emerged from when practiced by real world slave societies, which were more in the phase of the waxing of societies; as the Roman Empire's machine allows it to conquer, they take slaves as war booty; as the Islamic World's economies allows it to export trade goods, members of Slavic tribes captured in internecine warfare are traded to them; as the Atlantic European economies expand, they can sell trade goods to people who have not much more to offer than slaves, and an opportunity presents itself in transporting human lives to a place where European workers die in droves.<br /> <br /> That is, as something that emerges from economic expanse and the lack of any real check on the institution, more than a substitution of labour effect<br /> <br /> As to the question of whether the First Age Solars would ever have had need of slavery, there is the character of general abundance about which would limit *need* to resort to slavery, but, at the same time, I would point to the stance that the institution emerges more because of a lack of a sufficient check and incentives than from necessity.<br /> <br /> And on whether the Solars would ever have had *need* of slaves, to use in their magics, to perform ritual labour that free people probably would not do of choice, I think that seems reasonably possible. Factory cathedrals, &quot;through a mirror darkly&quot;, suggests some images. Especially given the caprice that certain magics show for people who meet a particular intersection of qualities (&quot;This ritual must be performed by a member of this specific bloodline&quot;). And whether malice is intended, there is probably a fair bit of moral compromise about in the wars of Solar Empires and between them and other things. (Though of course, much of depends on how much your First Age is defined as one of grandeur vs one of justice.)</div> </div> </div> </div>This post reminds me a bit on what I said earlier, on how slavery doesn't really come about because of some ecnoomic benefits it has over other things or the need for it, as you said. It seems often to arise because some other itnerest or opportunity arises such that some folks find it profitable early on to use slavery and then it becomes self-perpetuating since well, stolen labor is pretty good marginswise.<br /> <br /> And due to how generally autonomous many Solars in the First Age are depicted, slavery has a pretty high possibility at least of existing in a world with a lot of folks who if you wanted them tos top, could say &quot;Make me&quot; quite confidently.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-07T15:07:26-07:00","id":1243616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243576#post1243576\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> This seems like an argument to drop Dune People entirely. Even if you made them peaceful explorers you're still using a real life genetic condition as a shorthand to create exoticism.<br /> <br /> <br /> They do experience active persecution. It's just that they went through a period in their history where civilisation really did turn against them and their relations with people since have been informed by that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dropping them is a ship that has sailed, but I think it's worth examining ways to minimize harm.<br /> <br /> Society turning against them doesn't seem to have anything to do with their genetic restraining bolt.<br /> <br /> There's other stuff, but that needs to wait until I'm not at a PlayStation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T03:51:01-07:00","id":1243749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243616#post1243616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Society turning against them doesn't seem to have anything to do with their genetic restraining bolt.</div> </div> </div> </div> Their genetic restraining bolt is the thing that marked them as something post Usurpation society deemed undesirable.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T04:26:21-07:00","id":1243754,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243749#post1243749\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Their genetic restraining bolt is the thing that marked them as something post Usurpation society deemed undesirable.</div> </div> </div> </div>All right. I can now see the chain connecting your statements.<br /> <br /> I know that might sound condescending, but I sincerely mean it in basic terms of having not understood how some things that were said connected, and feeling as though I do now. <br /> <br /> So, just to confirm: you're coming at this from a position of a society of people with ubiquitous albinism living in the open desert is highly improbable, hence emphasizing that a willingness to do that to escape persecution meant that the persecution must have been very bad, with the persecution being compelled by the fact that they were made by a Solar and enabled by their inability to pass, the distinguishing trait itself having been deliberately imbued for the sake of making the kind of escape into the desert that they came to rely on being something that still causes them to suffer.<br /> <br /> Okay, I can sincerely see how all of those details lined up together would be compelling to somebody. I swear that I don't mean that as a crack or anything, and that I can see the appeal even if I don't share it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243589#post1243589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">And due to how generally autonomous many Solars in the First Age are depicted, slavery has a pretty high possibility at least of existing in a world with a lot of folks who if you wanted them tos top, could say &quot;Make me&quot; quite confidently.</span></span></span></div></div> </div> </div> </div><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><br /> <br /> I feel as though, as motivations go &quot;why not?&quot; is a few steps below &quot;they're crazy, you'll never be able to understand&quot;.<br /> <br /> I'd say that in this case there's still a missing step in the question of where those values originate in the first place. Solars can't be so autonomous that they have no formative experiences provided by culture and community and circumstance that create what might be called an identity. They don't spring fully formed from the forehead of the Unconquered Sun.<br /> <br /> Plus it gets into that thing of seeing Solars as including people that are just ungovernable monsters that I find distinctly uninteresting.</span></span></span></div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T04:50:20-07:00","id":1243757,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243754#post1243754\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd say that in this case there's still a missing step in the question of where those values originate in the first place. Solars can't be so autonomous that they have no formative experiences provided by culture and community and circumstance that create what might be called an identity. They don't spring fully formed from the forehead of the Unconquered Sun.</div> </div> </div> </div>This seems like the most obvious reason Solars would have slaves to me; that in the early First Age, before the Exalts had developed advanced enough magic to make slavery more hassle than it was worth, many areas (including the large parts of Creation not actually ruled by a Celestial circle at all) might have had slavery. And that it therefore seemed quite normal.<br /> <br /> I can well imagine in the various periods of Exalt vs Exalt wars especially, that press-ganging people into armies, work-crews, etc, would be quite common.<br /> <br /> Now saying all this, I don't feel it's particularly important that the Solars did have slaves in the High First Age. The Sidereals didn't overthrow them for being tyrannical rulers*, but that they feared the Solars would actually wreck the world. Just that I don't feel it's completely unreasonable.<br /> <br /> *The Dragonblood did though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":2402,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","user":"The Wizard of Oz","date":". 08-08-2018, 07:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T05:54:06-07:00","id":1243763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243757#post1243757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This seems like the most obvious reason Solars would have slaves to me; that in the early First Age, before the Exalts had developed advanced enough magic to make slavery more hassle than it was worth, many areas (including the large parts of Creation not actually ruled by a Celestial circle at all) might have had slavery. And that it therefore seemed quite normal.</div> </div> </div> </div>I suppose that depends on what one imagines their social models and population at the beginning of history to look like.<br /> <br /> I imagine it as being basically pre-historic, with some mythical edges in terms of the feats of their individual heroes. So there might be a place for the more personal slaves, but institutionalizing it on a massive scale? Your community consists of about two thousand people.<br /> <br /> The construction of communally significant monumental architecture might be the kind of thing that warrants slavery (even if, to my understanding, archaeological evidence into the origin of such things between ten and six thousand years ago suggests that they were more of a communally shared obligation, rather than something that prompted going over to a neighbouring community and forcing them to do it), but again, you can hash that out a lot differently when you've got a dedicated work gang of demigods accompanied by some spirits and people who are a bit more enthusiastic in taking part in such grandiose feats.<br /> <br /> Certainly, the origins of the First Age as promoted in Arms of the Chosen have a stronger focus on exploration of the newly open world.<br /> <br /> Overall, the kind of advanced magic that is needed by a collection of societies at that level of complexity is probably covered by Essence 3 Craft Charms. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can well imagine in the various periods of Exalt vs Exalt wars especially, that press-ganging people into armies, work-crews, etc, would be quite common.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds a lot more like conscription than janissaries.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T07:25:31-07:00","id":1243789,"title":"","content":"If you conscript people for forced labour, and don't let them go, is there any difference with slavery?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T08:08:59-07:00","id":1243797,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Wizard of Oz</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243789#post1243789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you conscript people for forced labour, and don't let them go, is there any difference with slavery?</div> </div> </div> </div>It might depend on who or what the system obligates them to, and how commercial their service is (i.e. can they be sold for profit) or what the terms of release are.<br /> <br /> Sure, the janissaries were a case in which it was a system of obligation to the state (or the monarch? I need to look more into that), but that was a system in which the state required people of certain religious affiliation to give up children in tribute to be wards of the state, who were then raised to be members of a dedicated military caste, so it's an unusual circumstance in any case. Really, considering some of the privileges that came with the role, to even refer to them as &quot;slave soldiers&quot; is a simplification, even if it gets the basic gist across.<br /> <br /> Still, to say that they're slaves because they're given a national obligation to serve in a military capacity is to say that a lot of people who served in both World Wars and Vietnam were slaves.<br /> <br /> It also begs a question of whether conscripted or levied military forces were desirable or necessary.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T09:32:14-07:00","id":1243821,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243797#post1243797\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It might depend on who or what the system obligates them to, and how commercial their service is (i.e. can they be sold for profit) or what the terms of release are.<br /> <br /> Sure, the janissaries were a case in which it was a system of obligation to the state (or the monarch? I need to look more into that), but that was a system in which the state required people of certain religious affiliation to give up children in tribute to be wards of the state, who were then raised to be members of a dedicated military caste, so it's an unusual circumstance in any case. Really, considering some of the privileges that came with the role, to even refer to them as &quot;slave soldiers&quot; is a simplification, even if it gets the basic gist across.<br /> <br /> Still, to say that they're slaves because they're given a national obligation to serve in a military capacity is to say that a lot of people who served in both World Wars and Vietnam were slaves.<br /> <br /> It also begs a question of whether conscripted or levied military forces were desirable or necessary.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't forget, Janissaries were lifetime servicemen who also couldn't have their own families.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T09:39:47-07:00","id":1243824,"title":"","content":"Well, presumably the exact terms of control and rights would vary across the First Age (just as slavery has throughout RL history). But it doesn't seem implausible to me that a Solar Monarch in the late First Age would think that forcing a hundred or so people to work on maintaining their desert palace is not entirely ridiculous. They may not have any idea of selling them, so you could perhaps call them serfs rather than slaves, but the essential point is that they're forced labour.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T09:54:55-07:00","id":1243829,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epee102</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243821#post1243821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Don't forget, Janissaries were lifetime servicemen who also couldn't have their own families.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think my point is already sufficiently concluding that janissaries were in fact slaves.<br /> <br /> Hmm, I was reading a book about all of the pharaohs all weekend... remembering stuff about backstory in Mage: the Awakening Second Edition...<br /> <br /> Early Solar: All right, those Yozis are good and sealed up, so we've got the run of the world now. You people? You're all my people now; you stick with me, and I promise that you're all gonna live like kings!<br /> Early people: Hooray!<br /> Solar: I've already started figuring out this system for getting more food more regularly, where we keep taking tiny parts of some of it, sticking it in the ground, tending it for months, and then picking the new growth.<br /> People: That sounds like a lot of hassle.<br /> Solar: Naw naw, I've got an angle on that. See those people over there? They don't really have any Exalted looking out for them, so I'm gonna go over there with somewhere between a few hundred and few thousand of y'all, and we're gonna bring those people back and make them do all of the farm work. It'll be great.<br /> People: So they'll be slaves?<br /> Solar: I think they'll be more of a perennially subservient undercaste hard bound to a menial but necessary task than something like the chattel slavery tied to high profit mining and cash crops of the late 18th through 19th century, but yeah, that term about covers it.<br /> People: We don't understand what half of those words mean.<br /> Solar: It's fine; once we're all set up, I'm gonna subsidise about half of you solely for the task of sitting around coming up with new words.<br /> People: Sounds great, let's go for it.<br /> <br /> {later on}<br /> Other Solar: Hey, so, I've gone and built a Wood manse that realigns the local Essence patterns in a way that makes farming a lot easier. Some work still needs to be put in, but a lot of the drudgery takes care of itself.<br /> Solar: Hmm, there are a few reasons why that won't work for me, including lack of Wood demesnes, not really being the best builder myself and having reservations about being beholden to somebody else, and already having gone all in on this slavery thing. It's kind of, I let them go, and there's going to be some tension.<br /> Other Solar: Isn't there going to be tension anyway if they start to know that their slavery isn't really necessary, and you're withholding an alternative.<br /> Solar: My Sidereal friend keeps telling me that that's future me's problem.<br /> Other Solar: I kind of feel as though I need to make war on you within the next few years on ethical grounds.<br /> Solar: Well, do what you've got to do. Just be warned that my guys have a lot of experience with weapons, since we need to be constantly under arms to keep the farm workers from running away.<br /> Random slave: {suddenly Exalts as a Solar or Lunar or possibly something else} I'm mad as hell and I'm not gonna take it anymore!!!<br /> Solar: Shit, I've got to go put this fire out. Some days, you almost sympathise with those Yozis, am I right?<br /> <br /> It's not quite without holes by my own standards, I don't entirely believe in it, and I could see it being the kind of thing that might go through a few overhauls across the eras (for instance, part of the advantage and the mandate of a First Deliberative being cooperation and interconnectedness that gets more labour saving Wood manses around), but I can't quite ignore it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T11:47:39-07:00","id":1243866,"title":"","content":"I don't think it's something that would definitely happen; if an ST said &quot;nope, this wasn't really a thing in the First Age&quot; I wouldn't feel it's ridiculous, but I also don't think it'd be ridiculous if it did happen.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> *The First Age was 5000 years; I'm absolutely sure that, during that time, at some point, there would be some slaves, somewhere. But that's not particularly meaningful.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-08T15:46:31-07:00","id":1243958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Blaque</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1243589#post1243589\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This post reminds me a bit on what I said earlier, on how slavery doesn't really come about because of some ecnoomic benefits it has over other things or the need for it, as you said. It seems often to arise because some other itnerest or opportunity arises such that some folks find it profitable early on to use slavery and then it becomes self-perpetuating since well, stolen labor is pretty good marginswise.<br /> <br /> And due to how generally autonomous many Solars in the First Age are depicted, slavery has a pretty high possibility at least of existing in a world with a lot of folks who if you wanted them tos top, could say &quot;Make me&quot; quite confidently.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I think that's a good way to put it. And then once that thing happens, after an particular interest seizes that opportunity, systems can build around it that make it more difficult to disestablish, even for those who care to. Perhaps they end up with, built around an opportunistic structure begun by predecessors, not with magic that provides you with the ability to substitute for labour, but magic that allows you manage coercion much more securely than any mortal, and magic that complements simple, repetitive labour rather than replaces it, and military power that lets you mitigate rebellion, and then there's no clear way out of it that doesn't have a cost that they will willingly pay (without being &quot;made&quot; to).<br /> <br /> (This is on top of accepting the conceit that Ex3 seems to run with, that magic is more art than science - you don't get what you want, necessarily, but what flows out of a process of exploration that is less than perfectly under rational control.)<br /> <br /> (And I'd add we virtually know that at least some First Age Solars had coercion craft beyond what the Dragonblooded can easily bring to bear in the Age of Sorrows - the Perfect's staff is virtually the first artefact published for Exalted, and I'd be surprised if it was necessarily a hugely far outlier! Even it was somewhat..).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-17T10:38:22-07:00","id":1247072,"title":"","content":"This feels like a good opportunity to make a point:<br /> This thread has been running quite happily on First Age Conjecture for several pages now. Doesn't it make you the least bit sad, Third's whole &quot;The First Age is Unknowable Mystery, and the Present Day is So Very Far From That Mystery that there's no meaningful connectivity between the Unknowable Mystery of the First Age and the Broke Rundown Iron-Medieval Present&quot;? (question mark outside quote deliberate)<br /> <br /> I get the idea of the Devs not wanting to spell things out so much, because they don't want What Was to default to Blueprint of What Should Be...Yet, I remain unconvinced that achieving that desired goal is worthwhile enough to abandon the elements of 2E that did work (2E Thaumaturgy allowing for meaningful mechanisms to advance the world, history and relics of the past having interesting and meaningful connectivity with goings-on in the present era) ....<br /> <br /> To me, the decision to veil the First Age seems more like a desire to not write up the First Age in any meaningful way for Third, dressed up in high-sounding concept-talk about not wanting to create an Ideal Blueprint for PCs to follow. If Storytellers find they're having problems with their players drawing on meta-lore knowledge with PCs who have no way of drawing on such lore, they can simply have a talk with their players. For me, the irritation of the 1000 Dooms has been replaced by &quot;EX3, now with More Ruined Ruins, ZERO Mortal Empowerment, and More Primitivism.&quot;<br /> <br /> I really don't see the advantage game-wise in replacing a deep and game effect-meaningful Thaumaturgy-system like 2E's with EX3's Thaumaturgy-as-One-Trick-Miracles...Which itself has to strain to make exceptions, for stuff they conceptually wanted to keep, but that aligned with call-out's to 2E Thaumaturgy (Varangian Astrology, Sijan Funerists/Exorcists primarily).<br /> <br /> On the historical level, I'm VERY pleased to see the map fill up, but the redaction of things like Opal, Tzatli etc etc ..again, don't see the upside.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-17T10:40:53-07:00","id":1247074,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1247072#post1247072\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To me, the decision to veil the First Age seems more like a desire to not write up the First Age in any meaningful way for Third</div> </div> </div> </div>Read Arms of the Chosen.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-17T12:57:36-07:00","id":1247132,"title":"","content":"The desire to obscure the First Age is in part to just CTRL+ALT+DEL what came before so that they could tell new, probably more interesting in total, stories. Like, I think a big thing that's being begged here is the presentation fo the First Age in 2e was good. Even if we were going to get a more detailed First Age, it woudl be a very different one form the one we had before.<br /> <br /> I think the shifts on presentation of the eras before is a combination of not having the present defined by the past (which was a problem as many books in 2e more or less assumed you were a place of signifcance due to some relci fo the First Age), creates room for there to be more interesting things that just dont' fit a neat tidy timeline like DotFA had, and also banishing much of it the land of wind and ghosts allows for just more interesting storeis, a different take on what the First Age even was, and more ambiguity about the lens of history to work from.<br /> <br /> And as noted, there are hints of a lot of this. <b>Arms of the Chosen</b> isn't without First Age stuff, it's just a very different First Age than was in 2e.<br /> <br /> On thaumaturgy, the big thing there is that it had huge mission creep in 2e. A lot of it was stuff that either kind of warped the setting due to its mechanical assumptions (notably weather control and the spirit stuff), kind of made Occult this weird omni-Ability (alchemy again, geomancy, and some toher stuff stepped on toes of other things) and even wasn't clear why some things existed as they were (why is animal husbandry it?) It also had a side-effect of kind of being too common and taking away a bit of the idea of the setting being like Earth enough to make sense and also removed plot points like conmen or simply imisinformed people. When every village had a genuine shaman, it kind of made huxters not really have a place. <br /> <br /> There's alsoa gain, a lot of things that probably just didn't need the thaumaturgy subsystem to work. Like, most of animal husbandry is just....training and breeding animals. Alchemy works fine for Craft or Medicine. And things are moving towards more the sort of thing to make an interesting world PCs work in, not a speculative world-builiding thing to set novels in.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>And stuff.<br /> <sub><a href=\"https://blaqueandstuff.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My DeviantArt Page</a> // <a href=\"http://blaqueandstuff.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My tumblr</a> // <a href=\"http://www.dropbox.com/sh/trxegr5hl27s1l5/AAAegBBzxbDhvk1YpYJqdhz8a?dl=0\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted 3e Houserules</a></sub>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/353-blaque","name":"Blaque","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar353_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2976"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-17T20:58:55-07:00","id":1247279,"title":"","content":"Fundamentally, thaumaturgy in 2e was inconsistent with the setting presented in any version of Exalted. There were accordingly three approaches:<br /> <br /> 1) Radically change thaumaturgy.<br /> 2) Radically change the presented setting.<br /> 3) Treat the presented setting as an unstable equilibrium that could easily be tipped by the PCs into the Age of Enlightened Mortals wielding the Procedures of Creation in the Magical Industrial Revolution.<br /> <br /> I understand how people who think #3 is a great gaming opportunity could be disappointed by the choice made.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T10:47:04-07:00","id":1247755,"title":"","content":"I'm a #3 man then:<br /> I don't see the point in doing away with the 1000 Dooms if they were going to just downgrade Creation into the hopeless sink its become in Third. (Yes, I have read Arms of the Chosen. Don't even get me started on how much it pisses me off that making Sidereals dissonant to everything but Starmetal IS going to be used to justify some pro-Sidereal mechanics shenanigans, when Artifacts were never even a big schtick for the splat to begin with.)<br /> <br /> Pushing the First Age down into this &quot;Everything cool and magical is pretty much long since busted or spent, because pretty much everything relied on the Solars&quot; just makes me sad. It implies the Lunars and Sidereals made next to no meaningful and lasting infrastructure contributions of an independent nature, and that's TOO Solar-centric for my tastes, when the level of Solar-centricity was already at a level I was quite comfortable with.<br /> <br /> I agree 2E Thaumaturgy had some problems. I was never fond of how Demon Summoning was an easy-mode loosing of high-Circle Demons into Creation, when there was no corresponding support for why those Demons aren't running about Creation all the time. I thought Creation would look very different as a natural result of it, but it didn't, and that made the Art both weird, inconsistent and just eh. (Not that I WANTED a Creation that looked like that)....I woulda been much happier if it focused around contacting said Demons without calling them up (when it came to Second+ Demons)...calling up unbound Firsts was fine. The other Arts you mentioned weren't favorites either.<br /> <br /> I basically liked Alchemy, Geomancy, Enchantment, Dead, and Warding/Exorcism. The others needed major reworking or folding into sorcery. EX3 Thaumaturgy making humanity this helpless mass though, by making thaumaturgy one-trick-pony miracles like tea leaf reading just wasn't the way to go IMHO. Now, if an Exalt wants to build a kingdom and create progress...all roads lead to &quot;Create/Fund Sorcery Academy, start generating citizen-sorcerers&quot;...and I really am not fond of needing to mass-produce mortal sorcerers in order to make humans useful as more than troop masses or an inferior labor force.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T11:42:10-07:00","id":1247763,"title":"","content":"You can't mass-produce sorcerers, it just doesn't work. Only about 1 in 10,000 mortals have the potential to learn. The others just lack a certain something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T12:28:30-07:00","id":1247777,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1247755#post1247755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pushing the First Age down into this &quot;Everything cool and magical is pretty much long since busted or spent, because pretty much everything relied on the Solars&quot; just makes me sad. It implies the Lunars and Sidereals made next to no meaningful and lasting infrastructure contributions of an independent nature, and that's TOO Solar-centric for my tastes, when the level of Solar-centricity was already at a level I was quite comfortable with.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that's not Third Edition's official answer. The answer is that Lunar Exalted have been deliberately breaking these wonders to deny them to the Dragon-Blooded.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, if an Exalt wants to build a kingdom and create progress...all roads lead to &quot;Create/Fund Sorcery Academy, start generating citizen-sorcerers&quot;...and I really am not fond of needing to mass-produce mortal sorcerers in order to make humans useful as more than troop masses or an inferior labor force.</div> </div> </div> </div>We don't know the full details of Volivat and how they created an entire society of enhanced mortals yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T13:07:37-07:00","id":1247787,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1247755#post1247755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a #3 man then:<br /> I don't see the point in doing away with the 1000 Dooms if they were going to just downgrade Creation into the hopeless sink its become in Third. (Yes, I have read Arms of the Chosen. Don't even get me started on how much it pisses me off that making Sidereals dissonant to everything but Starmetal IS going to be used to justify some pro-Sidereal mechanics shenanigans, when Artifacts were never even a big schtick for the splat to begin with.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Really OT, but have to say, I don't see that being a significant balance point in the devs plans, just because artefacts can to a degree be reskinned to different metals (not without limits, but...), and because artefacts are so character concept and campaign specific.<br /> <br /> It seems to me more like a tool that allows the GM more control about what artefacts PCs can loot off NPCs. Sidereals are kind of about having powerful but limited powers (people have exaggerated it, &quot;rubber chicken powers&quot; and so on, but it's true), and putting in the limitation should just allow GMs to put in lots of NPCs with mostly whatever artefacts they want without having to worry too much about players looting them and using them to expand their roster of capabilities, and then having to think more about what options PCs can combine.<br /> <br /> As well as being helpful in explaining the setting, which is about why Sidereal crafters would tend to chase and hoard Starmetal so much, if it's rare and not particularly resonant with them, and encouraging Sidereals who craft with other metals to be the bearers of mysterious gifts which are best used by other Exalted, rather than just decking themselves out.<br /> <br /> I also have to express some mild shock that you see Arms of the Chosen presents a *more* Solar centric view of artefacts and magic than Dreams of the First Age? And implies a more Solar centric view of magical infrastructure than Dreams of the First Age? If you wish to expand on that, and if you don't then nm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/929-ghosthead","name":"Ghosthead","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T15:15:59-07:00","id":1247822,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1247755#post1247755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a #3 man then:<br /> Don't even get me started on how much it pisses me off that making Sidereals dissonant to everything but Starmetal IS going to be used to justify some pro-Sidereal mechanics shenanigans</div> </div> </div> </div>That seems quite an assumption you're making.<br /> <br /> The obvious thing the dissonance does is reinforce Sidereal themes. It strictly limits how well Sidereals (as a group) can escape their limited charm-set by crafting artifacts for access to Evocations, since they cannot use the more common magical materials effectively. They accordingly remain with martial arts and sorcery as their alternatives to their limited charmset -- rather than running around in jade panoplies acculmulated in Yu Shan over thousands of years of Sidereal crafting (and robbing Dragon-Blooded). It also emphasizes how much they want to acquire starmetal, because it is the one material that allows them to craft artifacts that let them have full-powered Evocations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T17:00:09-07:00","id":1247842,"title":"","content":"You just said it Verzio:<br /> Sorcery. Sids are going to get mechanical sorcery-bennies, with the justification being their mass-MM-dissonance limits their Evocations. I am NOT thrilled by the notion of Sidereals moving into the #2 spot behind Solars as the preeminent sorcerers, and that seems to be the way things are headed. Way I see it, Sidereal MA provides the Sids with their Special Button, and Sidereal Astrology their &quot;Something Extra.&quot; The INSTANT I saw the &quot;Dissonant to all but Starmetal&quot; reading Arms of the Chosen, I just had this flash of utter certainty that mass-dissonance is going to be the justification for giving the splat something they shouldn't have.<br /> <br /> My comment about mass producing sorcerers was meant to make exactly that point. Ie: You can't, which makes mortals fairly useless. With 2E-style Thaumaturgy, an Exalt kingdom-builder could do something like what the Perfect of Paragon did. Now? Everything mortals can do, demons + elementals can do better. In practice, I can tell you what's happening in my game. I pretty much need a &quot;No Stamp&quot; for sorcerous workings, because my two Twilights have figured out there's vastly more mileage to be gained from Celestial-Circle Workings xp-wise than the vast majority of after-chargen increase Merits, and CERTAINLY infinitely more efficiency than crafting. Why buy ANYTHING that their Storyteller can take away, when if they dump the XP into a Working that accomplishes the same thing, it gets mechanical script-immunity? Wyld Hunt blows up their 2nd-Circle Demon equivalent-power artificial being? They get the XP back in full. Wyld Hunt kills Followers? Loss of Merit-levels. My guys and gals can do basic arithmetic...<br /> <br /> I have difficulty even getting that mad, even when what they want is something outrageous.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24378-wyldwraith","name":"Wyldwraith","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T18:21:26-07:00","id":1247857,"title":"","content":"Personally, I would be <i>thrilled</i> if Sidereals got some comparative advantage in sorcery. Their advantage in martial arts has been eroded this edition, since they only have conditional access to Mastery and Lunars make up for the lack by wielding their native Charms in combination -- and even beyond that, their hyperfocus on martial arts meant that it was a One True Build for every Sidereal in previous editions, at least after buying up the extremely limited number of combat Charms in their closed set. At least having strong support for sorcery would give them <i>two</i> true builds, and also give them something nice to balance the new level of necromancy Lunars get these days.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately for me, but fortunately for Wyldwraith, when I asked about this in the Ask the Devs thread, the answer I got was that SMA should be enough of a balancing factor. So I guess there's no need to worry about Sidereals fulfilling their concept of being sorcerers and mystics in addition to kung fu bureaucrats this edition, if that's a thing people were worrying about.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/39-ysadrel","name":"ysadrel","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"79"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T18:36:42-07:00","id":1247860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1247842#post1247842\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You just said it Verzio:<br /> Sorcery. Sids are going to get mechanical sorcery-bennies, with the justification being their mass-MM-dissonance limits their Evocations. I am NOT thrilled by the notion of Sidereals moving into the #2 spot behind Solars as the preeminent sorcerers, and that seems to be the way things are headed. Way I see it, Sidereal MA provides the Sids with their Special Button, and Sidereal Astrology their &quot;Something Extra.&quot; The INSTANT I saw the &quot;Dissonant to all but Starmetal&quot; reading Arms of the Chosen, I just had this flash of utter certainty that mass-dissonance is going to be the justification for giving the splat something they shouldn't have.<br /> <br /> My comment about mass producing sorcerers was meant to make exactly that point. Ie: You can't, which makes mortals fairly useless. With 2E-style Thaumaturgy, an Exalt kingdom-builder could do something like what the Perfect of Paragon did. Now? Everything mortals can do, demons + elementals can do better. In practice, I can tell you what's happening in my game. I pretty much need a &quot;No Stamp&quot; for sorcerous workings, because my two Twilights have figured out there's vastly more mileage to be gained from Celestial-Circle Workings xp-wise than the vast majority of after-chargen increase Merits, and CERTAINLY infinitely more efficiency than crafting. Why buy ANYTHING that their Storyteller can take away, when if they dump the XP into a Working that accomplishes the same thing, it gets mechanical script-immunity? Wyld Hunt blows up their 2nd-Circle Demon equivalent-power artificial being? They get the XP back in full. Wyld Hunt kills Followers? Loss of Merit-levels. My guys and gals can do basic arithmetic...<br /> <br /> I have difficulty even getting that mad, even when what they want is something outrageous.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Hey Wyldwraith! I'd love to have a discussion about how to balance workings vs mortal merits vs artifice as a ST who has had a few sorcerers and artifact makers but that seems like a new thread topic (along with gripes about thautmurgy, something I am far less intreasted in to be honest, since literally every art of thaughtmurgy people have told me [someone who came in during 3e] sounds like it belongs in an existing abillity, but that's a part of discussing the sorcery vs artifice system anyway).<br /> <br /> But to the point reagarding Sidereals: SMAs and Astrology are still a thing, and still the main thing. I suspect they'll have sorcery boosters--literally every splat does now--but they probably aren't sorecerer focused. The Devs have said evocations are a knob to twist--notably, Dragon Blooded's benefits from Artifacts are 2nd only to solars, and better considering the charmset difference. Abyssals resonance only with soulsteel is likewise intentional to allow them better necromancy. <i>More likely</i>, Sidereals will get MA bonuses or the like that balance out dissoance with non-starmetal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>The Book of Laughing Serpents Series(Latest <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/293793/Book-of-Laughing-Serpents-Volume-7\" target=\"_blank\">Here</a>)<br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/287741/Many-Limbed-Manual?src=hottest_filtered&amp;filters=45622\" target=\"_blank\">Many Limbed Manual</a><br /> Patreon here: <a href=\"https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety\" target=\"_blank\">https://patreon.com/undeadauthorsociety</a><br /> San Jeanro Co-Op writer. Volume 1 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/278871/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 2 <a href=\"https://t.co/SXRLElZlw4?amp=1\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> Volume 3 <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/297692/The-Short-Games-Digest-Volume-3?affiliate_id=389642\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> My folklore and horror blog, here:<a href=\"http://undeadauthorsociety.wordpres.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://undeadauthorsociety.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11835-epee102","name":"Epee102","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11835_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2102"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-19T22:38:16-07:00","id":1247917,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1247842#post1247842\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Why buy ANYTHING that their Storyteller can take away, when if they dump the XP into a Working that accomplishes the same thing, it gets mechanical script-immunity? Wyld Hunt blows up their 2nd-Circle Demon equivalent-power artificial being? They get the XP back in full. Wyld Hunt kills Followers? Loss of Merit-levels. My guys and gals can do basic arithmetic...</div> </div> </div> </div>The merits which are tagged &quot;Story&quot; don't cost xp to acquire in play, once you jump through the necessary hoops to acquire them they're yours for free also the way you're presenting this looks like a serious trust issue.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-31T12:45:40-07:00","id":1250303,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wyldwraith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1247842#post1247842\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You just said it Verzio:<br /> Sorcery. Sids are going to get mechanical sorcery-bennies, with the justification being their mass-MM-dissonance limits their Evocations. I am NOT thrilled by the notion of Sidereals moving into the #2 spot behind Solars as the preeminent sorcerers, and that seems to be the way things are headed. Way I see it, Sidereal MA provides the Sids with their Special Button, and Sidereal Astrology their &quot;Something Extra.&quot; The INSTANT I saw the &quot;Dissonant to all but Starmetal&quot; reading Arms of the Chosen, I just had this flash of utter certainty that mass-dissonance is going to be the justification for giving the splat something they shouldn't have.</div> </div> </div> </div>Doubtful. Being Resonant/Dissonant is mostly a matter of flavour, the main reason why Solars are Resonant with everything is because finding the panoply of your previous incarnation before the game starts had become a cliche.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/535-bastet","name":"Bastet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar535_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-31T12:55:19-07:00","id":1250307,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bastet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1250303#post1250303\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Doubtful. Being Resonant/Dissonant is mostly a matter of flavour, the main reason why Solars are Resonant with everything is because finding the panoply of your previous incarnation before the game starts had become a cliche.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That plus mechanically they want to incentivize Solars doing whatever Solars want to do and getting maximum power out of any artifact means there's no artifact a Solar will turn their nose up at / be unable to engage with fully. Solars being the predominant protagonist didn't go away in 3e, it just took the form of giving extra escorts and blow to Solars, rather than the 2e form of &quot;disincentivizing&quot; other Exalt types by breaking their limbs with a baseball bat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/756-meianno-yuurei","name":"Meianno Yuurei","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar756_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-31T14:25:38-07:00","id":1250321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bastet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1250303#post1250303\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Doubtful. Being Resonant/Dissonant is mostly a matter of flavour, the main reason why Solars are Resonant with everything is because finding the panoply of your previous incarnation before the game starts had become a cliche.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed.<br /> <br /> The main reason that Sidereals are dissonant with everything but starmetal is to emphasize &quot;chasing down fallen stars&quot; as a thing that they do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines.</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/user?u=451515\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Vance's Patreon</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/527-robert-vance","name":"Robert Vance","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-31T14:41:23-07:00","id":1250326,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bastet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1250303#post1250303\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Doubtful. Being Resonant/Dissonant is mostly a matter of flavour, the main reason why Solars are Resonant with everything is because finding the panoply of your previous incarnation before the game starts had become a cliche.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I included &quot;(as a group)&quot; in my previous post advisedly; for a PC, artifact Merit dots are artifact Merit dots, with no material availability qualifiers. Availability of appropriate artifacts acquired in-play is a matter of Storyteller discretion, not statistical simulation; this is not an MMO with loot drops skewed against starmetal. Sidereals' limited Resonance and broad Dissonance shapes the setting, not the power of PCs.<br /> <br /> (Not that that'll convince someone with a belief that the Devs were planting an excuse for giving Sids a power-up. For someone who believes that, all the explanation does is make him more convinced the excuse for the inevitable power-up is all the more threadbare.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/572-verzio","name":"Verzio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar572_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-09-01T04:05:07-07:00","id":1250428,"title":"","content":"As for Thaumaturgy. The spiritual successors to the second edition system are Declare a Fact and Social Influence.<br /> <br /> -The rituals that were basically chemistry can be recreated through the use of Declaring Facts to introduce natural or supernatural phenomena that can be co-opted for what you're trying to do.<br /> -The spirit beckoning part mostly falls to Social Influence, 2e had a lot of rituals that consisted of contacting a spirit patron and asking nicely for things. I assume this got handled with thaumaturgy because of how difficult it was ordinarily for a mortal to persuade a spirit with a (Charisma + Performance) roll. In 3rd edition its much easier for you to actually bargain as long as you can leverage the spirit's intimacies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><a href=\"https://swordofcreation.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Sword of Creation</a> a hub for Exalted related content</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7-lioness","name":"Lioness","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7_2.png","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5443"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-09-06T02:56:19-07:00","id":1251870,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lioness</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1250428#post1250428\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">-The spirit beckoning part mostly falls to Social Influence, 2e had a lot of rituals that consisted of contacting a spirit patron and asking nicely for things. I assume this got handled with thaumaturgy because of how difficult it was ordinarily for a mortal to persuade a spirit with a (Charisma + Performance) roll. In 3rd edition its much easier for you to actually bargain as long as you can leverage the spirit's intimacies.</div> </div> </div> </div>The prayers to Siakal in Scroll of Kings are the best example of this, they're rooted in a very D&amp;D assuption that your patron god isn't an NPC you're asking things of so much as the portfolio of themes/powers that your cleric invokes. Hence you pay xp for your &quot;spells&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>success is the ability to go from one failure to the next without any loss of enthusiasm","edited":{"userid":1115,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1115-the-hug-ninja","user":"The Hug Ninja","date":". 09-06-2018, 06:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1115-the-hug-ninja","name":"The Hug Ninja","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1115_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"324"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-09-06T10:21:12-07:00","id":1251946,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Verzio</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/sidereal-exalted/1236176-what-s-your-read-on-sidereal-control-of-heaven-re-factions?p=1250326#post1250326\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah, I included &quot;(as a group)&quot; in my previous post advisedly; for a PC, artifact Merit dots are artifact Merit dots, with no material availability qualifiers. Availability of appropriate artifacts acquired in-play is a matter of Storyteller discretion, not statistical simulation; this is not an MMO with loot drops skewed against starmetal. Sidereals' limited Resonance and broad Dissonance shapes the setting, not the power of PCs.<br /> <br /> (Not that that'll convince someone with a belief that the Devs were planting an excuse for giving Sids a power-up. For someone who believes that, all the explanation does is make him more convinced the excuse for the inevitable power-up is all the more threadbare.)</div> </div> </div> </div>In game it's rather arbitrary. I don't get why the harping on this. Also, sidereals have more resources at start more than any exalted type. If anyone can get a starmetal artifact, it'll be them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}